import{c as e}from"./catalog-CAAnYY3b.js";var t=[{id:`1-1`,code:`1.1`,elementId:`e1`,title:`Kinds of early years and school provision, and the settings in each`,page:40,notes:{gist:`Provision in England sits in three groups: early years (home-based care, nurseries and preschools), maintained schools (community, voluntary, foundation, trust and special) and non-maintained schools (academies, free schools, independent schools and alternative provision, including home schooling). Who owns the site, who employs staff and who funds the place changes the practitioner's duties, the curriculum the setting must follow and who inspects it. Exam answers must name the type of setting, not just say school or nursery.`,mustKnow:[`Early years home-based care: childminders (usually in the childminder's home, Ofsted- or agency-registered) and nannies (employed by the family, usually in the child's home).`,`Nurseries provide day care and education, often full-day; they may be private, voluntary or independent (PVI) or maintained nursery schools or classes attached to a primary.`,`Preschools (playgroups) are typically sessional, term-time, community- or charity-run places for two- to four-year-olds.`,`Maintained schools are funded via the local authority: community, voluntary controlled, voluntary aided (church or faith), foundation, trust and special schools.`,`Community schools: the LA owns the land, employs staff and is the admissions authority. Voluntary controlled schools are often church-linked but the LA still employs staff.`,`Voluntary aided (faith) schools: the governing body usually owns the site, employs staff and has more control over admissions and religious education.`,`Foundation and trust schools: the governing body (and, for trust schools, a charitable trust) owns the land and employs staff, with more independence from the LA than a community school.`,`Non-maintained provision: academies and free schools (state-funded, independent of the LA); private/independent schools and independent special schools (fee-paying); alternative provision, including home schooling.`],sections:[{heading:`Early years provision: home-based care, nurseries and preschools`,body:[`Childminders are self-employed carers, usually in their own home, often mixing ages. If they care for children under eight for more than two hours a day they must register with Ofsted or a childminder agency and follow the EYFS. Nannies are employed by the family and work in the child's home; they are not required to join the Ofsted Early Years Register unless they choose the voluntary register or care for children from more than two families.`,`Day nurseries typically offer full-day care from baby rooms through to preschool rooms. A PVI nursery is a private business, charity or independent provider; a maintained nursery school or nursery class is LA-funded and attached to the maintained sector. Both registered types must implement the EYFS statutory framework.`,`Preschools (historically playgroups) are often sessional — mornings, afternoons or a few days a week — and community-run. They remain a common route for three- and four-year-olds taking the funded entitlement who do not need full-day care. In the exam, do not treat preschool, nursery and childminder as interchangeable: hours, premises and who employs the adult all differ.`]},{heading:`Maintained school provision`,body:[`Maintained schools are funded through the local authority and must teach the National Curriculum. Community schools are the simplest model: the LA owns the site, employs staff and sets admissions. Special schools in the maintained sector educate pupils whose Education, Health and Care Plans (EHCPs) name specialist provision; they still sit under the LA.`,`Voluntary controlled (VC) schools are frequently Church of England or other faith schools, but the LA employs staff and usually controls admissions; the church or foundation often owns the land and influences worship and ethos. Voluntary aided (VA) schools give the governing body more power: it employs staff, is usually the admissions authority and determines religious education and worship, with the site typically owned by the church or a religious foundation.`,`Foundation schools hold their own land and employ their own staff through the governing body. Trust schools are a form of foundation school supported by a charitable trust that appoints some governors and may bring in a partner such as a university or business. In a scenario, decide VA versus VC before you write about who hires the teacher or who sets the admissions policy.`]},{heading:`Non-maintained school provision and alternative provision`,body:[`Academies are state-funded but independent of the local authority. They operate under a funding agreement with the Secretary of State; the academy trust employs staff, holds the land (or a long lease) and has more control over curriculum, term dates and pay. Free schools are academies set up by groups such as teachers, parents, charities or existing trusts in response to local need. Most still teach a broad and balanced curriculum closely aligned to the National Curriculum.`,`Private (independent) schools charge fees. They do not have to follow the National Curriculum in full, though many do for public examinations. Independent special schools provide specialist places, often for pupils with EHCPs whose needs cannot be met in a maintained special school; the LA may still fund the place. Inspection may be by Ofsted or, for many independent schools, the Independent Schools Inspectorate (ISI).`,`Alternative provision is education arranged when a child cannot attend a mainstream school, for example after exclusion, for medical reasons or because a placement has broken down. It includes pupil referral units, AP academies and independent AP. The specification also lists home schooling (elective home education) as an example: parents have a duty under the Education Act 1996 to cause their child to receive a suitable education, which may be at school or otherwise. Practitioners meeting a home-educated child, for example at a childminder or a part-time nursery place, still follow EYFS and safeguarding duties for the hours the child attends.`]}],legislation:[{name:`School Standards and Framework Act`,year:`1998`,point:`Sets the categories of maintained school: community, foundation, voluntary controlled and voluntary aided.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`2002`,point:`Requires maintained schools to deliver the National Curriculum and a broad, balanced curriculum.`},{name:`Childcare Act`,year:`2006`,point:`Underpins Ofsted registration of early years providers and the duty to implement the EYFS.`},{name:`Academies Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Enabled the expansion of academies and the creation of free schools, independent of the local authority.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`1996`,point:`Places a duty on parents to cause their child of compulsory school age to receive a suitable education, at school or otherwise (home schooling).`}],examMoves:[`Identify means name the provision type — community, VA, academy, childminder — not a paragraph on funding unless the command is Explain.`,`Outline a difference with who employs staff, who owns the site, who funds the place, or who inspects. VA versus VC is a frequent discriminator.`,`Apply the scenario: a church primary is VA or VC, not automatically an academy; an academy is state-funded, not a fee-paying independent school.`,`Do not dump post-16 routes or a history of the 1870 Act unless the question asks; this specification point is types of provision and the settings within them.`]},quiz:[{id:`1-1-q1`,topicId:`1-1`,stem:`Which statement correctly distinguishes a childminder from a nanny?`,options:[`Both must always join the Ofsted Early Years Register, and both work only in the child's home.`,`A nanny is employed by the local authority and counts as maintained provision; a childminder is an academy employee.`,`A childminder usually cares for children in their own home and must register with Ofsted or a childminder agency; a nanny is typically employed by the family in the child's home.`,`Childminders may only care for children over eight; nannies may only care for babies.`],answer:2,explanation:`Childminders are home-based, self-employed and registrable with Ofsted or an agency. Nannies work in the family's home and are not automatically required to join the Early Years Register. The other options confuse employment, registration and age rules.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`1-1-q2`,topicId:`1-1`,stem:`A parent asks how a voluntary aided Church of England primary differs from a community primary. Which statement is accurate?`,options:[`Both are fee-paying independent schools inspected only by the Independent Schools Inspectorate.`,`In a community school the local authority typically owns the land and employs staff; in a voluntary aided school the governing body usually employs staff and has more control over admissions and religious education.`,`A voluntary aided school is an academy and cannot teach religious education.`,`Community schools are not maintained; voluntary aided schools are funded only by the diocese.`],answer:1,explanation:`VA schools give the governing body employment and (usually) admissions control and a distinctive faith character; community schools are LA-run. VA schools are still maintained and state-funded, not independent or automatically academies.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`1-1-q3`,topicId:`1-1`,stem:`Which of the following is an example of non-maintained school provision?`,options:[`An academy operating under a funding agreement with the Secretary of State`,`A community primary owned and staffed by the local authority`,`A voluntary controlled Church of England school where the local authority employs the teachers`,`A maintained special school named in a pupil's EHCP`],answer:0,explanation:`Academies are state-funded but not maintained by the LA. Community, VC and maintained special schools all sit in the maintained sector. Mixing academy with independent (fee-paying) is a common error; the correct contrast here is maintained versus non-maintained.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`1-1-o1`,topicId:`1-1`,marks:4,command:`Outline`,stem:`Outline two differences between maintained school provision and non-maintained school provision.`,modelAnswer:`Maintained schools are funded and overseen through the local authority. In a community school the LA typically owns the site and employs staff, and the school must teach the National Curriculum. Non-maintained provision includes academies and free schools, which are still state-funded but independent of the LA under a funding agreement with the Secretary of State, so the trust employs staff and has more control over curriculum and term dates. A second difference is that private (independent) schools and many independent special schools charge fees and do not have to follow the National Curriculum in full, whereas maintained schools cannot charge for a school place and must deliver the National Curriculum. Alternative provision and home schooling also sit outside the maintained school categories.`,markScheme:[`Identifies a valid difference (funding route, employment, land, curriculum duty, fees or inspection) (1)`,`Briefly develops that difference with a named setting type (1)`,`Identifies a second distinct difference (1)`,`Develops the second difference (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`1-2`,code:`1.2`,elementId:`e1`,title:`General, specialist and support roles in early years and education, and what each person does`,page:40,notes:{gist:`The sector is organised around general classroom and room-based roles, specialist posts that carry a whole-setting duty, and supporting professionals who are usually employed outside the setting. Exam answers must match the adult to a unique responsibility: an early years educator is not a teacher, a DSL is not a SENDCo, and the LADO is not the child's social worker. Apply the role to the setting in the scenario — nursery language for 0–5, teacher and TA language for school.`,mustKnow:[`General roles: early years practitioner, early years educator, room leader, teaching assistant, teacher, headteacher.`,`An early years practitioner (typically level 2) supports care, play and routines under supervision and does not fill the level 3 staff:child ratio on their own.`,`An early years educator (level 3, full and relevant) can count in EYFS ratios, plans and observes, and often acts as key person.`,`Room leaders coordinate a room or age band; teaching assistants support teaching, interventions and inclusion under the teacher; teachers are accountable for curriculum, assessment and behaviour; the headteacher holds overall leadership of the setting.`,`Specialist roles: SENDCo (SEND Code of Practice 2015); DSL (Keeping Children Safe in Education); PANCo (physical activity and nutrition in early years); mental health first aider (MHFA).`,`Supporting roles: speech and language therapist; health visitor; social worker; GP; LADO; educational psychologist.`,`The LADO (Local Authority Designated Officer) advises on allegations about adults who work with children; they are not the practitioner who delivers the child's daily care.`,`Supporting professionals usually visit or advise; the setting still holds day-to-day safeguarding and learning duties and must work in partnership with them.`],sections:[{heading:`General roles in early years and schools`,body:[`An early years practitioner works under direction: care routines, play, observations and support for the key person. An early years educator holds a full and relevant level 3 qualification, counts in ratios, plans sequences of play and learning against the EYFS, completes assessments and is often the named key person for a group of children. A room leader (or room supervisor) sets the daily rhythm of a baby, toddler or preschool room, deploys staff, models practice and escalates safeguarding or SEND concerns to the right lead.`,`In schools, a teaching assistant scaffolds learning, runs interventions (for example phonics or speech), supports pupils with SEND and must not be used as a substitute teacher. The class teacher holds qualified teacher status in maintained schools and is accountable for progress, adaptive teaching, assessment and behaviour in that class. The headteacher is the setting's lead professional: vision, staffing, budget, standards and overall accountability, including that a DSL is in post and that safeguarding policy is followed.`,`In the exam, match the job title to a duty the others do not hold. A teaching assistant notices and reports; a teacher plans the sequence of lessons; a headteacher is accountable for the whole school. Do not write that every adult in a nursery is a teacher.`]},{heading:`Specialist roles: SENDCo, DSL, PANCo and MHFA`,body:[`The SENDCo (or SENCO) coordinates the graduated approach (assess, plan, do, review), the SEND register and Education, Health and Care Plan processes, and advises colleagues on adaptations. In maintained mainstream schools the role has specified training expectations (the NPQ for SENCOs, replacing NASENCO). The designated safeguarding lead is the named person for child protection concerns, referrals to children's social care, confidential records and staff training under Keeping Children Safe in Education. Every adult still has a duty to notice and report; only the DSL coordinates the setting's response.`,`The PANCo (Physical Activity and Nutrition Coordinator) is an early years specialist role. They champion healthy eating, physical activity, related policies and staff knowledge so that food, movement and outdoor play are consistent across rooms. They are not a dietitian and they do not replace the DSL or SENDCo.`,`A mental health first aider (MHFA) is trained to spot signs of mental ill-health, listen non-judgementally, give initial support and signpost to the senior mental health lead, GP or specialist services. They are not a counsellor or therapist and they must not keep a disclosure of harm confidential from the DSL. In a scenario, if a Year 9 pupil talks about feeling unable to cope, the MHFA listens and passes on; they do not start a treatment plan.`]},{heading:`Supporting roles beyond the setting`,body:[`Speech and language therapists assess and treat speech, language and communication needs and give the setting strategies (for example visual supports or targeted vocabulary). Health visitors lead the Healthy Child Programme for 0–5, including developmental reviews, and are often the first professional to flag a delay to a nursery. GPs assess health, make referrals and share relevant medical information with consent or under safeguarding procedures.`,`Social workers act under the Children Act 1989 and Children Act 2004 for children in need and children who may be at risk of significant harm. They lead statutory assessment and child protection processes; the setting contributes information and follows the child protection plan. Educational psychologists advise on cognition, learning, development and the child's educational needs, often contributing psychological advice to an EHCP. They do not replace the teacher's daily assessment.`,`The LADO sits in the local authority and manages allegations against people who work with children. If a parent alleges that a member of staff has behaved in a way that may have harmed a child, the DSL or headteacher contacts the LADO for advice; staff do not investigate on their own or alert the accused in a way that could compromise the process. Keep exam answers procedural: name the LADO, the DSL and the duty to report, without graphic detail.`]}],legislation:[{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,year:`2024`,point:`Sets which qualifications are full and relevant for counting in early years staff:child ratios and requires providers to have regard to safeguarding and welfare.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice`,year:`2015`,point:`Requires an identified SENDCo and sets out the graduated approach and partnership with specialists.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Requires a designated safeguarding lead and sets out staff duties to report concerns.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Underpins social work duties for children in need (s.17) and child protection (s.47), and the wider safeguarding partnership including the LADO process.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Introduced EHCPs and strengthened multi-agency duties, including input from educational psychologists and health professionals.`}],examMoves:[`Match the role to a unique duty: DSL for referrals, SENDCo for the graduated approach, PANCo for nutrition and activity, LADO for allegations about adults, MHFA for initial mental-health support and signposting.`,`Do not treat an early years educator as a qualified teacher, or EYTS as QTS, unless the question is about teacher status — this point is roles and responsibilities, not career routes.`,`Apply the setting: nursery answers should use EYE, room leader, PANCo and health visitor; Year 5 answers should use TA, teacher, SENDCo and educational psychologist.`,`For supporting roles, say what they contribute and that the setting still holds day-to-day duty — listing job titles with no function scores little.`]},quiz:[{id:`1-2-q1`,topicId:`1-2`,stem:`In an Ofsted-registered nursery, which practitioner can typically count in the level 3 staff:child ratio?`,options:[`An early years practitioner holding only a full and relevant level 2 qualification`,`A volunteer parent helper`,`A mental health first aider with no early years qualification`,`An early years educator holding a full and relevant level 3 qualification`],answer:3,explanation:`EYFS ratios require a full and relevant level 3 (early years educator) for the level 3 count. Level 2 practitioners support but do not fill that slot. MHFA is a specialist skill, not a ratio qualification.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`1-2-q2`,topicId:`1-2`,stem:`A Reception child has an emerging SEND concern. Who is primarily responsible for coordinating the graduated approach and any later EHCP review?`,options:[`The designated safeguarding lead`,`The special educational needs and disabilities co-ordinator (SENDCo)`,`The PANCo`,`The local authority designated officer (LADO)`],answer:1,explanation:`The SENDCo coordinates assess-plan-do-review and EHCP processes. The DSL handles safeguarding, the PANCo champions activity and nutrition, and the LADO manages allegations against adults — three frequent mix-ups.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`1-2-q3`,topicId:`1-2`,stem:`A parent alleges that a teaching assistant has behaved in a way that may have harmed a child. Which supporting role advises the setting on how to respond to that allegation?`,options:[`The local authority designated officer (LADO)`,`The educational psychologist`,`The health visitor`,`The speech and language therapist`],answer:0,explanation:`The LADO advises on allegations against adults who work with children. Educational psychologists, health visitors and SALTs support the child's learning or health, not allegation management. The DSL or headteacher makes the contact; they do not investigate alone.`,ao:`AO2`}],open:[{id:`1-2-o1`,topicId:`1-2`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the responsibilities of a designated safeguarding lead (DSL) and how they differ from those of a teaching assistant and of the local authority designated officer (LADO).`,scenario:`A teaching assistant in a Year 4 class is worried about a pupil's comments at home time and, separately, a parent later emails an allegation about another member of staff.`,modelAnswer:`The DSL is the named senior person for child protection. They receive concerns, decide on referrals to children's social care, keep secure records, train staff on Keeping Children Safe in Education and liaise with partner agencies. A teaching assistant still has a duty to notice, record and report immediately, but they do not decide whether a referral is made and they must not investigate or promise confidentiality. The LADO is a supporting local-authority role: they advise the setting when an allegation is about an adult who works with children, so the parent email about a staff member goes to the DSL or headteacher, who contacts the LADO rather than dealing with it as a routine pastoral issue. The split matters because delayed or confused reporting leaves the child unprotected: the TA passes on, the DSL coordinates the child's case, and the LADO advises on the adult allegation.`,markScheme:[`Describes DSL duties (referrals, records, training, KCSIE, coordinating concerns) (up to 2)`,`Describes TA duties (notice, record, report; not investigate) (1)`,`Describes the LADO as advising on allegations against adults (1)`,`Makes a clear difference between the three roles / why the split exists (up to 2)`],ao:`AO2`}]}],n=[{id:`2-1`,code:`2.1`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Who regulates education in England, and what regulators do`,page:41,notes:{gist:`Three national regulators shape every setting a T Level student will work in: the Department for Education (DfE) sets policy and statutory frameworks; Ofsted inspects quality of education and safeguarding; Ofqual regulates qualifications and assessments. They do different jobs. Mixing them up — writing that Ofsted writes the National Curriculum, or that Ofqual inspects nurseries — is a high-frequency exam error.`,mustKnow:[`The three regulators named in the specification are the DfE, Ofsted and Ofqual.`,`The DfE sets national education and childcare policy, funding rules and statutory frameworks including the EYFS and the National Curriculum.`,`The DfE does not inspect individual classrooms or award GCSEs; it creates the rules that others enforce and assess against.`,`Ofsted (the Office for Standards in Education, Children's Services and Skills) inspects registered early years provision, state-funded schools, many independent schools, further education and some children's social care.`,`Ofsted judges quality of education and safeguarding against published frameworks and can take regulatory action, including cancelling early years registration.`,`Ofqual (the Office of Qualifications and Examinations Regulation) regulates GCSEs, AS and A levels, T Levels and other regulated vocational qualifications, and the awarding organisations that deliver them.`,`Ofqual's core functions are maintaining standards over time, protecting learners and regulating assessments so a grade in one year is comparable with another.`,`Local authorities still have duties (for example school places, SEND and some early years sufficiency) but they are not one of the three national regulators named here.`],sections:[{heading:`Department for Education (DfE)`,body:[`The DfE is a central government department led by the Secretary of State for Education. It issues statutory guidance and frameworks that settings must follow or have regard to, including the EYFS statutory framework, the National Curriculum programmes of study, Keeping Children Safe in Education and the SEND Code of Practice. It also sets teacher standards, early years qualification expectations used in ratios, and the rules for funded childcare entitlements.`,`Funding policy sits with the DfE: the dedicated schools grant, pupil premium, early years entitlements and 16–19 funding formulae all start as national decisions. Academy funding agreements are with the Secretary of State, which is why academies are independent of the local authority but still bound by DfE rules.`,`In a nursery, the DfE is the reason the EYFS exists; in a maintained primary, it is the reason English, mathematics and science programmes of study are statutory. Practitioners do not write to the DfE about a single child's progress — they implement the frameworks the DfE publishes and are then held to account by inspection and qualifications regulators.`]},{heading:`Ofsted`,body:[`Ofsted inspects so that parents, ministers and the public have an independent judgement of quality and so that weak provision is challenged. In early years it is also the registration authority for most childminders and day nurseries. Inspection frameworks cover quality of education, behaviour and attitudes, personal development, leadership and management, and always safeguarding.`,`Inspectors do not write the curriculum. They evaluate how well leaders implement the EYFS or a broad and balanced school curriculum, including for disadvantaged pupils and those with SEND. Judgements can trigger monitoring, academisation discussions or, in early years, enforcement against the registration.`,`Apply this to a scenario: if a preschool's safeguarding practice is poor, Ofsted is the body that can restrict or cancel registration. If a headteacher wants to change the phonics programme, that is a professional decision within DfE policy, not an Ofqual matter.`]},{heading:`Ofqual`,body:[`Ofqual regulates qualifications, not day-to-day teaching. It decides whether an awarding organisation may offer GCSEs, T Levels or vocational qualifications, sets general conditions, and can fine or restrict an awarding organisation that fails those conditions. It oversees exam delivery, including malpractice and special consideration rules.`,`Standards over time matter for fairness: a grade 5 in GCSE mathematics should mean a comparable level of demand from one year to the next. Ofqual's work on grading, including after disrupted series, is about that comparability. It does not mark every script itself; awarding organisations mark, and Ofqual regulates them.`,`A Year 11 teacher delivering GCSE English is therefore working under all three: the DfE's National Curriculum and accountability measures, Ofsted's inspection of the quality of education, and Ofqual's regulation of the GCSE itself. A childminder with no exam classes still meets DfE (EYFS) and Ofsted (registration and inspection) but rarely Ofqual, unless they later assess a regulated qualification.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Education and Inspections Act`,year:`2006`,point:`Sets Ofsted's functions to inspect and regulate education, early years, skills and some children's services.`},{name:`Apprenticeships, Skills, Children and Learning Act`,year:`2009`,point:`Established Ofqual as the independent regulator of qualifications and assessments in England.`},{name:`Childcare Act`,year:`2006`,point:`Gives Ofsted the registration and regulation function for most early years providers.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`2002`,point:`Gives the Secretary of State (DfE) powers over the National Curriculum in maintained schools.`}],examMoves:[`Name the function, not just the acronym: DfE = policy and frameworks; Ofsted = inspection and early years registration; Ofqual = qualifications and standards.`,`If the stem is a nursery, Ofsted and the DfE (EYFS) are usually enough; only bring in Ofqual if a regulated qualification is being assessed.`,`Do not write that Ofsted writes the National Curriculum or that Ofqual inspects safeguarding walks — markers treat that as a howler.`,`Explain why regulation exists: children's welfare, comparable standards, and accountability for public money.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-1-q1`,topicId:`2-1`,stem:`Which body inspects the quality of education and safeguarding in most state-funded schools and registered early years settings in England?`,options:[`The Department for Education (DfE)`,`Ofqual`,`The local authority designated officer (LADO)`,`Ofsted`],answer:3,explanation:`Ofsted inspects quality and safeguarding. The DfE sets policy and frameworks but does not inspect individual settings; Ofqual regulates qualifications; the LADO advises on allegations against adults.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-1-q2`,topicId:`2-1`,stem:`A secondary school is preparing students for GCSE Combined Science. Which regulator is responsible for maintaining the standard of that qualification from one year to the next?`,options:[`Ofsted, because it observes science lessons`,`Ofqual, because it regulates GCSEs and awarding organisations`,`The local authority, because it employs the teachers`,`The DfE, because it marks every examination script`],answer:1,explanation:`Ofqual regulates GCSEs and comparable standards over time. Ofsted inspects teaching; the DfE sets curriculum policy but does not mark scripts; LAs do not regulate national qualifications.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-1-q3`,topicId:`2-1`,stem:`Which statement best describes a function of the Department for Education?`,options:[`It inspects childminders and can cancel their registration.`,`It awards T Level certificates to individual students.`,`It publishes statutory frameworks such as the EYFS and the National Curriculum programmes of study.`,`It acts as the designated safeguarding lead for every academy trust.`],answer:2,explanation:`The DfE issues statutory frameworks and policy. Inspection and registration of childminders is Ofsted; awarding T Levels sits with awarding organisations under Ofqual; DSLs are setting-based roles.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-1-o1`,topicId:`2-1`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the different functions of Ofsted and Ofqual in the education landscape.`,modelAnswer:`Ofsted inspects the quality of education and safeguarding in registered early years settings, state-funded schools and other provision, and it is the registration authority for most childminders and nurseries. Its function is to report on how well leaders implement the curriculum and keep children safe, and to take regulatory action where provision is weak. Ofqual does not inspect teaching. It regulates qualifications and assessments such as GCSEs, A levels and T Levels, and the awarding organisations that deliver them, so that standards are comparable over time and learners are protected. A Year 6 teacher is therefore inspected by Ofsted on the quality of education, while the KS2 national tests and later GCSEs sit under Ofqual's qualifications regulation.`,markScheme:[`States that Ofsted inspects quality of education and/or safeguarding (1)`,`Develops with registration, frameworks or action on weak provision (1)`,`States that Ofqual regulates qualifications/assessments/awarding organisations (1)`,`Develops with standards over time, named qualifications or contrast with inspection (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`2-2`,code:`2.2`,elementId:`e2`,title:`What shapes priorities and debates in education`,page:41,notes:{gist:`What settings prioritise is not only professional judgement. Governmental policy, technology, economics and funding, and the welfare of practitioners and of children and young people all pull leaders in different directions. Exam answers should name a factor, show the debate it creates, and apply it to a nursery, primary or secondary — not write a news essay.`,mustKnow:[`Four main influences to know: government policy, new technology, money and funding, and the wellbeing of staff and learners.`,`Governmental policy makes some practices statutory (EYFS, phonics screening, RSHE, attendance duties) and therefore a leadership priority.`,`Policy debates include academisation, the SEND and alternative provision improvement agenda, funded childcare hours, and how tightly the National Curriculum should specify content.`,`Advancements in technology (devices, platforms, generative AI) change how teachers plan, assess and communicate, and raise debates about the digital divide, screen time and assessment integrity.`,`Economics and funding include the dedicated schools grant, pupil premium, early years entitlements and the high-needs SEND block; real-terms pressure affects staffing, ratios and what interventions a setting can afford.`,`Welfare of practitioners covers recruitment, retention, workload, pay and professional burnout; shortages of level 3 educators and of teachers directly limit what a setting can offer.`,`Welfare of children and young people covers poverty, mental health, attendance and belonging; settings increasingly prioritise breakfast, pastoral support and a senior mental health lead alongside curriculum goals.`,`These factors interact: a funding squeeze plus a new policy plus staff shortages will shape the same debate (for example, whether to keep a nursery's two-year-old offer).`],sections:[{heading:`Governmental policy and advancements in technology`,body:[`Policy from the DfE tells settings what they must do. The EYFS statutory framework, National Curriculum, phonics screening check, Reception Baseline Assessment, RSHE guidance and Working together to improve school attendance all create non-negotiable priorities. Leaders then debate how to implement them: for example, whether a prescribed phonics programme raises early reading for all, or whether a knowledge-rich KS3 squeezes arts and play.`,`Recent policy on funded entitlements (expanded hours for working parents) has shifted early years priorities towards places and staffing at the same time as Ofsted still judges quality. In secondary, accountability measures (Progress 8, EBacc) influence option blocks and which pupils are entered for which qualifications. Name the policy and the effect on practice, not a party-political rant.`,`Technology is now a daily tool and a debate. Tablets, learning platforms and AI can speed planning, personalise practice and support SEND access, but they also create a digital divide if families lack devices or data, and they raise safeguarding questions about filtering, monitoring and generative-AI cheating. DfE and Keeping Children Safe in Education expectations on online safety mean a secondary cannot treat 'laptops in every bag' as an unexamined good.`]},{heading:`Economics, funding and what settings can actually provide`,body:[`Public money decides ratios, TA hours, specialist support and whether a PVI nursery remains viable. Pupil premium is intended to close gaps for disadvantaged pupils; the high-needs block funds EHCPs. When those budgets are tight, leaders debate whether to cut a reading intervention, increase class size, or reduce the nursery's opening hours.`,`Early years economics are sharp: funded hourly rates, occupancy and the cost of qualified staff determine whether a setting can offer babies as well as funded three-year-olds. A room leader who loses a level 3 educator may no longer meet EYFS ratios, so a funding issue becomes a quality and compliance issue overnight.`,`In a primary, a frozen budget might mean sharing a SENDCo across two schools or losing a speech-and-language intervention. In a secondary, it might mean fewer option subjects at KS4. Exam answers should link the pound to the child's experience, not stop at 'schools are underfunded'.`]},{heading:`Welfare of practitioners and of children and young people`,body:[`Practitioner welfare is a workforce debate: pay, workload, Ofsted pressure, and access to CPD all affect who stays. High turnover in a toddler room breaks key-person attachment; a secondary covering lessons with supply staff weakens behaviour routines and subject knowledge. Prioritising wellbeing policies, PPA time and a reasonable marking load is therefore an educational decision, not a luxury.`,`Children's and young people's welfare sits in the same frame. Poverty, food insecurity, poor housing, waiting lists for mental health services and rising persistent absence all change what a setting must put first. A breakfast club, emotion coaching or a senior mental health lead can be as influential on learning as a new scheme of work, because a hungry or anxious child cannot use working memory on phonics or algebra.`,`The debate is about balance. Critics argue that welfare and 'character' programmes displace knowledge; others argue that ignoring welfare wastes the curriculum because children are not ready to learn. A strong answer weighs both: statutory curriculum remains the entitlement, but it only lands if staffing is stable and children are safe, present and regulated.`]}],legislation:[{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,year:`2024`,point:`A governmental policy document that sets learning, development and welfare priorities for 0–5 provision.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Places online-safety, filtering and monitoring duties on schools and colleges, shaping technology priorities.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`2002`,point:`Underpins the Secretary of State's power to set curriculum policy that schools must implement.`}],examMoves:[`Name one of the four spec factors in the first sentence, then show a debate (for and against, or two competing priorities).`,`Apply to the age and setting: funded hours and ratios for a nursery; pupil premium and phonics for a primary; Progress 8, AI and attendance for a secondary.`,`Analyse means link factor to priority to impact on children or staff — a list of news headlines is AO1 at best.`,`Show interaction where you can: policy without funding, or technology without practitioner welfare, will not deliver the intended improvement.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-2-q1`,topicId:`2-2`,stem:`Which of these is a major influence on priorities and debates in education?`,options:[`Economics and funding`,`The colour of the school uniform`,`The personal hobbies of the chair of governors`,`Whether the setting is north or south of the River Thames`],answer:0,explanation:`The four named factors are governmental policy, advancements in technology, economics and funding, and welfare of practitioners and of children and young people. Uniform colour and geography are not specification factors.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-2-q2`,topicId:`2-2`,stem:`A PVI nursery is deciding whether to keep its baby room open after a change to funded entitlements and a rise in staff costs. Which pair of factors is most clearly in play?`,options:[`Ofqual grading and GCSE reform`,`The National Curriculum at key stage 4 only`,`Governmental policy and economics and funding`,`Elective home education guidance alone`],answer:2,explanation:`Funded entitlements are governmental policy; staff costs and viability are economics and funding. GCSE and KS4 are not the nursery's framework. A strong AO2 answer names both factors and the impact on places and ratios.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-2-q3`,topicId:`2-2`,stem:`Leaders want every Year 10 pupil to use generative AI to draft coursework. Which statement best captures a current debate this creates?`,options:[`Technology always raises attainment, so there is no debate.`,`Ofqual inspects the nursery's baby room if AI is used.`,`Governmental policy forbids all use of computers in KS4.`,`Advancements in technology can support planning and accessibility, but they raise questions about assessment integrity, digital literacy and pupil welfare online.`],answer:3,explanation:`The debate is benefits versus integrity, access and safeguarding — not a blanket 'technology is good' or a false claim that computers are banned. Ofqual does not inspect baby rooms.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`2-2-o1`,topicId:`2-2`,marks:6,command:`Analyse`,stem:`Analyse how two key factors may influence priorities in an educational setting.`,scenario:`Greenfield Primary is a maintained school with a nursery class. Persistent absence has risen, several teaching assistants have left, and the local authority has reduced top-up funding for pupils with EHCPs. The headteacher is also being asked to roll out a new tablet scheme.`,modelAnswer:`Economics and funding are squeezing what Greenfield can offer. Reduced EHCP top-up and TA resignations mean less adult time for tailored interventions, so leaders may have to prioritise statutory EHCP provision over extra reading groups, which risks widening gaps for disadvantaged pupils who relied on those groups. Welfare of practitioners and of children pulls in a second direction: staff shortages increase workload for those who remain, while higher absence suggests some children are not settled or well enough to learn, so pastoral and attendance work becomes a priority even though it competes with curriculum time. Technology cuts across both. Tablets could support SEND access and sharing of information with families, but buying and staffing a scheme during a funding squeeze may look like the wrong priority if it adds to teacher workload or excludes families without Wi-Fi. The analysis is that policy-driven digital plans only work if funding and welfare make implementation possible; otherwise the priority on paper is not the priority children actually experience.`,markScheme:[`Names a relevant key factor (funding, welfare, policy or technology) (1)`,`Analyses how that factor shapes a priority or debate in the scenario (up to 2)`,`Names a second distinct factor (1)`,`Analyses the second factor and/or the interaction between factors and impact on children or staff (up to 2)`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`2-3`,code:`2.3`,elementId:`e2`,title:`What the EYFS and the national curriculum (KS1 to KS4) cover, and why they exist`,page:41,notes:{gist:`The EYFS covers birth to five (the end of Reception). The National Curriculum then runs through primary key stages 1 and 2 (5–11) and secondary key stages 3 and 4 (11–16). Together they give a common entitlement, continuity at transfer and a basis for assessment. Development Matters is non-statutory guidance that helps practitioners implement the EYFS; it is not the law.`,mustKnow:[`EYFS scope: 0–5 years, from birth to the end of the Reception year, for registered early years providers and Reception classes.`,`EYFS purpose: statutory learning, development and safeguarding/welfare requirements so every young child has a consistent entitlement.`,`Development Matters is non-statutory curriculum guidance that helps practitioners plan from the EYFS educational programmes; it does not replace the statutory framework or the Early Learning Goals.`,`Key stage 1 (Years 1–2, ages 5–7) and key stage 2 (Years 3–6, ages 7–11) make up the primary National Curriculum (5–11).`,`Key stage 3 (Years 7–9, ages 11–14) and key stage 4 (Years 10–11, ages 14–16) make up the secondary National Curriculum (11–16).`,`Purpose of the National Curriculum: a broad, balanced, common entitlement, continuity between schools, and a basis for national assessment (for example phonics screening, KS2 tests, GCSEs).`,`Maintained schools must teach the National Curriculum; academies must teach a broad and balanced curriculum including English, mathematics and science, and most still use National Curriculum programmes of study.`,`Do not apply Early Learning Goals to Year 2, or GCSE specifications to a toddler — match the framework to the child's age.`],sections:[{heading:`EYFS 0–5 and Development Matters`,body:[`The EYFS statutory framework sets the educational programmes, the Early Learning Goals used in the EYFS Profile at the end of Reception, the characteristics of effective teaching and learning, and the safeguarding and welfare requirements (including ratios, key person, premises and child protection). It applies to childminders, nurseries, preschools and Reception, so a four-year-old in a PVI nursery and a four-year-old in a school Reception class share the same statutory map.`,`Seven areas of learning comprise three prime areas (communication and language; physical development; personal, social and emotional development) and four specific areas (literacy; mathematics; understanding the world; expressive arts and design). Prime areas are the foundations; specific areas grow from them.`,`Development Matters (currently the 2023 DfE guidance) is non-statutory. It offers checkpoints and examples that help practitioners observe, plan and check typical progress from birth to five. Birth to 5 Matters is similarly guidance from the sector. Exam answers that treat Development Matters as the legal document, or that treat the Early Learning Goals as a curriculum to teach from the baby room, lose marks.`]},{heading:`Primary: key stage 1 and key stage 2 (5–11)`,body:[`Once children leave Reception they enter key stage 1. The National Curriculum programmes of study set out what must be taught in maintained schools. Core subjects — English, mathematics and science — run throughout. Foundation subjects include art and design, computing, design and technology, geography, history, music and PE, with a modern foreign language from key stage 2. RE and relationships education sit alongside via separate statute.`,`Purpose at primary is mastery of the basics, a broad experience of the disciplines, and comparable assessment: the Year 1 phonics screening check, the Year 4 multiplication tables check and key stage 2 national tests and teacher assessment in Year 6. These outcomes inform the Year 7 teacher and national accountability; they are not the whole curriculum.`,`A Year 2 teacher planning reading therefore uses the National Curriculum English programme of study and the school's phonics programme, not the EYFS educational programmes. Children who were 'emerging' against ELGs still need a KS1 curriculum that builds from that starting point, with SEND support where needed.`]},{heading:`Secondary: key stage 3 and key stage 4 (11–16)`,body:[`Key stage 3 (11–14) continues a broad National Curriculum, adding citizenship and a compulsory modern foreign language alongside the cores and foundations. Its purpose is to deepen subject knowledge before pupils specialise. Key stage 4 (14–16) retains English, mathematics, science, computing, PE and citizenship, with religious education and RSHE, while options — often including EBacc subjects such as history, geography and languages — lead to GCSEs and equivalent qualifications regulated by Ofqual.`,`Scope stops at 16 for this specification point. Post-16 study programmes (A levels, T Levels, apprenticeships) are not a continuation of the National Curriculum. A 15-year-old in Year 10 is KS4; a 12-year-old in Year 7 is KS3; neither is in the EYFS.`,`Purpose across KS1–KS4 is entitlement and coherence: a child who moves from a community primary to an academy secondary should not lose science or PE because of postcode. Inspection then judges how well that entitlement is implemented for all pupils, including those with SEND and those who speak English as an additional language.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Education Reform Act`,year:`1988`,point:`Introduced the first National Curriculum and national assessment in England.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`2002`,point:`Requires maintained schools to deliver the National Curriculum and a broad, balanced curriculum.`},{name:`Childcare Act`,year:`2006`,point:`Underpins the requirement for registered early years providers to deliver the EYFS.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,year:`2024`,point:`Sets the statutory learning, development and welfare requirements for 0–5.`}],examMoves:[`Name the three prime and four specific areas in full if asked — initials alone rarely score.`,`State that Development Matters is non-statutory whenever a question contrasts guidance with the framework.`,`Match the age in the scenario: Reception = EYFS; Year 4 = KS2; Year 8 = KS3; Year 11 = KS4.`,`For purpose questions write entitlement, consistency/continuity and assessment — not a history essay unless asked.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-3-q1`,topicId:`2-3`,stem:`What is the status of Development Matters in relation to the EYFS?`,options:[`It is the statutory assessment document replacing the Early Learning Goals.`,`It is non-statutory curriculum guidance that helps practitioners implement the EYFS.`,`It is Ofqual's specification for T Levels.`,`It is compulsory only in academies at key stage 4.`],answer:1,explanation:`Development Matters is non-statutory. The EYFS framework and the ELGs are statutory. Mixing guidance with law is a classic AO1 error.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-3-q2`,topicId:`2-3`,stem:`A Year 6 teacher in a maintained school is planning for the same cohort next year. Which framework applies once they enter Year 7?`,options:[`The National Curriculum at key stage 3`,`The EYFS statutory framework`,`The National Curriculum at key stage 1`,`Development Matters checkpoints for birth to three`],answer:0,explanation:`Year 7 is the start of KS3 (11–14, secondary). KS1 is Years 1–2; the EYFS ends at Reception; Development Matters does not apply to 11-year-olds.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-3-q3`,topicId:`2-3`,stem:`Which of the following is a prime area of learning in the EYFS?`,options:[`Understanding the world`,`Literacy`,`Personal, social and emotional development`,`Expressive arts and design`],answer:2,explanation:`PSED is a prime area, with communication and language and physical development. The other options are specific areas.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-3-o1`,topicId:`2-3`,marks:4,command:`Outline`,stem:`Outline the scope of the EYFS and of the National Curriculum at key stages 1 to 4.`,modelAnswer:`The EYFS covers children from birth to five, through to the end of the Reception year, and sets statutory learning, development and welfare requirements for registered early years providers and Reception classes. Development Matters supports implementation but is non-statutory guidance. The National Curriculum then applies from Year 1: key stages 1 and 2 are the primary phase for ages 5–11, and key stages 3 and 4 are the secondary phase for ages 11–16, giving a common entitlement in core and foundation subjects and a basis for national assessment up to GCSE. Maintained schools must deliver it; the two frameworks should not be mixed across age groups.`,markScheme:[`States EYFS covers 0–5 / birth to end of Reception (1)`,`Notes statutory learning and welfare requirements or that Development Matters is guidance (1)`,`States KS1–KS2 as primary 5–11 (1)`,`States KS3–KS4 as secondary 11–16 / purpose of entitlement or assessment (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`2-4`,code:`2.4`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Professional skills and qualities, and why they matter for learners`,page:41,notes:{gist:`Professional behaviour is both what practitioners do (skills) and how they are (attributes). Skills without attributes look cold; warmth without skill looks careless. Paper answers score when a named skill or attribute is paired with why it raises participation, progress or behaviour for a child of a stated age.`,mustKnow:[`Skills: let learners help plan their own learning, so they have a say and can explain their next steps.`,`Skills: communicating clearly using positive, age- and stage-appropriate language; avoiding sarcasm and unexplained jargon.`,`Skills: giving effective feedback and facilitating self-reflection so learners can close gaps.`,`Skills: managing your own and others' time, including transitions, group rotations and TA deployment.`,`Skills: providing nurturing experiences so children and young people can express feelings, which promotes positive behaviour.`,`Skills: observing and assessing, then putting in tailored interventions through early identification — wait-to-fail is not a strategy.`,`Skills: engaging children and young people in their own learning and assessment, including those who have switched off.`,`Qualities to name: empathy, patience, respect, confidence, being approachable, subject knowledge, enthusiasm, a positive outlook, being reflective and being resourceful.`],sections:[{heading:`Skills that support learning and behaviour`,body:[`Involving children in planning — choice boards and 'what do we want to find out?' in the nursery, shared success criteria in KS2, negotiated revision plans in KS4 — makes goals visible and raises motivation. Communication must match the stage: a two-year-old needs short, positive instructions and a visual timetable; a Year 10 needs precise subject vocabulary without humiliation. Positive language tells the child what to do, not only what to stop.`,`Effective feedback is specific, timely and tied to the criterion, then followed by time to improve. Facilitating self-reflection means teaching children to use a checklist, a talk partner or a learning journal rather than waiting for the adult to notice every error. Observation and early identification turn that information into a tailored intervention: a targeted phonics group in Year 1, a language group in preschool, a catch-up tutor in Year 8, instead of waiting for statutory assessment to fail the child.`,`Time management is a professional skill because lost minutes are lost learning. A TA decorating a display during the teacher's input is wasted support; a nursery that overruns snack by twenty minutes shrinks continuous provision. Nurture sits alongside this: children who can name and express feelings, with a key person or tutor who co-regulates, are more likely to show positive behaviour and to stay in the room long enough to learn.`]},{heading:`Attributes and why they change outcomes`,body:[`Approachability and respect make it safe to ask a 'silly' question; without them, misconceptions stay hidden. Empathy and patience keep a child with SEND, EAL or an unsettled home in the learning, because the adult waits, adapts and does not take behaviour personally. Confidence and knowledge mean the practitioner can change the task mid-session rather than lower the ceiling or freeze.`,`Passion and positivity are contagious motivation: a practitioner who clearly values the activity invites children to value it too. Reflection and resourcefulness turn a failed activity into tomorrow's plan and find a different resource when the first one misses. These attributes are evidenced in behaviour — greeting by name, arriving prepared, admitting a mistake, following through on a promise to a parent — not in a poster on the staff-room wall.`,`In a Year 2 class with a withdrawn latecomer and a child with speech needs, empathy settles the first child without a public interrogation, and patience gives the second child processing time. The skills (observation, feedback, nurture) only work if the attributes make the relationship safe.`]},{heading:`Why the combination matters for education`,body:[`Skills and attributes together are what the specification means by professional behaviour. A knowledgeable teacher who gives no wait time, or a warm TA who never shares an observation with the SENDCo, will not effectively support education. Engaging children in their own assessment is the test: do they know what they are learning, how they will know they have learned it, and what to do when they are stuck?`,`Age and stage change the application, not the list. A toddler needs co-regulation and a key person; a Year 6 pupil needs success criteria and time to act on marking; a Year 11 student needs honest feedback and a share in planning revision. In every case the why is the same: participation, progress, positive behaviour and a child who can talk about their own learning.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Carl Rogers`,idea:`Unconditional positive regard, empathy and congruence create a climate in which learners will take risks — the humanist root of several professional attributes.`},{name:`John Hattie`,idea:`Teacher–student relationships and feedback have high effects when they make the next step visible, supporting the skill of effective feedback and self-reflection.`}],examMoves:[`Do not list ten attributes with no why — pick two and link each to a child's learning or behaviour.`,`Command word Explain = named skill or attribute + because + impact on education.`,`Apply to the age in the stem: co-regulation and key person in nursery; success criteria and self-assessment in KS2–4.`,`If the child is disengaged, write about involving them in planning and assessment, not only 'make it fun'.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-4-q1`,topicId:`2-4`,stem:`Which professional attribute most directly helps a practitioner notice that a planned activity is not working and change it mid-session?`,options:[`Passion`,`The seniority of the headteacher`,`Punctuality of parents`,`Reflection`],answer:3,explanation:`Reflection is the in-the-moment and after-the-event review that drives adaptation. Passion motivates but does not by itself diagnose a failing task; seniority is not on the specification attribute list.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-4-q2`,topicId:`2-4`,stem:`A Year 8 pupil has stopped handing in work. Which action shows the strongest professional skills?`,options:[`Publicly compare their effort with a higher-attaining peer.`,`Remove all feedback so they are not stressed.`,`Involve them in setting a short-term target and how they will self-assess it.`,`Wait until the next parents' evening in four months.`],answer:2,explanation:`The specification emphasises involving disengaged learners in their own learning and assessment, plus effective feedback. Public comparison damages safety; delaying or withholding feedback blocks progress.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-4-q3`,topicId:`2-4`,stem:`Why should practitioners provide nurturing experiences that allow children to express feelings?`,options:[`Because expressing feelings promotes positive behaviour and frees children to learn.`,`Because it replaces the need for any behaviour boundaries.`,`Because Ofqual requires a feelings diary for GCSE entry.`,`Because only secondary pupils are permitted to name emotions.`],answer:0,explanation:`The specification links nurture and emotional expression to positive behaviour. It does not abolish boundaries, and it applies from early years upwards, not only at GCSE.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-4-o1`,topicId:`2-4`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how one professional skill and one professional attribute help practitioners support children and young people's education.`,scenario:`A new teaching assistant is joining a Year 2 class that includes a child with speech and language needs and a child who is often late and withdrawn.`,modelAnswer:`A relevant skill is giving effective feedback and facilitating self-reflection. The TA can use specific, visual success criteria in phonics so the child with speech needs knows what a good answer looks like, wait for a full response, and then help the child tick what they managed, which builds independence rather than adult prompt-dependence. A relevant attribute is empathy. Empathy helps the TA read the withdrawn child's lateness as a possible unmet need rather than rudeness, greet them privately and settle them before learning starts, which protects self-esteem and attention. Together, the skill and the attribute make approachability real: children will risk a wrong answer. Without empathy, even a well-planned intervention fails because the child will not talk; without the skill of feedback, warmth does not show the child how to improve.`,markScheme:[`Names a specification skill (1)`,`Explains how that skill supports education in the scenario (up to 2)`,`Names a specification attribute (1)`,`Explains how that attribute supports education / links skill and attribute to impact (up to 2)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-5`,code:`2.5`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Pedagogical approaches and how they are used in education`,page:42,notes:{gist:`Five theoretical approaches sit behind everyday pedagogy: behaviourism, cognitive constructivism, social constructivism, connectivism and humanism. Each has a distinct view of how learning happens and a matching set of teaching methods. Exam answers must pair the theory with a named pedagogy and an age-appropriate example, not dump every theorist from 2.6.`,mustKnow:[`Behaviourism: learning as a change in observable behaviour through antecedents, consequences, association and continuity; operant conditioning uses positive and negative reinforcement.`,`Behaviourist pedagogy: questioning structured as antecedent–behaviour–consequence, and direct instruction (teacher-led input, practise together, check understanding, feedback).`,`Cognitive constructivism: learners build schemas through assimilation and accommodation in sequential, schematic stages.`,`Cognitive-constructivist pedagogy: HighScope plan-do-review and project-based learning.`,`Social constructivism: learning as an active social process shaped by the quality of interactions and by environment, home and culture.`,`Social-constructivist pedagogy: enquiry-based learning, modelling, flipped learning, commentary, sustained shared thinking and reflection.`,`Connectivism: learning as making links between nodes of information, often through technology and AI; 'knowing where' and currency of information matter, including informal learning.`,`Connectivist pedagogy: MOOCs, social networking, gamification and immersive learning.`,`Humanism: holistic, student-centred learning towards self-actualisation and agency. Pedagogy: student-initiated learning, holistic coaching and a safe nurturing environment.`],sections:[{heading:`Behaviourism and cognitive constructivism`,body:[`Behaviourism (Pavlov, Watson, Skinner — developed in 2.6) treats learning as association and as a response to what comes before (antecedent) and after (consequence) a behaviour. Positive reinforcement adds a desirable consequence (specific praise, a token); negative reinforcement removes an aversive one (earning a break from a noisy hall). Continuity matters: the same behaviour should meet the same response. In a Reception class, a visual 'now and next' card is an antecedent; calm praise for lining up is a consequence.`,`Pedagogy that follows is often teacher-led. Direct instruction sequences explain, model, practise together, check understanding and give feedback — a phonics lesson or a KS3 science demonstration. Questioning can be planned as ABC: the question (antecedent) cues the response (behaviour) and the teacher's follow-up (consequence) reinforces or reshapes it. Used well it gives clarity; used poorly it becomes bribery or ignores why the child was distressed.`,`Cognitive constructivism, associated with Piaget, says children are not empty vessels. They assimilate new information into existing schemas or accommodate by changing the schema when it no longer fits, moving through sequential stages. HighScope's plan-do-review cycle in nursery and Reception makes that construction visible: the child plans at a pegboard, does the play, then reviews with an adult. Project-based learning in KS2 (for example a local-history enquiry built over weeks) lets pupils test and rebuild ideas rather than only receive them.`]},{heading:`Social constructivism and connectivism`,body:[`Social constructivism (Vygotsky's tradition) holds that learning is social first. The quality of talk, the more knowledgeable other, and the child's home and cultural tools all shape what can be understood. Pedagogy therefore invests in interactions: modelling a sentence, commentary while a toddler pours, sustained shared thinking in the block area, enquiry-based questions in KS3 geography, flipped learning where KS4 pupils meet content at home and use lesson time for discussion, and structured reflection.`,`Sustained shared thinking is a high-value early years example: the adult and child think together, not the adult testing the child. Flipped learning is a secondary example of the same idea — lesson time becomes the social construction, not the first transmission. Environment matters: a language-rich role-play corner and a well-planned talk-partner routine are both social-constructivist spaces.`,`Connectivism (Siemens and Downes) argues that in a digital age the network is the learning. Knowledge lives in nodes — people, sites, databases, AI tools — and the skill is to know where to find current information and how to link it. Pedagogy includes MOOCs for staff or sixth-form stretch, educational social networking, gamification (points, quests, immediate feedback) and immersive learning (VR fieldwork, simulated labs). A Year 10 computing class fact-checking AI output is practising currency and 'know where', not just copying a handout.`]},{heading:`Humanism and choosing an approach`,body:[`Humanism (Maslow, Rogers) sees the learner as a whole person with emotional and social needs, moving towards self-actualisation and agency. Pedagogy is student-initiated: children choose challenges in continuous provision; a tutor coaches a young person on goals rather than only on marks; the environment is psychologically safe so that risk-taking is possible. Without safety, behaviourist tokens and constructivist projects both stall.`,`Approaches are not mutually exclusive in a real setting. A Monday phonics lesson may be direct instruction (behaviourist/cognitive), the afternoon block play may be sustained shared thinking (social constructivist), a Friday independent project may be humanist, and a homework quiz on an app may be connectivist gamification. The exam skill is to name the approach that the question is pointing to and apply it, not to declare one theory always best.`]}],theorists:[{name:`B. F. Skinner`,idea:`Operant conditioning — behaviour is shaped by reinforcement and consequences, underpinning behaviourist pedagogy.`},{name:`Jean Piaget`,idea:`Cognitive constructivism — assimilation, accommodation and sequential stages; children construct schemas.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Social constructivism — learning through social interaction, culture and the zone of proximal development.`},{name:`George Siemens`,idea:`Connectivism — learning as network-forming across technology nodes, with emphasis on currency and 'knowing where'.`},{name:`Abraham Maslow / Carl Rogers`,idea:`Humanism — hierarchy of needs, self-actualisation, student-centred climate and agency.`}],examMoves:[`Pair theory with pedagogy: do not write 'Piaget' if the question asks for HighScope or plan-do-review — name the approach (cognitive constructivism) and the method.`,`Apply to the setting: HighScope and sustained shared thinking for nursery; direct instruction and flipped learning for secondary.`,`Explain = how the mechanism (reinforcement, accommodation, ZPD, nodes, agency) produces learning in that example.`,`For compare questions, contrast what is being learned (a behaviour vs a schema vs a social meaning vs a network vs a whole person).`]},quiz:[{id:`2-5-q1`,topicId:`2-5`,stem:`HighScope's plan-do-review cycle is an example of which theoretical approach?`,options:[`Behaviourism`,`Cognitive constructivism`,`Connectivism`,`Humanism only, with no cognitive element`],answer:1,explanation:`Plan-do-review is named in the specification as cognitive-constructivist pedagogy (schemas built through sequential, active experience). Behaviourism would emphasise reinforcement; connectivism emphasises digital networks.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-5-q2`,topicId:`2-5`,stem:`A Year 9 teacher sets a short video for homework and uses the lesson for enquiry talk and modelling. Which approach and pedagogy are being combined most clearly?`,options:[`Behaviourism and token economy only`,`Connectivism, because any homework is a MOOC`,`Cognitive constructivism, because the video is HighScope plan-do-review`,`Social constructivism, using flipped learning, modelling and enquiry-based learning`],answer:3,explanation:`Flipped learning, modelling and enquiry are social-constructivist pedagogies in the specification. A homework video is not automatically a MOOC, and HighScope is an early years plan-do-review cycle, not a KS3 video.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-5-q3`,topicId:`2-5`,stem:`Which statement best describes connectivism?`,options:[`Learning is making and updating links between nodes of information, often using technology and AI, so that knowing where to find current knowledge matters.`,`Learning is only the result of teacher-led drill and reinforcement.`,`Learning is constructing schemas through assimilation and accommodation in fixed stages, with no role for technology.`,`Learning cannot happen unless the child is silent for the first six months.`],answer:0,explanation:`Connectivism centres on networks, nodes, currency and 'know where'. Drill is behaviourist; schemas and stages are cognitive constructivism; a silent period is an EAL stage, not a learning theory.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-5-o1`,topicId:`2-5`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how two theoretical approaches can be applied through pedagogy in an educational setting.`,scenario:`A Reception class uses a morning phonics session, then continuous provision in which children plan their play, and an adult joins two children in the construction area to think aloud about how to make a bridge stronger.`,modelAnswer:`The phonics session is well matched to behaviourist pedagogy. The teacher provides an antecedent (the grapheme and a clear question), children produce the sound (behaviour), and immediate praise or corrective feedback is the consequence, with direct instruction: explain, practise together, check, feedback. That continuity of reinforcement helps early reading become automatic. Continuous provision with a plan-do-review conversation is cognitive constructivism: children assimilate new block-play ideas into existing schemas or accommodate when the bridge collapses, which is HighScope in practice. The adult's sustained shared thinking in the construction area is social constructivism — learning as a quality interaction in which commentary and joint problem-solving sit in the child's zone of what they can do with help. Using more than one approach in a day is legitimate; the phonics need for accuracy does not cancel the need for construction of meaning in play.`,markScheme:[`Names a valid theoretical approach (1)`,`Links it to a named pedagogy and the scenario (up to 2)`,`Names a second distinct approach (1)`,`Links the second to pedagogy and impact on learning (up to 2)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-6`,code:`2.6`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Learning theories and how they shape education`,page:44,notes:{gist:`Named theorists explain why particular pedagogies work. Bandura explains role modelling; Piaget explains schematic stages; Pavlov, Watson and Skinner explain conditioning; Bruner explains discovery, modes, scaffolding and the spiral curriculum; Chomsky explains an innate language acquisition device; Vygotsky explains social interaction and the zone of proximal development. Influence on education means a concrete classroom or nursery practice, not a biography.`,mustKnow:[`Bandura (social learning): children learn by observing models; attention, retention, reproduction and motivation, plus vicarious reinforcement, explain role modelling.`,`Piaget (schematic/cognitive): sensorimotor (0–2), preoperational (2–7), concrete operational (7–11), formal operational (11+); schemas change by assimilation and accommodation.`,`Pavlov (classical conditioning): association between a neutral stimulus and a response. Watson applied behaviourism to children; Skinner (operant conditioning) emphasised reinforcement and consequences.`,`Bruner (discovery learning): three modes of representation — enactive, iconic and symbolic; scaffolding; spiral curriculum — the same idea revisited with increasing demand. (The v2.0 spec uses 'modes of representation', not 'modes of development'.)`,`Chomsky: a language acquisition device (LAD) and universal grammar — children are biologically prepared to acquire language, not only by imitation.`,`Vygotsky: learning is shaped by social interactions and culture; the zone of proximal development (ZPD) is what a child can do with a more knowledgeable other.`,`Influence means practice: modelling calm talk (Bandura), CPA maths (Bruner), talk partners in the ZPD (Vygotsky), not naming the theory and stopping.`],sections:[{heading:`Social learning and behavioural conditioning`,body:[`Bandura's Bobo-doll work showed that children imitate aggression they have observed, especially if the model is rewarded. In a nursery, staff who narrate sharing ('you waited, now you have a turn') become models; in a secondary, a peer mentor who talks through revision is a social model. Vicarious reinforcement — seeing someone else praised — can spread a behaviour without every child being rewarded directly. Influence: planned role modelling of oracy, behaviour for learning and online conduct.`,`Pavlov's classical conditioning pairs a stimulus with a response (a bell associated with food). In settings, a tidy-up song that always precedes stopping play can itself settle children because it has been associated with the transition. Watson argued that environments shape behaviour; the ethical lesson is that adults should not create fear associations (shouting, public humiliation).`,`Skinner's operant conditioning is the more common classroom legacy: positive reinforcement (specific praise, house points used carefully), negative reinforcement (removal of a disliked extra drill once work is complete), and extinction (not feeding low-level attention-seeking). Influence: consistent behaviour policies, but not bribery that ignores unmet need. A sticker chart that shames a child with SEND is a misuse of Skinner, not an application.`]},{heading:`Piaget, Bruner and discovery`,body:[`Piaget's stages still influence what adults judge 'age-appropriate'. Sensorimotor infants learn through senses and movement — treasure baskets, not worksheets. Preoperational children (nursery to early KS1) are egocentric, use symbolic play and are not yet reliable at conservation, so practical, visual activity beats abstract number talk alone. Concrete operational pupils (KS2) can classify and reverse operations with apparatus; formal operational adolescents (KS3–4) can hypothesise, which is why GCSE science investigations and debate become possible. Influence: match demand to stage, while remembering that stages are typical, not walls.`,`Bruner accepted construction of knowledge but was less rigid about ages. Enactive (doing), iconic (images) and symbolic (language and symbols) modes underpin the concrete–pictorial–abstract approach in primary mathematics. Scaffolding is temporary help that is withdrawn as competence grows — a writing frame, a worked example, a guided group. The spiral curriculum revisits place value, fractions or historical concepts each year with more demand, which is how National Curriculum sequencing is often designed.`,`Discovery learning, for Bruner, is not leaving children to guess without structure. It is arranging the environment so they can find the pattern, then scaffolding the language to describe it. A poorly resourced 'discover it yourself' lesson without a spiral or a scaffold is not Bruner.`]},{heading:`Chomsky, Vygotsky and language in the social world`,body:[`Chomsky argued that children acquire grammar too quickly and too creatively to be explained by imitation and reinforcement alone; a language acquisition device (LAD) prepares them to extract universal grammatical patterns from the talk they hear. Influence: a language-rich environment is not optional extra — it feeds an innate system. It also cautions behaviourist drill: repeating a sentence is not the whole of language development. For EAL, Chomsky supports continued home-language development rather than silencing it, because the LAD is not English-specific.`,`Vygotsky placed language and culture at the centre. What a child can do independently is their actual level; what they can do with a more knowledgeable other is the ZPD — the only place teaching has traction. Influence: guided writing, talk partners, sustained shared thinking, worked examples just above current performance, and careful TA deployment so adults do not do the task for the child (which removes the ZPD).`,`Taken together, these perspectives influence different parts of a day. A teacher might model (Bandura), scaffold a problem just above independent level (Bruner and Vygotsky), use apparatus because pupils are still concrete (Piaget), and flood the room with talk because of Chomsky. The exam wants the named idea and the practice it justifies.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Social learning theory — observational learning and role modelling, including vicarious reinforcement.`},{name:`Jean Piaget`,idea:`Four sequential stages of cognitive development and the construction of schemas.`},{name:`Ivan Pavlov / John B. Watson / B. F. Skinner`,idea:`Classical and operant conditioning — association, reinforcement and the shaping of observable behaviour.`},{name:`Jerome Bruner`,idea:`Discovery learning, three modes of representation, scaffolding and the spiral curriculum.`},{name:`Noam Chomsky`,idea:`Language acquisition device and universal grammar — nativist account of language.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Social interactions, cultural tools and the zone of proximal development.`}],examMoves:[`Name the theorist, the key term (ZPD, LAD, spiral, vicarious reinforcement, conservation) and one applied example.`,`Do not muddle Piaget's stages with Vygotsky's ZPD — one is a typical sequence, the other is a social space for teaching.`,`If the question says 'influence on education', spend more words on the practice than on the biography.`,`Match the age: sensorimotor to babies; formal operations and hypothesising to KS3–4; scaffolding at any age.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-6-q1`,topicId:`2-6`,stem:`Which theorist is most closely associated with the zone of proximal development?`,options:[`Piaget`,`Skinner`,`Vygotsky`,`Pavlov`],answer:2,explanation:`Vygotsky described the ZPD as what a learner can do with a more knowledgeable other. Piaget described stages; Skinner and Pavlov described conditioning.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-6-q2`,topicId:`2-6`,stem:`A Year 5 teacher uses Dienes apparatus, then a bar model, then digits alone to teach fractions, and returns to fractions in Year 6 at a higher level. Which theorist's ideas are being applied?`,options:[`Chomsky's language acquisition device only`,`Pavlov's unconditioned reflex`,`Bandura's Bobo doll as a maths resource`,`Bruner's enactive, iconic and symbolic modes and spiral curriculum`],answer:3,explanation:`Apparatus (enactive), bar model (iconic) and digits (symbolic), revisited later, is Bruner. Chomsky is about innate language; Pavlov is classical conditioning; Bandura is observational learning.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-6-q3`,topicId:`2-6`,stem:`According to Chomsky, why is a language-rich environment important?`,options:[`Because an innate language acquisition device needs a rich sample of language in order to extract grammatical patterns.`,`Because children have no biological preparation for language and must be drilled in every grammar rule.`,`Because language can only be learned through sticker charts.`,`Because Piaget said language cannot develop until formal operations at 16.`],answer:0,explanation:`Chomsky is nativist: the LAD is innate but still requires input. Pure drill is a behaviourist reading; sticker charts are operant; Piaget did not delay all language until 16.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-6-q4`,topicId:`2-6`,stem:`Which phrase does the current (v2.0) specification use for Bruner's enactive, iconic and symbolic stages?`,options:[`Modes of development`,`Modes of representation`,`Stages of moral reasoning`,`The four identity statuses`],answer:1,explanation:`NCFE spec v2.0 (April 2026) corrected the wording to Bruner's modes of representation: enactive (doing), iconic (images) and symbolic (language and symbols). 'Modes of development' was the old spec slip. Kohlberg is moral reasoning; Marcia is identity statuses.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-6-o1`,topicId:`2-6`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how two theoretical perspectives influence practice in education.`,scenario:`In a Year 1 class, the teacher models how to blend a CVC word, then sits with a guided group just beyond what they can read alone. In the nursery next door, staff narrate sharing and ignore a child who is shouting for attention at snack.`,modelAnswer:`Bandura's social learning theory influences the Year 1 modelling and the nursery narration. Children attend to a respected model, retain the sequence, reproduce it and are motivated by seeing the behaviour work or being praised; staff who calmly demonstrate blending or sharing provide that model, and peers who watch a child being praised for waiting experience vicarious reinforcement. Vygotsky's perspective influences the guided reading group: the teacher works in the zone of proximal development, offering prompts a more knowledgeable other would give and withdrawing them as the child blends independently. Skinner's operant ideas also sit in the nursery snack example: shouting is not reinforced (extinction) while waiting is positively reinforced, so the consequence teaches the behaviour the setting wants. The perspectives are compatible: children imitate models and they also need tasks pitched in the ZPD, with consequences that are consistent rather than shaming.`,markScheme:[`Names a valid theorist/perspective (1)`,`Explains the key idea and links it to practice in the scenario (up to 2)`,`Names a second distinct theorist/perspective (1)`,`Explains the second idea, practice and influence on learning or behaviour (up to 2)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-7`,code:`2.7`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Using current, suitable technology to support learning`,page:45,notes:{gist:`Technology supports education when it is current, age-appropriate and purposeful: monitoring progress, sharing information, offering a variety of media, designing and delivering materials and assessments, widening SEND access, enabling safe collaboration, preparing for future learning and careers, and building digital literacy. A tablet is not automatically good pedagogy. Inappropriate, out-of-date or unsafe use creates gaps, data risks and safeguarding issues.`,mustKnow:[`Accurate monitoring of progress: digital assessment, learning journals and trackers can show gaps quickly if data are entered and interpreted well.`,`Sharing information: secure platforms let teachers, TAs, SENDCos and families see the same observations, targets and homework.`,`Variety of media: audio, video, animation, simulations and interactive texts support different ways of accessing content.`,`Planning, designing and delivering materials and assessments: teachers use software to sequence lessons, create quizzes and adapt tasks.`,`Accessibility for SEND: text-to-speech, speech-to-text, captioning, switch access and overlay tools can remove barriers (Equality Act 2010).`,`Safe online communication and collaboration: filtering, monitoring, age-appropriate platforms and KCSIE duties.`,`Gets learners ready for later study and work: use real tools, not only closed games.`,`Digital literacy: evaluating sources, knowing how AI works, understanding privacy and behaving respectfully online.`],sections:[{heading:`Progress, information, media and design`,body:[`Up-to-date systems (for example a current EYFS learning journal or a school MIS) let practitioners record observations against the right framework and spot a child whose communication is not moving. Accuracy depends on professional judgement: a click-box that is never moderated is not assessment. Used well, the same system shares a Reception child's profile with the Year 1 teacher and with parents who cannot attend at 3.30 pm.`,`A variety of media matters because a single worksheet excludes some learners. A KS2 history enquiry can combine a short film, a map, a podcast clip and a museum app; a nursery can photograph block play and play the sequence back so children review (HighScope). Technology here serves cognition, not entertainment.`,`Planning and assessment tools help adults design sequences and low-stakes quizzes that give fast feedback. Generative AI can draft a differentiated resource, but the practitioner remains accountable for accuracy, bias and pitch. Out-of-date software that cannot open a pupil's access arrangement, or a platform the LA no longer supports, fails the 'up-to-date and appropriate' test.`]},{heading:`SEND access, safety and collaboration`,body:[`For SEND, technology is often a reasonable adjustment: a Year 8 pupil with literacy difficulties using read-aloud and a laptop in class; a child with physical needs using a switch to choose symbols; captioned video for a pupil with hearing loss. The Equality Act 2010 requires settings not to discriminate; appropriate technology is one way barriers are removed. It does not replace human relationships or a well-pitched task.`,`Safe online communication is a safeguarding duty, not an ICT extra. Keeping Children Safe in Education requires filtering and monitoring, staff who can act on alerts, and teaching that matches the age — from 'who can take your photo' in Reception to deepfakes and generative AI in KS4. Collaboration tools (shared docs, class blogs, video calls with a partner school) are powerful only on platforms the setting can supervise.`,`Home contact must follow GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018: no progress photos on a personal phone, no unsecured WhatsApp groups for a class. A nanny or childminder using a parent app still needs consent and a secure device. Inappropriate technology includes any channel that staff cannot monitor or that exposes a child's image or data.`]},{heading:`Future learning, careers and digital literacy`,body:[`Using real tools — spreadsheets in KS2, CAD in KS3 design, data loggers in science, assistive software that pupils will meet in college — prepares children for the next stage and for work. Closed, cartoonish apps that cannot transfer a skill do less of that job. Careers education (Gatsby benchmarks in secondary) includes digital workplaces; ignoring technology leaves pupils unprepared.`,`Digital literacy is the curriculum content that makes the tools educational: searching with a question, checking a source, knowing that an AI chatbot can hallucinate, protecting a password, and speaking respectfully in a shared document. Without it, technology can spread misinformation and harm. Teach it in context — evaluating websites in KS2 geography, referencing in KS4 — not as a one-off assembly.`,`Appropriate also means the child's stage. A toddler needs limited, adult-joint screen time that extends play, not a tablet as a pacifier. A Year 11 needs access that includes unsupervised research with taught criticality. The same device can support or undermine education depending on the adult's purpose, the software's currency and the safety wrap-around.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Requires schools and colleges to manage online safety, including filtering and monitoring, as part of safeguarding.`},{name:`Data Protection Act / UK GDPR`,year:`2018`,point:`Governs how settings store, share and delete children's digital information and images.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Requires reasonable adjustments; accessible technology can be part of removing barriers for SEND.`}],examMoves:[`Do not write 'technology is engaging' as a full answer — name a spec purpose (monitoring, SEND access, digital literacy) and an example.`,`Up-to-date means current software, current safeguarding settings and current professional knowledge (including AI), not a 2012 CD-ROM.`,`Appropriate means matched to age, need and task, and safe — a secondary collaboration tool is not automatically right in a baby room.`,`Finish with impact on the child's learning or safety, and the adult's remaining responsibility.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-7-q1`,topicId:`2-7`,stem:`Which use of technology best matches 'accurate monitoring of progress'?`,options:[`Letting children play any online game during wet play with no adult purpose`,`Using a current learning journal or tracker, moderated by the teacher, to spot a gap in phonics and plan a group`,`Posting a child's assessment scores on a public social network`,`Banning all devices so that progress can only be stored in the headteacher's memory`],answer:1,explanation:`Monitoring means purposeful, accurate digital records that change teaching. Unsupervised games, public posting (a data and safeguarding breach) and a total ban do not meet the specification purpose.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-7-q2`,topicId:`2-7`,stem:`A Year 8 pupil with literacy difficulties is falling behind in history. Which application of technology is most appropriate?`,options:[`Removing history from their timetable because technology cannot help SEND`,`Asking them to film other pupils without consent as a motivational treat`,`Using an unfiltered open chat room so they can 'collaborate' with strangers`,`Text-to-speech and captioned sources so they can access the same curriculum content`],answer:3,explanation:`Accessibility for SEND is a named purpose and can be a reasonable adjustment. Removal, consent-free filming and unfiltered chat fail curriculum, safeguarding and appropriateness.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-7-q3`,topicId:`2-7`,stem:`Why does Keeping Children Safe in Education matter when settings use technology for communication and collaboration?`,options:[`It requires schools to ignore all online activity as a private matter.`,`It is an Ofqual exam-board specification for GCSE Computing only.`,`It places duties on schools and colleges to manage online safety, including filtering and monitoring, so collaboration is supervised and age-appropriate.`,`It bans digital literacy teaching in primary schools.`],answer:2,explanation:`KCSIE makes online safety a safeguarding duty, including filtering and monitoring. It does not tell schools to ignore the internet, nor does it ban digital literacy.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-7-o1`,topicId:`2-7`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two reasons why up-to-date and appropriate technology is important in supporting children and young people's education.`,modelAnswer:`Technology supports accurate monitoring and sharing of information: a current, moderated tracker or EYFS journal lets the teacher, TA and family see the same picture of a child's phonics or communication and adapt teaching quickly, which would be slower with scattered paper notes. It also supports accessibility for SEND and digital literacy for the future. Text-to-speech, captions or switch access can be a reasonable adjustment under the Equality Act 2010 so a pupil can meet the same National Curriculum goals, while taught use of safe, current tools prepares young people for later study and work. Both reasons depend on the technology being appropriate: filtered, age-matched and used with a clear learning purpose, not as an unsupervised reward.`,markScheme:[`Identifies a valid reason from the specification (monitoring, sharing, media, planning, SEND, safety, careers, digital literacy) (1)`,`Explains impact on education or safety (1)`,`Identifies a second distinct reason (1)`,`Explains the second with an example or named duty (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-8`,code:`2.8`,elementId:`e2`,title:`How metacognition helps children and young people learn`,page:45,notes:{gist:`Metacognition is thinking about one's own thinking: planning how to learn, monitoring understanding, and reflecting afterwards. Practitioners support it by helping children identify and minimise barriers, notice strengths and gaps, adapt strategies, learn from mistakes and transfer skills. It is not an extra worksheet called 'metacognition'; it is built into how tasks are introduced, checked and reviewed from early years talk through to KS4 revision.`,mustKnow:[`Metacognition has three specification roles: supporting preparation and planning of learning; monitoring own understanding; supporting a reflective approach.`,`Preparation and planning includes identifying and minimising one's own barriers (noise, missing vocabulary, an unclear goal, a rushed start).`,`Monitoring own understanding means knowing strengths and areas for development during the task and adapting strategies when something is not working.`,`A reflective approach includes learning from mistakes and recognising transferable skills that can be used in a new subject or setting.`,`Self-regulated learners plan, monitor and evaluate; adults model that cycle aloud ('I don't understand this yet, so I will reread and try a diagram').`,`Age and stage change the tools: a preschool child uses a plan-do-review conversation; a Year 6 pupil uses success criteria; a Year 11 student uses exam wrappers and planned retrieval.`,`Barriers can be cognitive (working memory), linguistic (EAL), emotional (fear of mistakes) or environmental (an inaccessible resource); metacognition includes naming them.`],sections:[{heading:`Preparation and planning: identifying and minimising barriers`,body:[`Before a task, metacognitive learners ask what the goal is, what they already know, what they will need and what might get in the way. Practitioners make that visible: a nursery plan-do-review board, a KS1 'what I need' tray, a KS3 checklist of equipment and key words, a KS4 revision planner. Identifying a barrier — 'I lose my place when the text is long' — is the first step to minimising it with a ruler, a glossary or a quieter table.`,`Adults minimise barriers that children cannot yet remove themselves: previewing vocabulary for a child with EAL, breaking a multi-step instruction for a child with working-memory needs, or agreeing a help signal so a withdrawn pupil does not sit stuck. That is still metacognitive support, because it teaches the child what a barrier looks like and what a strategy feels like.`,`Without planning, children dive in, fail and conclude they are 'bad at maths'. The educational role of this first stage is to put the challenge inside the ZPD with a strategy attached, not to remove all difficulty.`]},{heading:`Monitoring understanding and adapting strategies`,body:[`During learning, children need ways to check whether they understand: traffic lights, a worked example to compare, explaining a method to a talk partner, or pausing a video to predict the next step. Strengths should be named as precisely as gaps ('you always check your units') so that self-concept is accurate, not globally 'I'm clever' or 'I'm useless'.`,`Adapting a strategy is the point. If underlining every word is not helping comprehension, the pupil tries a gist sentence; if a toddler's block tower keeps falling, the adult comments 'you could try a wider base' and the child monitors the new attempt. A TA who always jumps in with the answer blocks monitoring, because the child never has to notice they are stuck.`,`Effective feedback (2.4) feeds metacognition only if pupils are then expected to act. 'See me' marking with no time to respond does not teach monitoring. Exam wrappers in KS4 — a short reflection after a mock on what was revised, what was guessed, what to change — are a secondary application of the same idea.`]},{heading:`Reflection, mistakes and transferable skills`,body:[`After the task, a reflective approach asks what worked, what did not, and what will be reused. Learning from mistakes requires a climate in which error is information (humanist safety plus a growth stance), not humiliation. A Reception adult who reviews the painting with the child ('the water went muddy when all the colours mixed — next time we could rinse') is teaching reflection, not delivering an art lecture.`,`Transferable skills are the educational prize: a planning template used in a science write-up that later helps a history enquiry; oracy stems that move from the talk-partner rug to a KS4 presentation; a nursery child's plan-do-review habit that becomes project management. Practitioners should name the transfer ('this is the same checking you did in maths') or children will treat each lesson as a one-off.`,`EEF guidance on metacognition and self-regulated learning is useful context: effects are high when teachers explicitly model, when pupils have time, and when tasks are suitably challenging. It is not a bolt-on 'learning to learn' carousel with no subject content. In the exam, keep the three stages — plan, monitor, reflect — and apply them to the child in the stem.`]}],theorists:[{name:`John Flavell`,idea:`Introduced metacognition as knowledge about and regulation of one's own cognitive processes.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Private speech and scaffolding in the ZPD help children take over the planning and monitoring that adults first model.`},{name:`Jerome Bruner`,idea:`Scaffolding is withdrawn as the learner internalises strategies — a mechanism for metacognitive independence.`}],examMoves:[`Structure 6-mark answers around plan / monitor / reflect, with a barrier or strategy in each.`,`Apply tools to the age: pegboard plans in nursery; success criteria in primary; exam wrappers and retrieval plans in KS4.`,`Do not confuse metacognition with 'being confident' — it is specific knowledge of how I learn this task.`,`Show the adult modelling aloud, then handing over, or you have described teaching but not metacognition.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-8-q1`,topicId:`2-8`,stem:`Which set of actions best describes metacognition?`,options:[`The teacher does all planning, never shows work, and hides mistakes.`,`The learner is helped to plan (including barriers), monitor understanding and adapt, then reflect on mistakes and transferable skills.`,`The learner copies from the board in silence for an hour.`,`The SENDCo completes every task for the child so that there are no errors.`],answer:1,explanation:`The specification names planning (with barriers), monitoring (strengths, gaps, adapting) and a reflective approach (mistakes, transfer). Doing the thinking for the child, or hiding error, blocks metacognition.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-8-q2`,topicId:`2-8`,stem:`A Year 6 pupil says, 'I always panic in word problems, so I will underline the question and draw a bar model before I calculate.' What is this an example of?`,options:[`Identifying a barrier at the planning stage and choosing a strategy to minimise it`,`Classical conditioning of a fear reflex that cannot be changed`,`Connectivism, because a bar model is a MOOC`,`The pre-production silent period of EAL`],answer:0,explanation:`The pupil has named a barrier (panic in word problems) and selected strategies before starting — preparation and planning of learning. It is not Pavlov, a MOOC or an EAL stage.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-8-q3`,topicId:`2-8`,stem:`Why is learning from mistakes part of a metacognitive approach?`,options:[`Because mistakes prove the child cannot be taught.`,`Because Ofqual awards extra GCSE marks for each mistake.`,`Because reflection on error helps the learner adapt strategies and transfer what they will do differently next time.`,`Because Piaget banned practice after error.`],answer:2,explanation:`A reflective approach uses mistakes as information for the next plan. They are not evidence of unteachability, nor a qualifications bonus.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-8-o1`,topicId:`2-8`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how practitioners can use metacognition to support children and young people's education.`,scenario:`A Year 5 class is starting a multi-step mathematics investigation. One pupil has EAL, one gives up at the first mistake, and several cannot say what they found difficult last week.`,modelAnswer:`Practitioners first support preparation and planning. They make the goal and success criteria visible, preview key vocabulary for the pupil with EAL so language is not a hidden barrier, and ask every child to name one thing that might get in the way (working memory, rushing, an unclear diagram) and one strategy to minimise it. During the task they teach monitoring: mid-task stops where pupils compare a step with a worked example, use a talk partner to explain their method, and adapt if they are stuck rather than sitting in panic. The child who gives up is coached to treat the first error as information ('which step failed? try a bar model') so that reflection is practised in the moment. After the investigation the class logs what worked, what did not, and which skill transfers to tomorrow's science write-up. That cycle — plan, monitor, reflect — is the role of metacognition: it makes learning visible, reduces helplessness and builds independence instead of adult prompt-dependence.`,markScheme:[`Refers to planning/preparation and identifying or minimising barriers (up to 2)`,`Refers to monitoring understanding, strengths/gaps or adapting strategies (up to 2)`,`Refers to reflection, learning from mistakes or transferable skills (1)`,`Applies to the scenario / shows impact on education (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-9`,code:`2.9`,elementId:`e2`,title:`The five stages of learning an additional language`,page:46,notes:{gist:`Learners of English as an additional language typically move through five stages: pre-production, early production, speech emergence, intermediate fluency, and continued language development (advanced fluency). Progress is not strictly tied to age or to how talkative a child is in their home language. Social, everyday English (BICS) often appears years before the academic language of the curriculum (CALP), which is why a fluent playground speaker may still struggle in Year 5 science.`,mustKnow:[`Learners move through five stages, in this order: pre-production, early production, speech emergence, intermediate fluency, and advanced fluency (also called continued language development).`,`Pre-production (silent period): the child listens, watches, points, nods and may not speak in English for weeks or months; this is not a special educational need by itself.`,`Early production: one- or two-word answers, set phrases, telegraphic speech; high-frequency nouns and verbs; still limited comprehension of complex instructions.`,`Speech emergence: simple sentences, growing vocabulary, more questions, more errors as the child experiments; can join practical group work with support.`,`Intermediate fluency: longer, more complex sentences and improving comprehension, but gaps remain in specialist and abstract curriculum language.`,`Continued language development / advanced fluency: near-native fluency in many contexts; academic language, idiom, inference and subject-specific writing may still need teaching for five to seven years or more.`,`Cummins' distinction helps application: BICS (basic interpersonal communicative skills) can take around two years; CALP (cognitive academic language proficiency) much longer.`],sections:[{heading:`Pre-production and early production`,body:[`In pre-production the child is mapping the new language. They may follow a visual timetable, copy routines and use gesture, but say little or nothing in English. A newly arrived Reception child who plays silently beside peers, or a Year 7 pupil who tracks the practical in science without answering oral questions, may be in this stage. Pressure to 'just speak' raises anxiety and can prolong the silent period. Receptive language is still developing, so practitioners should continue to talk to the child, pair talk with visuals, and accept non-verbal responses as participation.`,`Early production brings single words and short chunks: 'toilet', 'my turn', 'I go now'. Errors are normal. Closed questions ('is it red or blue?'), choice boards and repeating classroom phrases help the child succeed. In a nursery, the key person labels actions during play; in a primary, the TA pre-teaches tomorrow's five words. Do not mistake limited English for limited cognition — the child may already read in another script or reason well in their home language.`,`Timeframes overlap. Some children speak earlier because they are confident or have had English on screens; others stay longer in pre-production after a stressful move. Stage, not months since arrival, should guide expectation.`]},{heading:`Speech emergence and intermediate fluency`,body:[`Speech emergence is a leap in output: simple sentences, more vocabulary, stories about the here-and-now, and a willingness to join group talk. Grammar and pronunciation errors increase because the child is experimenting, which is progress, not failure. A Year 3 pupil at this stage can retell a practical activity with stem sentences but will still miss implied meaning in a story and will need wait time.`,`Intermediate fluency sounds 'fine' in the corridor. The child chats, jokes and follows most social talk (BICS), so adults may withdraw support too soon. Academic tasks — explaining a fair test, writing a balanced argument, understanding a history source — still overload working memory because CALP is thinner. This is a high-risk stage for misidentification: either assuming no need, or assuming SEND, when the need is language.`,`Support at these stages is about stretching language, not replacing thought: visual scaffolds for the curriculum, talk before writing, and questions that move from 'what' to 'why' once the child has the vocabulary to answer.`]},{heading:`Continued language development and advanced fluency`,body:[`Advanced fluency means the young person can operate in most everyday and many academic contexts, including abstract discussion. Even then, idiom, cultural references, low-frequency academic vocabulary and high-stakes writing (GCSE English language, extended history essays) can expose remaining gaps. Continued language development is therefore a KS3–4 issue, not only a new-arrivals issue.`,`A Year 10 student who arrived in Year 5 may sound native in tutor time and still need explicit teaching of subject command words, nominalisation and essay structure. That is the fifth stage, not a failure of the first four. Chomsky's LAD supports the idea that the capacity is there; the school must still provide the English input and the academic genres.`,`Assessment should use the five stages (or a framework such as the Bell Foundation's) alongside curriculum assessment, so that a science grade is not read as a proxy for language stage. Exam answers should characterise the stage, give a typical behaviour, and say what the practitioner should not assume.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Jim Cummins`,idea:`BICS versus CALP — conversational fluency appears earlier than the academic language needed for the curriculum.`},{name:`Stephen Krashen`,idea:`Comprehensible input and the affective filter — anxiety in the silent period reduces how much language gets in.`},{name:`Noam Chomsky`,idea:`An innate language acquisition device means additional languages can be acquired given rich input, not only drill.`}],examMoves:[`Name the stage in the specification's words, then give two characteristics (what the child can do / cannot yet do).`,`Warn against treating the silent period as SEND or as refusal, and against withdrawing support at intermediate fluency.`,`Match the example to age: gesture and key person in nursery; curriculum language and GCSEs at stages four and five.`,`If the question is only 2.9, do not write the whole of 2.12 strategies unless asked — characteristics first.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-9-q1`,topicId:`2-9`,stem:`A newly arrived Year 7 pupil watches practicals, follows a visual timetable and does not yet speak in English. Which stage is this most likely to be?`,options:[`Intermediate fluency`,`Continued language development / advanced fluency`,`Formal operational stage in Piaget's theory`,`Pre-production`],answer:3,explanation:`Watching, using visuals and little or no spoken English characterise pre-production (the silent period). Intermediate and advanced fluency involve substantial speech. Piaget's stage is a cognitive theory, not an EAL stage.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-9-q2`,topicId:`2-9`,stem:`Which characteristic best matches early production?`,options:[`Extended academic essays with near-native idiom`,`One- or two-word answers and set phrases, with limited comprehension of complex instructions`,`No listening at all, because the silent period means the child cannot hear`,`Complete mastery of CALP in every subject`],answer:1,explanation:`Early production is telegraphic: short answers and chunks. Essays and CALP mastery belong much later. The silent period is about output, not deafness.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-9-q3`,topicId:`2-9`,stem:`Why might a child at intermediate fluency still underachieve in KS2 tests?`,options:[`Because BICS (playground talk) often develops before CALP (academic language), so they sound fluent but lack subject language.`,`Because the five stages stop at Reception.`,`Because Ofqual forbids EAL pupils from sitting tests.`,`Because intermediate fluency means they have no home language.`],answer:0,explanation:`Social fluency can mask academic-language gaps — the classic BICS/CALP trap. The stages continue through school; EAL pupils can sit tests; home language remains an asset.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`2-9-o1`,topicId:`2-9`,marks:4,command:`Outline`,stem:`Choose two of the five stages of learning an additional language and describe what a learner typically does at each.`,modelAnswer:`In pre-production the learner is typically in a silent period: they listen, watch, use gesture and may not speak in English, but they are still mapping the language and should not be treated as having SEND on that basis alone. In early production they begin to use one- or two-word answers and set phrases, with limited understanding of complex instructions, so closed choices and visuals help them succeed. By contrast, intermediate fluency often includes confident social talk (BICS) while gaps remain in academic language (CALP), so a child may sound fluent in the playground yet struggle with a science explanation. Naming what the child can and cannot yet do at each stage stops adults withdrawing support too soon or waiting passively for speech.`,markScheme:[`Names a valid stage (1)`,`Gives at least one accurate characteristic of that stage (1)`,`Names a second valid stage (1)`,`Gives at least one accurate characteristic of the second stage (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`2-10`,code:`2.10`,elementId:`e2`,title:`How home language shapes a child's learning and development`,page:46,notes:{gist:`Home language is an asset for education and development, not a problem to be removed. It supports overall language understanding, provides a family support network, shapes self-concept and identity, and underpins social interactions and relationships. Practice that bans or shames home language weakens those four supports and can slow, rather than speed, English.`,mustKnow:[`Home language feeds understanding of language overall: concepts, grammar and metalinguistic awareness transfer when the child is allowed to be bilingual, not forced to replace one language with another.`,`Cummins' common underlying proficiency idea: knowledge built in Language 1 can transfer into English once vocabulary is in place (additive bilingualism).`,`Home language sustains the support network: parents, grandparents and siblings can explain, comfort and teach in the language they hold most securely.`,`Self-concept and identity are bound up with the language of home, culture and community; honouring it supports belonging and confidence to take learning risks.`,`Social interactions and relationships at home — stories, jokes, faith, discipline — happen in home language; cutting it off isolates the child from that developmental resource.`,`A deficit view ('don't speak that here') is linked to poorer wellbeing and can delay academic English because anxiety raises the affective filter.`,`Practitioners should find out which languages are spoken, by whom, and to what literacy level, including children who speak a dialect or who have interrupted schooling.`],sections:[{heading:`Understanding of language overall`,body:[`A child who already names colours, retells events and argues in Somali, Polish or Urdu has the concepts. English is a new code for knowledge they largely hold. If the nursery only values English, those concepts sit unused and the child looks delayed. If the adult accepts a home-language word, a bilingual book or a parent-recorded story, understanding of language as a system grows — the child notices that two words can mean one thing, which is metalinguistic awareness linked to later reading.`,`Chomsky's LAD is not English-specific: feeding home language keeps the device working. Vygotsky's view adds that higher thinking is mediated by the language the child actually has; denying that language during a tricky task removes the tool they need to reason. Additive bilingualism (keep home language, add English) outperforms subtractive bilingualism (replace home language) on both linguistic and cognitive measures in the long run.`,`There is a practical limit: the curriculum in an English school is delivered in English, so English must be taught explicitly (2.12). The point of 2.10 is that home language is the foundation that teaching builds on, not a rival to be extinguished.`]},{heading:`Support network, self-concept and identity`,body:[`Families are more able to support homework, behaviour and emotional regulation in the language they think in. A Year 4 maths method explained by a parent in Portuguese still builds the schema; a parent told 'only English at home' may withdraw from partnership because they cannot help. The support network also includes siblings, supplementary schools and community groups that keep literacy in the home language alive.`,`Identity is at stake. A child's name, stories, faith vocabulary and humour live in home language. When a setting mispronounces names, forbids home-language chat at lunch, or treats the language as a behaviour problem, the child learns that a core part of self is unwelcome. Self-concept then tilts towards shame or towards opposition, both of which harm engagement with education.`,`Positive practice is simple and visible: dual-language displays that are actually used, staff who learn key phrases, inviting a carer to share a rhyme, and high expectations in English without requiring the family to abandon their language. The Equality Act 2010 and the EYFS partnership duty both sit behind this: language is part of ethnicity, culture and, often, religion.`]},{heading:`Social interactions and relationships`,body:[`At home, attachment, play and discipline are negotiated in home language. A toddler's first emotion words may not be English; a key person who ignores that cannot co-regulate as well as one who asks the family for the words the child uses for comfort. Sibling talk in home language is a developmental engine — narrative, negotiation, humour — that English-only rules in the playground can interrupt if staff over-police it.`,`Peer relationships in the setting cut both ways. Home-language buddies can settle a new arrival (a 2.12 strategy) and protect identity; they can also, if overused without English models, reduce exposure. The professional judgement is to value the relationship and still plan English-rich interactions, not to split friends as a default.`,`For looked-after children or children whose home language is not the carers' language, the picture is more complex: the 'home' language may be in transition. Practitioners still ask, record and plan, because relationships and identity do not pause for English lessons. Exam answers should take the four spec headings — overall language, support network, identity, social relationships — and show a consequence of supporting versus suppressing home language.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Jim Cummins`,idea:`Common underlying proficiency — conceptual knowledge transfers across languages in additive bilingualism.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Language is a cultural tool for thought; removing the child's strongest language from a hard task removes the scaffold for thinking.`},{name:`Noam Chomsky`,idea:`The language acquisition device is not specific to English, so home-language input continues to feed language development.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Protects race (including nationality and ethnic origin); language and cultural identity are part of not discriminating.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,year:`2024`,point:`Requires partnership with parents and an understanding of each child's background, including language.`}],examMoves:[`Use the four spec effects as a ready-made 6-mark structure: overall language, support network, identity, social relationships — pick two or three and apply.`,`Write additive, not subtractive: home language plus English, never 'ban it so they learn faster'.`,`Apply to the family in the scenario: who can help with homework, which language comforts the toddler, what peers share.`,`Link forward to 2.11 needs and 2.12 strategies only if the command invites it; this point is the effect of home language itself.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-10-q1`,topicId:`2-10`,stem:`Which statement best describes how home language affects understanding of language overall?`,options:[`Concepts and metalinguistic awareness developed in the home language can transfer into English if the child is allowed to keep both (additive bilingualism).`,`Home language must be stopped immediately or English cannot develop.`,`Only children who are monolingual in English can form schemas.`,`Home language affects identity but has no link to cognition or language development.`],answer:0,explanation:`Additive bilingualism and transfer of concepts are the educationally accurate view. Banning home language is a deficit approach and is not supported by the specification's emphasis on overall language understanding.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-10-q2`,topicId:`2-10`,stem:`A Reception teacher tells a parent, 'Please speak only English at home so she will catch up.' Why is this poor practice?`,options:[`Because the EYFS forbids any contact with parents.`,`Because children are legally required to be taught only by grandparents.`,`Because Ofqual marks down settings that communicate with families.`,`Because it weakens the family's support network, can harm the child's identity, and removes the language in which the parent can best explain and comfort.`],answer:3,explanation:`Parents support learning and wellbeing most securely in the language they hold. The instruction also risks shame about identity. The EYFS actually requires partnership, not silence.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-10-q3`,topicId:`2-10`,stem:`How can home language support social interactions and relationships?`,options:[`It has no social function once the child starts school.`,`It always prevents the child from ever making English-speaking friends.`,`It is the medium of family stories, play, humour and comfort, and it can help a new arrival form relationships with peers who share it.`,`It is relevant only in KS4 literature lessons.`],answer:2,explanation:`Home language carries attachment and peer belonging. It does not automatically block English friendships; adults can value it and still plan English-rich mix of peers.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-10-o1`,topicId:`2-10`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a child's home language can affect their education and development.`,scenario:`Amira is four. She speaks Arabic fluently with her grandparents, who collect her from preschool. She is in the early production stage of English. A practitioner has asked the family to stop using Arabic so that her English will 'hurry up'.`,modelAnswer:`Amira's Arabic is the language in which she already understands stories, rules and feelings, so it supports her overall language development; concepts she holds in Arabic can transfer into English (additive bilingualism) once the preschool offers the English labels, rather than being lost if Arabic is silenced. Her grandparents are her support network: they can talk through the day, share books and co-regulate in Arabic. If they are told to stop, they may withdraw from partnership and Amira loses the adults who currently help her most. Home language also feeds self-concept and identity — Arabic is part of family, possibly faith, and her name and stories; shaming it risks a sense that she herself is a problem, which raises anxiety and can slow English by raising the affective filter. Socially, Arabic sustains her relationship with the people who collect her and may help her bond with peers who share it, which is a base for belonging from which she can risk new English talk. The practitioner's request is therefore likely to harm, not hurry, both development and education.`,markScheme:[`Explains effect on overall language understanding / transfer / additive bilingualism (up to 2)`,`Explains effect on family support network (1)`,`Explains effect on self-concept/identity (1)`,`Explains effect on social interactions/relationships and applies to the scenario (up to 2)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-11`,code:`2.11`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Communication, social and emotional needs of learners with English as an additional language (EAL)`,page:46,notes:{gist:`EAL is not a special educational need, but it does create communication, social and emotional needs that settings must plan for. Learners may need more support with written than spoken English, help to understand the curriculum and to access English-language resources, time to respond, a setting that understands culture, language, ethnicity and religion, and support from peers and family. Unmet, those needs look like low ability, poor behaviour or isolation.`,mustKnow:[`Support for written rather than spoken English: writing (especially academic writing) usually lags behind playground talk; spelling, grammar and essay structure need explicit teaching.`,`Understanding the curriculum: command words, abstract concepts and culturally assumed knowledge (for example 'Victorian Christmas') can block subjects that are not 'English'.`,`Accessing resources in English: worksheets, textbooks, websites, exam papers and classroom displays may be unreadable without visuals, pre-teaching or translation of key items.`,`Time for responding to questions: processing two languages takes working memory; wait time, and not jumping in, is a communication need.`,`Wider understanding of culture, language, ethnicity and religion among educators and peers reduces misreading of behaviour and supports belonging.`,`Support from peers and family protects wellbeing and provides models and advocates; without it, EAL learners are at higher risk of social isolation and low self-concept.`,`Needs change by stage (2.9): pre-production is mostly receptive and emotional safety; intermediate fluency is often written CALP and exam language.`],sections:[{heading:`Communication needs: talk, writing, curriculum and resources`,body:[`Spoken English for social purposes often outruns written English. A Year 6 pupil who chats fluently may still under-write in a SATs reading paper because decoding, speed and inferred meaning in a dense English text are harder than conversation. Secondary coursework and GCSEs raise the bar again: nominalisation, passive voice and subject-specific vocabulary are communication needs, not proof that the student 'can't think'. Support therefore targets writing and academic talk, not only oracy games.`,`Understanding the curriculum is a communication need across every subject. Science investigations, maths word problems and history sources are delivered in English; if the learner cannot access the language of the question, the teacher never sees the knowledge. Resources — textbooks written for monolingual peers, websites, revision guides — may assume idioms and cultural references. Without visuals, glossaries, dual-language key-word banks or a reader in exams, the resource itself becomes the barrier.`,`Time to respond is frequently missed. Teachers who ask a question and take the first fluent hand in two seconds systematically exclude EAL learners, who may still be translating. Wait time, think-pair-share, mini whiteboards and accepting a first answer in home language (then recasting into English) meet the need without lowering the cognitive demand.`]},{heading:`Social and emotional needs, culture and belonging`,body:[`Arriving in a new language is socially exposing. Children in pre-production cannot easily join jokes or defend themselves in the playground; they may shadow one adult or one peer, or be misread as withdrawn, rude or 'autistic' when the need is language and belonging. Explicit peer support, a buddy who shares a language where possible, and staff who watch unstructured times are part of meeting social need.`,`Emotional needs include frustration, fatigue (listening in a second language is tiring), loss of the competent self they had in their previous school, and sometimes trauma or family separation connected to migration. Nurture, a key person or tutor, and the chance to express feelings (2.4) matter as much as a vocabulary list. Mental health first aiders and pastoral staff should know that behaviour changes may be language exhaustion, not defiance.`,`Culture, ethnicity, language and religion must be understood by educators and peers. A child fasting, using a different script, avoiding certain foods, or standing closer or further away when they talk is not a behaviour target. Mispronouncing names, ignoring festivals, or treating home language as a problem (2.10) creates an emotional need the setting itself has caused. The Equality Act 2010 requires more than a multicultural display: it requires informed, respectful practice so that identity is not the price of learning English.`]},{heading:`Peers, family and getting the need right`,body:[`Peer support gives models of classroom English and a social way in; family support gives the conceptual and emotional base in home language. Both need the setting to communicate accessibly — translated essentials, interpreters for high-stakes meetings, not a child used as the interpreter for a safeguarding conversation. When family partnership is weak, the child's needs widen because nobody is bridging the two worlds.`,`Do not collapse EAL into SEND. Some children have both; most do not. The SENDCo and the EAL lead (or specialist, 2.12) should not be treated as the same person doing the same job. A Year 9 student with strong first-language literacy who is quiet in English has a communication and social need, not automatically a cognition need.`,`Exam answers should name a communication need and a social or emotional need, apply the stage and the subject, and say what happens if the need is ignored (under-achievement, isolation, misidentification). Keep strategies light here unless the question also pulls in 2.12.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Protects pupils from discrimination related to race and religion or belief; language and culture are part of that duty.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice`,year:`2015`,point:`EAL is not in itself SEND; lack of English should not be equated with a special educational need.`}],examMoves:[`Use the spec list as headings: written vs spoken, curriculum, resources, wait time, cultural understanding, peers and family.`,`Apply the language stage from 2.9 so you do not give a silent new arrival a Year 10 essay need, or a fluent speaker only a buddy.`,`State that EAL is not automatically SEND — a common AO3 discriminator.`,`Show the risk of unmet need: misidentification, isolation, or under-achievement in subjects other than English.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-11-q1`,topicId:`2-11`,stem:`Which statement about EAL and SEND is accurate?`,options:[`Every child with EAL must be placed on the SEND register.`,`The LADO assesses all EAL pupils for cognition.`,`EAL is not in itself a special educational need, though a child may have both EAL and SEND.`,`Once a child can speak in the playground they have no further communication needs.`],answer:2,explanation:`The SEND Code of Practice is clear that lack of English is not SEND. Playground fluency (BICS) does not end written and curriculum needs. The LADO is not an EAL assessor.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-11-q2`,topicId:`2-11`,stem:`A Year 5 teacher asks a question and takes an answer after one second. The EAL pupil's hand goes up late. What need is being missed?`,options:[`The need to be taught only in isolation from peers`,`The need to abandon home language immediately`,`A legal need to sit GCSEs in Year 5`,`Time for responding to questions, because processing in an additional language needs wait time`],answer:3,explanation:`Wait time is a named communication need. Isolation, banning home language and early GCSEs do not meet it.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-11-q3`,topicId:`2-11`,stem:`Why might an EAL learner need more support with written English than with spoken English?`,options:[`Because EAL pupils are not allowed to speak.`,`Because writing, especially academic writing, typically lags behind social talk and makes heavier demands on vocabulary, grammar and structure.`,`Because Ofsted inspects only handwriting.`,`Because spoken English never develops in additional-language learners.`],answer:1,explanation:`Written CALP lags BICS. EAL learners do speak, and inspection is not the reason writing is harder.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`2-11-o1`,topicId:`2-11`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain what communication, social and emotional support a learner with EAL might need.`,scenario:`Yusuf is in Year 8. He chats with friends at break but produces very little writing in history and rarely answers in class. He became quieter after a teacher mispronounced his name for several weeks and a peer mocked his packed lunch during Ramadan.`,modelAnswer:`Yusuf's communication needs are typical of a learner whose spoken social English is ahead of written and academic English. He needs support for writing rather than only for talk: history essays demand CALP, command words and culturally loaded sources, and he needs access to those resources through glossaries, visuals and modelled paragraphs, plus wait time so he can formulate an answer instead of losing the turn to faster speakers. Socially he needs peer support that is respectful, not mockery of food or faith, and adults who understand culture, ethnicity, language and religion so that Ramadan and his name are part of belonging. Emotionally, the mispronounced name and the lunch comment attack self-concept; he may now choose silence to stay safe, which will be misread as low ability if staff only notice the missing hands-up. Family support, used well, can keep identity strong in home language while the school teaches the written academic English he still needs. If those needs are ignored, under-achievement in a subject that is not 'English' becomes likely, and EAL may be mistaken for SEND.`,markScheme:[`Explains a communication need (writing vs talk, curriculum language, resources or wait time) (up to 2)`,`Explains a social need (peers, belonging, cultural understanding) (up to 2)`,`Explains an emotional/identity need (up to 1)`,`Applies to the scenario / notes risk of misidentification or under-achievement (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`2-12`,code:`2.12`,elementId:`e2`,title:`Strategies to support learners with EAL, and how to use them`,page:46,notes:{gist:`The specification names a clear toolkit: EAL specialist support; peer and group support; making the verbal curriculum more visual; talk before writing; drama and role play; scaffolding; language-rich environments; and bilingual resources. Strategies must match the child's stage (2.9), protect home language (2.10) and meet communication and belonging needs (2.11). A colourful display without planned talk is not a strategy.`,mustKnow:[`EAL specialist support: trained staff who assess language stage, advise teachers, and may teach targeted sessions without removing the child from a broad curriculum.`,`Peer and group support: buddies, mixed-ability talk groups, and collaborative tasks that give models of English and a social way in.`,`Making the verbal curriculum more visual: pictures, diagrams, demonstrations, graphic organisers, widget/symbols, subtitled clips — so meaning does not depend on a stream of talk.`,`Talk before writing: rehearse ideas orally (including in home language) so writing is not a double burden of composing and translating at once.`,`Drama and role play: embodied, visual, repetitive language in a meaningful context, from nursery home corner to KS3 freeze-frames.`,`Scaffolding: temporary supports (stem sentences, writing frames, worked examples, pre-taught vocabulary) withdrawn as independence grows (Bruner).`,`Language-rich environments: adult commentary, songs, books, dual-language print, and planned vocabulary across the day, not only in English lessons.`,`Bilingual resources: dual-language books, translated key words, bilingual dictionaries, parent-recorded stories, apps — used to access meaning, not as a way to avoid English.`],sections:[{heading:`People: specialists, peers and groups`,body:[`An EAL specialist (or a trained lead in a smaller school or nursery) assesses against a language-stage framework, helps the class teacher pitch work, and may run short focused groups on academic language. They are not a substitute SENDCo and they should not permanently withdraw the child from art, PE or science, because those subjects are where English is also learned. In a PVI nursery the 'specialist' may be the SENCO/EAL lead using LA guidance; the principle is expert advice plus class-based application.`,`Peer and group support is daily and free if planned. A buddy who shares a home language can settle a new arrival in pre-production; mixed groups later give English models so the child is not only hearing other beginners. Think-pair-share, numbered heads, and collaborative investigations force purposeful talk. Unplanned seating that isolates the EAL learner at the back, or that parks all new arrivals together all year, is the opposite strategy.`,`Family partnership turns 2.10 into a method: parents pre-teach a concept in home language, record a story, or come in to share a rhyme. Using a child as the interpreter for a difficult meeting is not an acceptable application.`]},{heading:`Making meaning visible: visuals, talk, drama and scaffolds`,body:[`A verbal curriculum (a teacher talking through a method, a podcast, a class discussion) excludes learners still building English. Making it visual means showing the science practical before describing it, using a story map, a substitution table, a labelled diagram, or a now-and-next board in early years. KS4 is not exempt: a GCSE geography lesson still needs maps, data and modelled paragraphs, not only a spoken lecture.`,`Talk before writing reduces cognitive load. In Reception that is 'say the sentence, count the words, then write'; in Year 9 it is structured discussion, oral rehearsal of a thesis, then the essay. Allowing a first draft idea in home language, or on a voice note, then shaping it into English, is talk before writing in a bilingual form. Drama and role play give the same oral rehearsal with movement and emotion — a supermarket role play for a new-to-English Reception child, a conscience alley in KS3 history — repeating target language in context.`,`Scaffolding is the temporary architecture: key-word banks, sentence stems ('I agree because…'), writing frames, worked examples, and pre-teaching the ten words that will unlock tomorrow's lesson. Bruner's idea is that the scaffold comes down. If a Year 6 pupil still cannot write a report without the same frame they used in Year 3, the strategy has become a crutch and needs re-planning, not abandoning of support altogether.`]},{heading:`Language-rich environments and bilingual resources`,body:[`A language-rich environment is adult behaviour as much as display. Commentary during play ('you're rolling the long, sticky dough'), songs, repeated book language, and staff who recast a child's telegraphic English into a full sentence without humiliation all grow input. Print should include the languages of the community, names spelled correctly, and vocabulary that matches this week's learning, not faded generic posters.`,`Bilingual resources give a bridge: a dual-language picture book in nursery, a translated knowledge organiser in KS2, a bilingual dictionary or approved app in KS4 science. They support access and honour identity. They fail if they are the only input and the child never hears modelled English, or if they are banned because 'this is England'. The application is both/and.`,`Match strategy to stage. Pre-production: visuals, buddy, language-rich commentary, no pressure to speak. Early production: stems, closed choices, drama. Speech emergence and intermediate: talk before writing, group roles, specialist teaching of CALP. Advanced fluency: subject-specific scaffolding for exams, not withdrawal from English models. Exam answers should name the strategy, apply it to the child, and state the impact on access, talk, writing or belonging.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Jerome Bruner`,idea:`Scaffolding — temporary support, including language frames and models, withdrawn as competence grows.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`ZPD and social interaction — peers, specialists and adults provide the language just above independent level.`},{name:`Jim Cummins`,idea:`BICS/CALP — strategies must go beyond playground buddies to visual and scaffolded academic language.`}],examMoves:[`Name strategies in the specification's wording — 'talk before writing', 'bilingual resources', 'language-rich environment' — then apply.`,`Match to stage: silent period gets visuals and a buddy; intermediate fluency gets academic scaffolds, not only a dual-language poster.`,`Do not recommend prolonged withdrawal from the curriculum or banning home language as a 'strategy'.`,`For Discuss or Analyse, weigh a limitation (for example over-scaffolding, or a buddy who becomes an interpreter) as well as the benefit.`]},quiz:[{id:`2-12-q1`,topicId:`2-12`,stem:`Which strategy is an example of making a verbal curriculum more visual?`,options:[`Using a labelled diagram, demonstration or graphic organiser alongside spoken explanation`,`Talking faster so that the lesson finishes on time`,`Removing all pictures so that pupils rely on English only`,`Sending the child out of science until their English is advanced`],answer:0,explanation:`Visuals carry meaning when talk is too dense. Faster talk, removing pictures and withdrawal all increase the barrier.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`2-12-q2`,topicId:`2-12`,stem:`A Reception child is in pre-production. Which combination of strategies is most appropriate first?`,options:[`Demand a written recount every day and withdraw them from play.`,`GCSE past papers to accelerate CALP.`,`A behaviour chart for not answering the register in full sentences.`,`A peer buddy, a language-rich environment with adult commentary, visuals and bilingual resources, without pressure to speak.`],answer:3,explanation:`Pre-production needs input, safety and visuals, not premature writing or sanctions for silence. Matching strategy to stage is the AO2 point.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`2-12-q3`,topicId:`2-12`,stem:`Why should scaffolding be withdrawn over time?`,options:[`Because EAL learners are not allowed any support after six weeks.`,`Because Bruner's scaffolding is temporary help towards independence; a permanent writing frame can become a crutch.`,`Because Ofqual inspects nurseries for leftover worksheets.`,`Because talk before writing is illegal in KS2.`],answer:1,explanation:`Scaffolds are meant to come down as competence grows. There is no six-week legal cut-off, and talk before writing is a recommended strategy, not a banned one.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`2-12-o1`,topicId:`2-12`,marks:6,command:`Discuss`,stem:`Discuss the types of strategy practitioners may use to support a child or young person with English as an additional language.`,scenario:`Lina is in Year 4. She is at speech emergence: she uses simple sentences in class, enjoys drama, and can talk about a practical, but her science write-ups are very short. She shares a home language with one classmate. There is an EAL lead in school one day a week.`,modelAnswer:`An EAL specialist can assess Lina's stage and help the class teacher target the academic language of science, rather than assuming playground talk is enough, but a one-day-a-week lead cannot be the only strategy or Lina will wait for Thursday to learn. Peer and group support — including her home-language classmate as a planned talk partner, not an all-day interpreter — gives models of English and a social way in, though she also needs fluent English models so the group does not cap her progress. Making the verbal curriculum visual (diagrams of the practical, a results table) plus talk before writing and drama will play to her speech-emergence strengths: she can role-play the investigation and orally rehearse sentences, then write. Scaffolding with stem sentences ('I found that… because…') should be ambitious and then withdrawn so the write-up grows, and bilingual key-word cards plus a language-rich classroom commentary will keep home language as a bridge while English CALP develops. Used together, the strategies meet communication and belonging needs; used as a display only, or as permanent withdrawal, they will not.`,markScheme:[`Names EAL specialist and/or peer and group support with an application or limitation (up to 2)`,`Names visual support, talk before writing and/or drama with application to the scenario (up to 2)`,`Names scaffolding, language-rich environment and/or bilingual resources (1)`,`Discusses match to stage, a limitation, or why strategies must work together (1)`],ao:`AO3`}]}],r=[{id:`3-1`,code:`3.1`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Why current laws and statutory guidance shape setting policies and procedures`,page:47,notes:{gist:`Statute creates the legal duty; statutory guidance tells settings how to meet it. Policies and procedures in early years and schools are the local translation of both. Exam answers must name the Act or guidance, the year, the duty it creates, and how that duty appears in everyday practice — a wet floor, a bruise, a photograph, an EHCP, a Prevent concern.`,mustKnow:[`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 (HASAWA): employers must protect employees and the public (including children and visitors); employees must protect themselves and each other, so far as reasonably practicable.`,`Statutory health and safety: Health and safety: responsibilities and duties for schools (2022) and the EYFS safeguarding and welfare requirements turn HASAWA into setting-level risk assessment, first aid, premises and supervision.`,`Counter-Terrorism and Security Act 2015: Prevent duty — specified authorities, including schools and registered early years providers, must have due regard to the need to prevent people being drawn into terrorism. Prevent duty guidance for England and Wales (2023) is the how-to.`,`Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR: personal data must be used fairly, lawfully and transparently; recording, sharing and storing (learning journals, photographs, medical and safeguarding files) must have a purpose and be secure. Safeguarding sharing is permitted where necessary to protect a child.`,`Children Act 2004: multi-agency working to keep children safe; local safeguarding children partnerships and a duty to cooperate. Safeguarding Vulnerable Groups Act 2006: suitability checks (DBS / barred lists) so adults who pose a risk cannot work with children.`,`Children and Families Act 2014 Part 3: SEN and Education, Health and Care Plans (EHCPs) 0–25. Female Genital Mutilation Act 2003: FGM is a criminal offence; regulated professionals have a mandatory reporting duty for known cases in under-18s.`,`Safeguarding statutory guidance that must shape policy: Working Together to Safeguard Children (2026); Keeping Children Safe in Education (2026); Multi-agency statutory guidance on FGM (2020); Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015).`,`Equality and diversity: Equality Act 2010 (protected characteristics, reasonable adjustments, Public Sector Equality Duty); SEND Code of Practice 0–25 (guides for early years and schools); UNCRC 1989 (rights of the child, including non-discrimination, voice and protection).`],sections:[{heading:`Health, safety, security and confidentiality`,body:[`HASAWA 1974 is not a poster. It requires competent people, risk assessment, first aid, safe premises and, where five or more people are employed, a written health and safety policy. Employees share the duty: they follow procedures, report hazards and do not put others at risk. The 2022 schools duties guidance and the EYFS welfare requirements make that concrete (ratios, medicines, outings, premises).`,`Security and confidentiality sit on two statutes. The Prevent duty (2015 Act; 2023 guidance) is met through British values, age-appropriate curriculum, a culture where concerning isolation or language is noticed, and referral via the DSL — not through surveillance of families. The DPA 2018 and UK GDPR govern how settings record, share and store personal data; named photographs on a public blog, unlocked safeguarding files and personal phones are common breaches.`]},{heading:`Safeguarding legislation and the guidance that operationalises it`,body:[`The Children Act 2004 puts cooperation on a statutory footing so education, health and social care cannot treat a child's safety as someone else's problem. Working Together (2026) is the multi-agency rulebook. It applies to all children, including those in kinship care, looked-after children and unborn children where there are concerns. Family Help (Working Together 2026) combines targeted early help with Children Act 1989 s.17 support so families get a seamless offer, a consistent practitioner relationship and a family help plan led by a multi-disciplinary team, rather than bouncing between 'early help' and 'child in need'. Multi-Agency Safeguarding Arrangements (MASA) are how local partners (local authority, health, police) are held to account. KCSIE (2026, in force 1 September 2026) is the schools and colleges rulebook: all staff must read Part 1 in full; DSL, safer recruitment, allegations, child-on-child abuse, online safety including AI-generated harm, and prejudice-based harm. Early years providers that are not schools follow the EYFS safeguarding and welfare requirements plus Working Together.`,`The Safeguarding Vulnerable Groups Act 2006 underpins DBS and barred-list checks. Children and Families Act 2014 Part 3 rebuilt SEND around a single EHCP. The FGM Act 2003, read with the 2020 multi-agency guidance, requires known under-18 cases to be reported to the police. Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) requires individual healthcare plans so asthma, diabetes and allergy are planned for, not left to luck.`]},{heading:`Equality law and the purpose of the whole framework`,body:[`The Equality Act 2010 makes discrimination, harassment and victimisation unlawful across nine protected characteristics and requires reasonable adjustments for disability. The SEND Code of Practice 0–25 tells early years and schools how to identify need, use a graduated approach and coproduce with families. The UNCRC 1989 is an international treaty, not a UK Act, but it underpins the child's right to protection, education and a voice, and examiners credit it when it is applied rather than name-dropped.`,`Purpose is consistent protection and accountability. Statute without guidance is a duty with no method; guidance without a policy is a document no one can follow on a Tuesday afternoon. Ofsted inspects whether the chain works: Act → statutory guidance → setting policy → staff procedure → what an adult actually does.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act`,year:`1974`,point:`Employers protect employees and the public; employees protect themselves and each other, so far as reasonably practicable.`},{name:`Health and safety: responsibilities and duties for schools`,year:`2022`,point:`Statutory guidance translating HASAWA into school-level duties, risk assessment and first aid.`},{name:`Counter-Terrorism and Security Act`,year:`2015`,point:`Prevent duty on specified authorities, including schools and registered early years providers.`},{name:`Prevent duty guidance for England and Wales`,year:`2023`,point:`How specified authorities implement Prevent, including referral routes.`},{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Fair, lawful, transparent use of personal data; UK GDPR governs recording, sharing and storing.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`2004`,point:`Multi-agency working and a duty to cooperate to keep children safe.`},{name:`Safeguarding Vulnerable Groups Act`,year:`2006`,point:`Suitability checks so adults who pose a risk are barred from working with children.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Part 3: SEN system and EHCPs 0–25.`},{name:`Female Genital Mutilation Act`,year:`2003`,point:`Criminalises FGM; mandatory reporting of known cases in under-18s.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Statutory multi-agency safeguarding guidance: Family Help, MASA, family group decision-making, anti-discriminatory practice.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Statutory safeguarding guidance for schools and colleges; all staff must read Part 1 in full.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Protected characteristics, reasonable adjustments and the Public Sector Equality Duty.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Child's rights to protection, education, non-discrimination and a voice.`}],examMoves:[`Always pair the Act or guidance with its year and one concrete duty — 'Children Act' or 'safeguarding policy' alone is not AO1.`,`Show the chain: statute → statutory guidance → setting policy → procedure → what you do in the scenario.`,`Match the law to the scenario: wet floor = HASAWA; bruise = Working Together / KCSIE / EYFS; photograph = DPA 2018 / UK GDPR; EHCP = Children and Families Act 2014.`,`Purpose answers need a because: the law exists to protect X, therefore the practitioner must Y.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-1-q1`,topicId:`3-1`,stem:`Under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, which statement is accurate?`,options:[`Only the employer has duties; employees have none once they have signed a contract`,`Employers must protect employees and the public, and employees must protect themselves and each other, so far as reasonably practicable`,`The Act applies only to factories, not to schools or nurseries`,`Health and safety is optional if a setting has fewer than 50 children`],answer:1,explanation:`HASAWA 1974 places duties on both employers (including protection of children and visitors as members of the public) and employees. It applies to educational settings.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-1-q2`,topicId:`3-1`,stem:`A Reception teacher wants to post named photographs of children on a public-facing class blog. Which legislation is most directly engaged?`,options:[`Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR`,`Female Genital Mutilation Act 2003`,`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974`,`Counter-Terrorism and Security Act 2015`],answer:0,explanation:`Names plus images are personal data. Fair, lawful, transparent use, purpose and security sit under DPA 2018 / UK GDPR. The other Acts are real but do not govern publication of photographs.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-1-q3`,topicId:`3-1`,stem:`Working Together to Safeguard Children (2026) is best described as:`,options:[`A non-statutory literacy scheme`,`A replacement for the Equality Act 2010`,`Guidance that applies only to children's homes, not to schools or early years`,`Statutory multi-agency guidance that settings must use to shape safeguarding policies and procedures`],answer:3,explanation:`Working Together is statutory multi-agency safeguarding guidance. It informs every setting's child protection policy; it does not replace equality law and it is not limited to residential care.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-1-q4`,topicId:`3-1`,stem:`In Working Together to Safeguard Children (2026), Family Help is best described as:`,options:[`A replacement for child protection s.47 enquiries`,`A school breakfast club with no multi-agency plan`,`Targeted early help combined with Children Act 1989 s.17 support, with a family help plan led by a multi-disciplinary team`,`A charity that only works with looked-after children after they leave care`],answer:2,explanation:`Family Help (Working Together 2026) creates a seamless offer by combining targeted early help with s.17 child-in-need support. A family help plan is led by a multi-disciplinary team so families are not bounced between two systems. It does not replace s.47 child protection where significant harm is suspected.`,ao:`AO1`}],open:[{id:`3-1-o1`,topicId:`3-1`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how current legislation and statutory guidance influence policies and procedures in an educational setting. Refer to two different areas (for example health and safety, safeguarding, data protection or equality).`,modelAnswer:`The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 requires employers to protect staff, children and visitors, and employees to protect themselves and each other. Statutory guidance such as Health and safety: responsibilities and duties for schools (2022) and the EYFS safeguarding and welfare requirements turn that duty into a health and safety policy, risk assessments, first aid procedures and reporting of accidents, so a wet floor is mopped, signed and recorded rather than left to chance. Separately, the Children Act 2004 requires multi-agency working to keep children safe. Working Together to Safeguard Children (2026) and Keeping Children Safe in Education (2026) then tell schools how to write a child protection policy, name a DSL, train staff and refer concerns the same day. In both areas the chain is the same: statute creates the duty, guidance specifies the method, and setting policy and procedure tell every adult what to do.`,markScheme:[`Names a relevant Act or guidance with year in area 1 (1)`,`Explains the duty / purpose (1)`,`Links it to a setting policy or procedure (1)`,`Names a distinct Act or guidance with year in area 2 (1)`,`Explains the second duty / purpose (1)`,`Links the second to a setting policy or procedure (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-2`,code:`3.2`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Why settings must follow their policies and procedures`,page:48,notes:{gist:`Policies say what the setting will do; procedures say how, who and by when. Following them is not bureaucracy: it preserves life, protects wellbeing, keeps adults inside their role, standardises action so children get the same safe response whoever is on duty, and creates the record the law and inspectors require. First aid and health and safety are the specification's two worked examples.`,mustKnow:[`First aid policies exist to preserve life, prevent the condition worsening and promote recovery — the three aims of first aid — and to standardise what every adult does in an emergency.`,`Following first aid procedures also ensures the setting complies with HASAWA 1974, the EYFS welfare requirements and the 2022 schools health and safety duties, and that accidents, injuries and treatment are recorded accurately (including RIDDOR where the incident is reportable).`,`Health and safety policies protect the wellbeing of children, staff and visitors by identifying hazards and controlling them before harm occurs.`,`They clarify role boundaries: who completes the daily premises check, who administers medicine, who leads an evacuation, and who a student or TA must tell rather than 'just dealing with it'.`,`They standardise actions and lines of reporting so a supply teacher or student follows the same steps as a long-serving practitioner.`,`Compliance and recording matter: if it is not written down, the setting cannot show that the duty was met, and patterns (a repeated hazard, a child who is always 'clumsy') are missed.`,`Not following policy is itself a safeguarding and professional issue: it can cause harm, invalidate insurance and become an allegation or disciplinary matter.`],sections:[{heading:`First aid: why the procedure, not improvisation`,body:[`Preserve life, prevent worsening, promote recovery only work if the adult who finds the collapsed child knows to shout for help, send for the trained first aider, not move a child with a suspected spinal injury, and stay until handover. A policy that names paediatric first aiders, the location of kits and the parent-contact and recording steps makes that possible at 7.45 a.m. as well as at 2 p.m.`,`Accurate reporting (accident book, body map if required, head-injury letter, RIDDOR for specified serious incidents) protects the child if symptoms appear later, and protects the setting if a parent or inspector asks what was done. Informal 'I told mum at the door' is not a record.`,`Following the first aid policy also keeps practitioners inside the law and their competence: they do not give unauthorised medication, they do not photograph injuries on a personal phone, and they call 999 when the procedure says the injury is beyond setting first aid.`]},{heading:`Health and safety: wellbeing, boundaries and the audit trail`,body:[`A health and safety policy that is followed means the climbing frame is checked, the bleach is locked, the allergy child is not given the wrong snack, and the fire drill is not skipped because 'we did one last term'. Wellbeing is the point: children cannot play or learn on an unsafe site.`,`Role boundaries stop a well-meaning student from taking a child to the car park to collect a forgotten bag, or a volunteer from administering an inhaler they have not been trained and authorised to give. Lines of reporting (site manager, manager, DSL, first aider) exist so concerns do not die in a staffroom comment.`,`Standardisation and recording mean every adult uses the same outing risk assessment, the same medicine form and the same accident log. That is how a setting complies with HASAWA 1974 and shows Ofsted that health and safety is effective, not merely written.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act`,year:`1974`,point:`Legal reason settings must have, and staff must follow, health and safety and first aid arrangements.`},{name:`Health and safety: responsibilities and duties for schools`,year:`2022`,point:`Sets out school duties for risk management, first aid and recording.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Safeguarding and welfare requirements include premises, medicines, accidents, staff:child ratios and suitable people.`}],examMoves:[`For first aid, use the three aims (preserve life, prevent worsening, promote recovery) then add standardise / comply / record.`,`For health and safety, use protect wellbeing, role boundaries, standardise, lines of reporting, comply, record — not just 'so nobody gets hurt'.`,`Apply: name the policy in the scenario (accident book, medicine form, fire procedure) and say what goes wrong if it is skipped.`,`Do not confuse a policy (the commitment) with a procedure (the numbered steps). Examiners credit both if you distinguish them.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-2-q1`,topicId:`3-2`,stem:`Which set of aims correctly describes the purpose of first aid in a setting's policy?`,options:[`Diagnose illness, prescribe medication and close the setting`,`Replace the need for a health and safety policy`,`Avoid contacting parents so as not to cause alarm`,`Preserve life, prevent the condition worsening and promote recovery`],answer:3,explanation:`The three aims of first aid are preserve life, prevent worsening and promote recovery. Settings do not diagnose or prescribe, and first aid does not replace the wider health and safety policy.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-2-q2`,topicId:`3-2`,stem:`A student on placement watches a child bump their head. The room leader says 'they'll be fine' and does not complete the accident record or send the head-injury letter. Why is this a problem?`,options:[`Because letters are only needed if a parent complains`,`Because students must always take the child to A&E themselves`,`Because following first aid procedure standardises action, complies with legal duties and creates an accurate record if symptoms appear later`,`Because Ofsted only inspects display boards, not records`],answer:2,explanation:`Skipping the procedure means the child may not be monitored, parents are not informed, and the setting cannot show compliance. Students should not freelance a hospital trip; they should follow the named first-aid procedure.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-2-q3`,topicId:`3-2`,stem:`Why do health and safety policies need clear role boundaries and lines of reporting?`,options:[`So every adult knows what they may do, what they must not do, and who to tell, which protects wellbeing and stops unsafe improvisation`,`So only the headteacher is allowed to notice a hazard`,`So students can ignore procedures until they are qualified`,`So settings can avoid HASAWA 1974 by blaming individuals`],answer:0,explanation:`Boundaries and reporting lines standardise safe action. They do not concentrate all duty in one person, and they do not cancel the employer's legal duty.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-2-o1`,topicId:`3-2`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two reasons why it is important to follow first aid or health and safety policies and procedures in an education or early years setting.`,modelAnswer:`First aid procedures preserve life, prevent injuries worsening and promote recovery by telling every adult the same steps to take, so a child with a head injury is assessed by a trained first aider, parents are informed and the accident is recorded. Health and safety procedures protect wellbeing by controlling hazards, clarify who may do what (for example who administers medicine), and create the records that show the setting has complied with the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974. In both cases, not following the procedure leaves the child unprotected and the setting unable to evidence that the legal duty was met.`,markScheme:[`Valid reason linked to first aid or H&S (preserve life / prevent worsening / wellbeing / standardise) (1)`,`Develops that reason with a setting example or legal link (1)`,`Second distinct reason (comply / record / role boundaries / reporting lines) (1)`,`Develops the second reason (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-3`,code:`3.3`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Managing and reporting risk to keep children safe and well`,page:48,notes:{gist:`Risk management is a cycle, not a one-off form. The five steps are: identify hazards; determine who is at risk; evaluate the risks and select mitigations; record the findings and implement them; monitor and review. In education and early years this covers premises, activities, outings, individual children (allergy, SEND, medical needs) and safeguarding risks, and findings must be reported through the setting's named lines.`,mustKnow:[`Step 1 — Identify hazards: anything with the potential to cause harm (wet floor, unsecured gate, unlabelled cleaning fluid, uncollected child, unsupervised one-to-one, online contact, a known medical need without a plan).`,`Step 2 — Determine who is at risk: children (including babies, children with SEND, visitors, pregnant staff, contractors). Risk is not the same for every person.`,`Step 3 — Evaluate risks and select mitigations: likelihood × severity; then control measures (remove, reduce, isolate, train, PPE, supervision, ratios, locked stores, visual signs).`,`Step 4 — Record findings and implement: written risk assessment, individual healthcare plan, outing form, or cause-for-concern form — then actually do the controls, not file them.`,`Step 5 — Monitor and review: after an incident, when a child or activity changes, at the date set on the form, and when guidance changes. A risk assessment from 2019 is not current.`,`Reporting risk is part of the duty: tell the named person (manager, site lead, DSL, first aider) in a timely way. Students and TAs do not 'leave it' because they are not in charge.`,`HASAWA 1974, the 2022 schools duties guidance, EYFS welfare requirements and Working Together / KCSIE all require this cycle. Dynamic (on-the-spot) risk assessment sits alongside written assessments — for example stopping an activity when a storm starts.`],sections:[{heading:`The five steps in an early years or school context`,body:[`Identify hazards by walking the environment and by knowing the children: a trailing cable, a broken fence, a child who bolts, a visitor without a DBS-supervised status, a social-media request from a parent. Hazards include safeguarding and wellbeing, not only slips and trips.`,`Who is at risk changes the control. A four-year-old near a pond, a child with a severe allergy at snack, a non-mobile baby on a play mat, a pregnant colleague lifting chairs — the same room holds different risks for different people.`,`Evaluate and mitigate using the hierarchy of control. Removing the hazard (not having a lit candle on a birthday cake in a toddler room) is stronger than a sign. Supervision and ratios are mitigations, not a substitute for a safe environment. Record the residual risk honestly.`]},{heading:`Record, implement, monitor — and report`,body:[`A risk assessment that is not implemented is theatre. The locked COSHH cupboard, the two-person outing, the individual healthcare plan for the child with asthma, and the visitor badge system are the assessment in action.`,`Monitor after near-misses as well as accidents. If three children slip in the same cloakroom, the control has failed. Review dates, new children, new equipment and new statutory guidance (a KCSIE update, a revised EYFS requirement) all trigger a fresh look.`,`Reporting: premises hazards go to the site/manager; child-level medical risk to the first aider and key person; safeguarding risk to the DSL the same day; immediate danger to the police on 999. Lines of reporting must be in the procedure so a student knows who to tell if the manager is off site.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act`,year:`1974`,point:`Requires risk to be assessed and controlled so far as reasonably practicable.`},{name:`Health and safety: responsibilities and duties for schools`,year:`2022`,point:`Expects written risk assessments, implementation and review.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Providers must take all necessary steps to keep children safe and well, including premises, outings and risk assessment.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Safeguarding risk is reported through local procedures, not handled as a private worry.`}],examMoves:[`Write the five steps in order. Examiners penalise 'do a risk assessment' with no steps.`,`Apply each step to the scenario's hazard (pond, outing, allergy, unlocked gate) rather than reciting a generic list.`,`Finish with who you would report to and when — manage and report are both in the heading.`,`Distinguish hazard (the thing) from risk (the chance and severity of harm to a named group).`]},quiz:[{id:`3-3-q1`,topicId:`3-3`,stem:`Which sequence correctly describes the steps required to manage risk?`,options:[`Call Ofsted, close the setting, write a policy, then identify hazards`,`Monitor first, then identify hazards only if someone is injured`,`Record findings, then decide later whether anyone was at risk`,`Identify hazards; determine who is at risk; evaluate risks and select mitigations; record findings and implement; monitor and review`],answer:3,explanation:`The five-step cycle starts with identifying hazards and ends with monitor and review. Recording without identifying who is at risk is the wrong order; waiting for injury is not prevention.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-3-q2`,topicId:`3-3`,stem:`A nursery plans a walk to the park. A two-year-old is a known bolter and a four-year-old has a severe nut allergy. After identifying the road and the packed-lunch hazards, what should staff do next?`,options:[`Cancel all outdoor play permanently`,`Take the group as usual because the park is familiar`,`Determine who is at risk (including those two children), evaluate the risks and select mitigations such as extra adults, reins or a buggy, a nut-free pack and a charged phone, then record and share the plan`,`Leave the two children at the setting without telling anyone, to keep the walk easy`],answer:2,explanation:`Step 2 and 3 personalise the assessment. Familiarity does not remove risk. Excluding children without a plan is poor inclusion and still needs recording and parental agreement.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-3-q3`,topicId:`3-3`,stem:`Why is monitoring and review essential after a risk assessment is written?`,options:[`Because a written assessment never needs to be used`,`Because children, activities, premises and statutory guidance change, and near-misses show when controls have failed`,`Because HASAWA 1974 bans written records`,`Because only Ofsted may change a risk assessment`],answer:1,explanation:`Review keeps controls current. Settings, not only inspectors, own the cycle; HASAWA requires control of risk, which includes records that are actually used.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-3-o1`,topicId:`3-3`,marks:6,command:`Outline`,stem:`Outline, step by step, how a setting should manage a risk and report it.`,modelAnswer:`First, identify hazards — anything with the potential to cause harm, such as a broken gate, an outing, or a child with a medical need. Second, determine who is at risk, including babies, children with SEND, staff and visitors, because risk is not the same for every person. Third, evaluate the likelihood and severity and select mitigations (remove the hazard, increase supervision, lock substances, write an individual healthcare plan). Fourth, record the findings on the setting's risk-assessment or concern form and implement the controls. Fifth, monitor and review after incidents, when children or activities change, and at the review date. Risk must also be reported through named lines: premises issues to the manager or site lead, medical issues to the first aider, safeguarding issues to the DSL the same day, and immediate danger to the police.`,markScheme:[`Identify hazards (1)`,`Determine who is at risk (1)`,`Evaluate risks and select mitigations (1)`,`Record findings and implement (1)`,`Monitor and review (1)`,`Report via named lines / timely reporting / 999 if immediate danger (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`3-4`,code:`3.4`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Children at risk and children in need, and the safeguarding principles behind practice`,page:49,notes:{gist:`A child at risk is at risk of harm, in immediate danger, or has already been harmed. A child in need is unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without local authority services. The six principles — empowerment, protection, prevention, proportionality, partnerships and accountability — stop practice becoming either delay or over-reaction. Practitioners record, tell the DSL, and do not decide the legal section themselves.`,mustKnow:[`At risk: the child is at risk of harm, is in immediate danger, or has already been harmed. This maps onto significant harm and, in law, a Children Act 1989 s.47 child protection enquiry. Parental consent is not required to start that enquiry.`,`In need: the child is unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without local authority services (Children Act 1989 s.17). Disabled children are included. Working Together 2026 places much of this support inside Family Help — targeted early help combined with s.17, a family help plan and a consistent practitioner — with partnership and consent as the default.`,`Empowerment: the child's voice and lived experience are heard; they are supported to make choices where it is safe. UNCRC Article 12 sits behind this.`,`Protection: support and representation for those in greatest need, including children who cannot protect themselves.`,`Prevention: it is better to take action before harm occurs — Family Help, not waiting for a crisis.`,`Proportionality: the least intrusive response appropriate to the risk; do not under-react to injuries or over-react to poverty alone.`,`Partnerships: local solutions through services working with the child, family and each other (Children Act 2004; Working Together 2026).`,`Accountability: everyone is accountable for their role; record, report, follow through. 'I thought someone else would do it' is not a defence.`],sections:[{heading:`At risk and in need are different thresholds`,body:[`In need looks like a family that cannot meet health, housing, disability or developmental needs without help: a disabled child who needs short breaks, a young carer falling behind, persistent hunger and missed sessions without a child protection injury. The response is assessment and services, usually with the family.`,`At risk looks like harm or likely harm: unexplained injuries, a disclosure, severe neglect, a baby in a household of escalating domestic abuse, a child who is frightened to go home. Immediate danger means 999. Same-day referral via the DSL is the education response; children's social care decide s.47.`,`A child can sit in both boxes: unmet need can become significant harm if help is refused or injuries accumulate. Serious case reviews repeatedly show children left in the 'in need' column while harm continues. Uncertainty is not a reason to delay a referral; reasonable cause to suspect is the test.`]},{heading:`How the six principles shape what you actually do`,body:[`Empowerment and partnership mean talking with the child and family where it is safe, using their language, and not doing to them. They do not mean asking a parent for permission before referring when a child may be at risk of significant harm.`,`Prevention and protection pull in different directions if you only remember one. Prevention is Family Help: a consistent practitioner, a family help plan, a Team Around the Family, a food-bank and health-visitor plan. Protection is the DSL, strategy discussion and, where needed, police. Proportionality is choosing the right one for the facts, not the one that feels least awkward.`,`Accountability is the paper trail and the follow-up: dated record, named DSL, what was said, what was agreed, whether social care called back. Working Together (2026) and KCSIE (2026) make that everyone's job, including students and TAs.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`s.17 child in need; s.47 duty to investigate significant harm; welfare paramount.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`2004`,point:`Duty to cooperate so in-need and at-risk work across agencies.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Family Help, assessment, child protection, MASA and information sharing.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Staff duties, DSL, referral and the child's welfare as paramount.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Article 12 (voice) and Article 19 (protection from violence, abuse and neglect).`}],examMoves:[`Open with the two definitions, then apply the scenario to a threshold. Examiners look for at risk versus in need, not a generic 'safeguard them'.`,`Name s.17 and s.47 if you can — they unlock AO1 — but still use the specification wording (harm / immediate danger / LA services).`,`Weave at least two principles through the action: e.g. prevention (Early Help) plus accountability (record and DSL).`,`Never decide the legal section yourself in the answer: record, DSL, social care decide.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-4-q1`,topicId:`3-4`,stem:`Which description matches a child in need?`,options:[`A child who has already been convicted of an offence`,`Only a child who is on a child protection plan`,`A child who is unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without local authority services`,`Any child who is slightly behind in phonics`],answer:2,explanation:`In need is a support threshold: without LA services the child is unlikely to reach a reasonable standard of health or development. It is not the same as a child protection plan or ordinary educational lag.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-4-q2`,topicId:`3-4`,stem:`A four-year-old is often hungry and misses sessions. This week she has a bruise on her upper arm, an implausible explanation, and she flinches from a male volunteer. How should the thresholds be read?`,options:[`Hunger and absence are in-need indicators, but the bruise, explanation and flinch push the case towards at risk of harm and need same-day DSL action, not a parenting workshop in three weeks`,`It is only in need, so staff should wait for parental consent before telling anyone`,`It is automatically a police matter that the teaching assistant should investigate at the child's home`,`Phonics scores will show whether safeguarding is required`],answer:0,explanation:`Both need and possible significant harm are present. Delay, home investigation and using attainment as a test are unsafe. The DSL refers; staff do not wait for consent where a child may be at risk.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-4-q3`,topicId:`3-4`,stem:`Proportionality in safeguarding means:`,options:[`Always the most dramatic intervention available`,`The least intrusive response that is still appropriate to the risk — Early Help for unmet need, child protection where harm is suspected`,`Doing nothing until you are certain, because certainty is the legal test`,`Treating every child in poverty as a s.47 case`],answer:1,explanation:`Proportionality matches intensity of help to intensity of risk. Certainty is not the test; reasonable cause to suspect is. Poverty is not, by itself, significant harm.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-4-o1`,topicId:`3-4`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the difference between a child being at risk and a child being in need, and how two key principles of safeguarding should underpin a practitioner's response.`,modelAnswer:`A child at risk is at risk of harm, in immediate danger, or has already been harmed, which in law sits with a Children Act 1989 s.47 enquiry into significant harm. A child in need is unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without local authority services (s.17), so the duty is support, often through Early Help, rather than a child protection investigation. Prevention means acting before harm escalates — offering Early Help for unmet need rather than waiting for a crisis. Protection and accountability mean that if indicators of harm appear (an injury, a disclosure, a child frightened to go home) the practitioner records facts, tells the DSL the same day and does not delay for a TAC meeting in three weeks. Partnerships still matter: work with the family where it is safe, but parental consent is not required to start a child protection referral. The practitioner does not decide the legal section; children's social care do.`,markScheme:[`Defines at risk (harm / immediate danger / has been harmed / s.47) (1)`,`Defines in need (unlikely to achieve reasonable health or development without LA services / s.17) (1)`,`Names a valid principle (1)`,`Applies that principle to practice (1)`,`Names a second distinct principle (1)`,`Applies the second principle, including DSL / record / do not delay (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-5`,code:`3.5`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Forms of abuse and their signs`,page:49,notes:{gist:`Abuse is grouped as physical, emotional, sexual and neglect, with domestic abuse recognised in its own right under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021 when a child sees, hears or experiences the effects. Signs are professional indicators, not proof. One mark is not a diagnosis; a cluster, a change from that child's baseline, an implausible explanation or a disclosure is a reason to record and tell the DSL the same day. Write signs in professional, non-graphic language.`,mustKnow:[`Physical abuse signs: repeated illnesses or investigations; bruising, bites, burns; non-accidental injury; fractures; reluctance to remove clothes; flinching or cowering.`,`Possible signs of emotional abuse: the child becomes withdrawn or seeks comfort a lot, has new toileting accidents, goes back to earlier stages of development, struggles to concentrate or make friends, or is overly affectionate with adults they do not know.`,`Sexual abuse signs: sexualised behaviour or language that is not age-appropriate; sexually transmitted infections; swelling, soreness or discharge (record and refer; never examine the child).`,`Neglect signs: failure to thrive; health problems that are not treated; persistent hunger or thirst; failure to attend; housing that is unsafe or unstable.`,`Domestic abuse (Domestic Abuse Act 2021): a child who sees, hears or experiences the effects is a victim in their own right. Signs can include aggression, antisocial behaviour, depression or anxiety.`,`Indicators are patterns. Change from that child's normal, clusters of signs, and an explanation that does not match the injury or the child's development (a non-mobile baby with a bruise) carry more weight than a single snapshot.`,`Bias: neglect is under-identified in affluent families; marks can be missed or over-read depending on skin tone; disability can hide or mimic signs. Uncertainty is not a reason to delay a referral.`],sections:[{heading:`The four categories plus domestic abuse`,body:[`Physical abuse is inflicted injury or illness. Notice location and pattern, repeated presentations, and behaviour around undressing or a particular adult. Flinching and cowering are as important as the mark itself.`,`Emotional abuse is persistent humiliation, rejection, intimidation or exposure to harm. The child may be withdrawn or 'very good', may regress, may struggle to concentrate or make friends, or may be indiscriminately affectionate because they have not learned a safe attachment pattern.`,`Sexual abuse may show as sexualised behaviour or language beyond the child's stage, or as physical signs such as soreness, swelling, discharge or an STI. The professional task is to record and refer, not to identify a perpetrator or to question the child repeatedly.`,`Neglect is omitted care over time: failure to thrive, untreated health needs, hunger, non-attendance, unsafe housing. Poverty is not automatically neglect, but poverty can coexist with neglect; offer support and still refer if care is persistently inadequate.`,`The Domestic Abuse Act 2021 recognised children as victims if they see, hear or experience the effects. Aggression, antisocial behaviour, anxiety or depression in the setting may be the visible edge of harm at home.`]},{heading:`From a sign to a professional action`,body:[`Record date, time, facts, exact words and who was present. Distinguish observation from opinion. Do not photograph injuries on a personal phone or examine a child.`,`Tell the DSL or deputy the same day. If the child is in immediate danger, call 999. Do not discuss in the staffroom or with the person who may be the source of harm.`,`Signs are not proof. Working Together (2026) and KCSIE (2026) still require same-day action on clusters, disclosures and injuries that do not match the explanation.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Significant harm includes ill-treatment and impairment of health or development.`},{name:`Domestic Abuse Act`,year:`2021`,point:`A child who sees, hears or experiences the effects of domestic abuse is a victim.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Definitions of abuse and neglect and the referral pathway.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Indicators, child-on-child abuse and staff duties to report.`}],examMoves:[`Name the type, give two specification signs, then the action (record, DSL, do not investigate, do not examine).`,`Never write graphic sexual or injury detail — professional language is what the mark scheme wants.`,`If the scenario is domestic abuse, name the 2021 Act and the child's status as a victim.`,`AO3: one sign is not proof, but waiting for certainty is not the legal test.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-5-q1`,topicId:`3-5`,stem:`Which cluster is the most typical professional indicator of neglect?`,options:[`A single missed PE lesson`,`A child who prefers drawing to football`,`A parent who works full time`,`Failure to thrive, untreated health problems, persistent hunger or thirst, poor attendance and housing issues`],answer:3,explanation:`Neglect is persistent omitted care across health, nutrition, attendance and living conditions. Employment or a missed lesson is not, by itself, neglect.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-5-q2`,topicId:`3-5`,stem:`A child who lives with domestic abuse has become aggressive in nursery and has started to wet after previously being dry. Under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021 this child is:`,options:[`Unaffected in law because they were not the adult who was hit`,`Automatically a perpetrator who must be excluded`,`Recognised as a victim if they see, hear or experience the effects, and the behaviour change is a possible sign that must be recorded and passed to the DSL`,`Only a concern if their phonics score falls`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2021 Act treats children who see, hear or experience the effects as victims. Aggression and regression are listed signs. Attainment is not the test; exclusion is not the first response.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-5-q3`,topicId:`3-5`,stem:`Sexualised language in a young child is best understood as:`,options:[`Proof of a named perpetrator's identity`,`A possible indicator of sexual abuse that must be recorded and passed to the DSL, not investigated or graphically described by the practitioner`,`Always typical curiosity that must be ignored`,`A reason to question the child repeatedly until they name someone`],answer:1,explanation:`It is an indicator, not a completed investigation. Repeated questioning and amateur detective work contaminate evidence and harm the child.`,ao:`AO2`}],open:[{id:`3-5-o1`,topicId:`3-5`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain three types of abuse and one associated sign of each that a practitioner in an education or early years setting should recognise.`,modelAnswer:`Physical abuse may show as bruising, bites, burns, fractures, flinching or a reluctance to remove clothes, especially if explanations do not match the injury. Emotional abuse may show as withdrawal, regression, poor concentration, difficulty making friends or indiscriminate affection. Neglect may show as failure to thrive, untreated health problems, hunger or thirst, missed sessions or housing issues. (Sexual abuse may show as sexualised behaviour or language that is not age-appropriate, or as soreness, discharge or an infection, which must be referred, not examined.) In every case the sign is an indicator, not proof: the practitioner records facts in professional language and tells the DSL the same day under Working Together (2026) and KCSIE (2026) or the EYFS.`,markScheme:[`Names type 1 + a valid associated sign (2)`,`Names type 2 + a valid associated sign (2)`,`Names type 3 + a valid associated sign (2)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`3-6`,code:`3.6`,elementId:`e3`,title:`How adults in trusted roles can manipulate and influence children`,page:50,notes:{gist:`A position of trust is any role that gives an adult power over a child — teacher, practitioner, coach, volunteer, student, family member. That power can be used to care, or to manipulate and influence so the child is harmed and kept silent. The specification routes are interfamilial abuse, inappropriate games, online materials and communication, making a child observe the sexual behaviour of others, and threats of harm to the child or their family. Professional boundaries exist because the child cannot freely refuse an adult they depend on.`,mustKnow:[`A position of trust is built on inequality: the adult grades, cares, feeds, transports, comforts and is believed. Children want to please that adult. That hold can be abused even when the adult 'means well'.`,`Interfamilial abuse: a parent, step-parent, sibling, cousin or other family member uses the trust of family life — overnight care, shared rooms, 'don't tell, you'll split the family' — to harm and silence the child.`,`Inappropriate games: tickling, play fighting, dares, 'secrets' games or sexualised play that the child cannot easily refuse because an adult or older person is running them.`,`Online materials and communication: sending or showing sexual or otherwise inappropriate content; private messaging, gaming chat, personal social media; moving a child from a public platform to a private one.`,`Observing sexual behaviour of others: exposing a child to sexual activity or images as a way of normalising harm, shocking them into silence, or preparing further abuse. Record and refer; do not describe graphic detail.`,`Threats of harm to the individual or family: 'if you tell, your mum will be sent away / your brother will get hurt / you will be taken into care'. Threats explain why children recant or appear loyal to the person harming them.`,`Students, volunteers and supply staff occupy a position of trust. 'I am only on placement' is not a defence. Breaches go to the DSL / head and, where it is a staff allegation, the LADO — they are not staffroom gossip.`],sections:[{heading:`How manipulation uses the role`,body:[`Interfamilial abuse is often hidden by loyalty and by the child's fear of breaking up the family. Settings see the effects (withdrawal, sexualised behaviour, fear of going home) rather than the act. Believe the possibility; do not assume 'nice families' do not harm.`,`Inappropriate games and 'jokes' test what a child will accept. An adult who needs a child to sit on their lap, who dares children to remove clothing, or who runs secrets as a game is using play as cover. Policy on touch, intimate care and open-door working exists for this reason.`,`Online, the same adult can extend the role beyond the building: a personal account, a late-night message, a link, a request for a picture. KCSIE (2026) treats this as a safeguarding and allegations issue, not a 'blurred boundary'.`]},{heading:`Silence, threats and the professional response`,body:[`Exposure to the sexual behaviour of others, and threats against the child or people they love, are methods of control. They create shame and fear, which is why a child may defend the adult or refuse to say more. That is not evidence that nothing happened.`,`The practitioner's job is not to prove who did what. Listen, record the child's words, do not promise secrecy, do not ask leading questions, tell the DSL the same day, and do not alert the alleged source of harm. If a colleague is the person of concern, use the allegations procedure and the LADO.`,`The Sexual Offences Act 2003 still matters in the wider legal picture: sexual activity with a 16- or 17-year-old by a person in a specified position of trust is an offence even though 16 is the age of consent. Professionally, the duty starts well before a criminal threshold.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Sexual Offences Act`,year:`2003`,point:`Position of trust offences; sexual activity with 16- and 17-year-olds in specified roles is an offence.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Staff behaviour, online safety, allegations and the expectation that all staff occupy a position of trust.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Intra-familial harm and the multi-agency response.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Welfare paramount; significant harm includes sexual and emotional abuse.`}],examMoves:[`Define position of trust as power and influence, then name two specification methods (interfamilial, games, online, observing sexual behaviour, threats).`,`Keep sexual content professional and non-graphic — the examiner wants the method of manipulation, not a description of acts.`,`Apply: if the scenario is a family member, do not only write about teachers; if it is a popular TA, still put the child first.`,`Finish with DSL / do not investigate / do not promise confidentiality.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-6-q1`,topicId:`3-6`,stem:`Which of these is an example of how an adult in a trusted role might manipulate a child?`,options:[`Using inappropriate games, private online communication, or threats of harm to the child or their family`,`Greeting every child at the door`,`Following the intimate-care policy with a second adult nearby`,`Recording a safeguarding concern and telling the DSL`],answer:0,explanation:`The specification lists inappropriate games, online materials/communication, exposure to sexual behaviour of others, interfamilial abuse and threats. Ordinary warm practice and correct reporting are the opposite.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-6-q2`,topicId:`3-6`,stem:`A child says their older cousin told them not to tell anyone 'or mum will get taken away', and has started to avoid family events. The practitioner should:`,options:[`Assume family disagreements are private and take no action`,`Record the child's words, recognise a possible threat used by someone in a position of family trust, and tell the DSL the same day without alerting the cousin`,`Visit the cousin to hear their side before involving the setting`,`Promise the child it will stay secret so they keep talking`],answer:1,explanation:`Threats of harm to family are a listed manipulation method in interfamilial abuse. Record, DSL, no investigation, no promise of secrecy.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-6-q3`,topicId:`3-6`,stem:`Why does 'I am only a placement student' not remove a position of trust?`,options:[`Because students are legally the same as headteachers in every respect`,`Because students are not allowed on placement`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 bans students from settings`,`Because children still experience the student as an adult with access, authority and influence in the setting`],answer:3,explanation:`The child's experience of adult power, not the student's job title, is what matters professionally. Students must keep the same boundaries.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-6-o1`,topicId:`3-6`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two ways in which a person in a position of trust can manipulate or influence a child or young person.`,modelAnswer:`A family member in a position of trust may use interfamilial abuse, for example by telling the child to keep secrets or by threatening that the family will be split up if they tell, which silences the child. An adult in a setting or online may use inappropriate games, private messages or online materials, or threats of harm to the child or their family, to test boundaries and keep control. In both cases the child cannot freely refuse someone they depend on, so the practitioner must record any concern and tell the DSL rather than investigate or treat it as a private matter.`,markScheme:[`First valid method (interfamilial / games / online / exposure to sexual behaviour / threats) (1)`,`Explains how that method manipulates or silences (1)`,`Second distinct method (1)`,`Explains the second method / links to power or professional action (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-7`,code:`3.7`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Grooming and exploitation: signs and possible outcomes`,page:50,notes:{gist:`Grooming is the process of building a relationship, trust and an emotional connection so a child can be manipulated, exploited and abused. Exploitation is the outcome: the child is used. Specification outcomes include taking advantage of the child, unauthorised access to private or sensitive information, manipulation, using trusted status to bully, shame or undermine, and threatening punishment for non-compliance. Signs include a change from typical behaviour, withdrawal or isolation, inability to concentrate, and sexualised behaviour, language or understanding that is not age-appropriate.`,mustKnow:[`Grooming is a process (target, build trust, isolate, secrets, test boundaries, exploit), not a single event. It can be sexual, criminal or ideological, online or in person, by adults or by other children.`,`Outcomes — taking advantage: using the child for sexual, criminal, domestic or online purposes, or for the adult's emotional needs, because the child cannot freely refuse.`,`Outcomes — unauthorised access to private or sensitive information: passwords, images, family details, safeguarding or medical information, which can then be used to coerce ('I will share that picture').`,`Outcomes — manipulation, and using trusted status to bully, shame or undermine: the same power that should protect is used to shame the child into compliance.`,`Outcomes — threatening punishment for non-compliance: sanctions, exposure, harm to family, or withdrawal of the 'special' relationship if the child does not do as they are told.`,`Signs: changes in typical behaviour; withdrawn or isolated; inability to concentrate; sexualised behaviour, language or understanding that is not age-appropriate. Also (applied): unexplained gifts, a new phone, missing episodes, defending an older 'friend'.`,`Children cannot consent to their own exploitation. 'They wanted to go' or 'they sent the picture' is the grooming talking, not a professional conclusion. Response: DSL the same day; do not confront the suspected groomer; record; follow KCSIE / local exploitation pathways.`],sections:[{heading:`Outcomes: what grooming is for`,body:[`Taking advantage and manipulation are the core: the relationship was never equal. Gifts, attention and secrets are the bait; the child is then used.`,`Unauthorised access to private or sensitive information is both a method and an outcome. Once a groomer has images, messages or family facts, shame and threats of exposure keep the child compliant. Settings that are sloppy with passwords, displays of named photographs or unlocked files make that easier.`,`Using a position of trust to bully, humiliate or undermine, and threatening punishment for non-compliance, show that exploitation is not only 'stranger danger'. A coach, tutor, older pupil or family friend can harm through the role itself. Victim-blaming repeats the groomer's logic.`]},{heading:`Signs and the professional response`,body:[`Change from that child's baseline is the most useful sign: a sociable child becomes withdrawn and isolated; concentration collapses; language or behaviour becomes sexualised in a way that does not match their stage. Clusters matter more than one off-day.`,`Online and extra-familial harm (criminal or sexual exploitation) may add unexplained money, phones, missing from education, or a new controlling contact. KCSIE (2026) requires schools to treat this as abuse.`,`Do not confront the suspected person, do not confiscate devices carelessly (evidence), do not promise secrecy, and do not delay because the child says they are 'fine'. Tell the DSL. Sexual Offences Act 2003 s.15 covers meeting a child following sexual grooming; the professional duty starts at the first indicator.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Sexual Offences Act`,year:`2003`,point:`Includes meeting a child following sexual grooming (s.15) and position of trust offences.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Child sexual and criminal exploitation, online safety and extra-familial harm.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Multi-agency response to exploitation and extra-familial harm.`}],examMoves:[`Define grooming as a process, then name two specification outcomes and two specification signs — do not only write 'being nice then abusing'.`,`Reject victim-blaming; name power and the child's lack of true consent.`,`Apply online and in-person; finish with DSL, no confrontation, record.`,`Keep sexualised-behaviour points professional and non-graphic.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-7-q1`,topicId:`3-7`,stem:`Which of these is a possible outcome of grooming and exploitation?`,options:[`Improved concentration and stronger friendships`,`Automatic exclusion of the child so the setting is not liable`,`A legal requirement to inform the suspected groomer first`,`Looking at private or sensitive information without permission, and using trusted status to bully, shame or undermine a child`],answer:3,explanation:`Outcomes include taking advantage, unauthorised access to information, manipulation, misuse of a position of trust to bully or humiliate, and threats for non-compliance. Confronting the groomer and punishing the child are wrong responses.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-7-q2`,topicId:`3-7`,stem:`A 13-year-old has become withdrawn, cannot concentrate, and is using sexualised language that is not age-appropriate. They defend a much older 'friend' they met on a game. The practitioner should:`,options:[`Treat it as typical teenage mood and wait until exams are over`,`Delete the child's accounts as a first step and tell the class`,`Recognise possible grooming and exploitation, record the signs, tell the DSL the same day and not confront the older person themselves`,`Congratulate them on making an adult friend`],answer:2,explanation:`Withdrawal, poor concentration and sexualised language are specification signs; an unexplained older online contact is a classic applied indicator. DSL and specialist pathways, not DIY confrontation or delay.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-7-q3`,topicId:`3-7`,stem:`A child says they 'wanted' to send a picture and take the gifts. Why is that not evidence that grooming has not occurred?`,options:[`Because apparent agreement is often the result of manipulation, isolation and threats; children cannot give informed consent to their own exploitation`,`Because children over 10 cannot be groomed`,`Because gifts always make a relationship lawful`,`Because online contact cannot be grooming if no meeting happened`],answer:0,explanation:`Apparent compliance is part of the mechanism. Grooming can remain online; there is no age-10 cut-off; gifts are often the bait.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-7-o1`,topicId:`3-7`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two potential outcomes or signs of grooming and exploitation that a practitioner should recognise.`,modelAnswer:`One outcome is that the groomer takes advantage of the child and may gain unauthorised access to private or sensitive information, such as images or family details, which can then be used to manipulate the child or threaten punishment if they do not comply. A sign practitioners should recognise is a change from typical behaviour: the child becomes withdrawn or isolated, cannot concentrate, or shows sexualised behaviour, language or understanding that is not age-appropriate. These indicators should be recorded and passed to the DSL the same day; staff should not confront the suspected person or blame the child.`,markScheme:[`Valid outcome or sign (1)`,`Develops it (process / impact / example) (1)`,`Second distinct outcome or sign (1)`,`Develops the second / links to professional action (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-8`,code:`3.8`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Warning signs of inappropriate relationships between adults in the setting and children`,page:50,notes:{gist:`Adults who harm in settings often test boundaries over time. The specification indicators are: being overly affectionate; gifts; favouritism; time alone with a child; making friends with parents and/or visiting at home; private texts or social media with the child. None of these is proof on its own; a pattern is a safeguarding concern. Staff report through the allegations procedure and the LADO — loyalty to a colleague is not a safeguarding value.`,mustKnow:[`Overly affectionate: touch, language or 'special hugs' that go beyond the setting's code of conduct and that the child cannot easily refuse.`,`Gifts: especially secret, costly or frequent gifts to one child that create obligation or a 'special' bond.`,`Favouritism: one child singled out for treats, jobs, praise, or exemption from rules.`,`Time alone with a child: always volunteering for one-to-one, intimate care, lifts, or finding reasons to be unsupervised; ignoring open-door, ratio or toilet policies.`,`Making friends with parents and/or visiting at home: blurring professional and personal life to gain access and reduce suspicion (grooming the family as well as the child).`,`Private texts or social media with the child: personal phones, apps, games or email that bypass the setting's accounts and logging.`,`KCSIE (2026) Part 4: allegations that a person has harmed a child, may pose a risk, or may have committed a criminal offence. Low-level concerns that are inconsistent with the code of conduct should still be recorded so patterns are visible. Report to the head/DSL; LADO coordinates; do not investigate a colleague yourself.`],sections:[{heading:`What 'inappropriate' looks like before a proven offence`,body:[`Abuse in organisations is rarely a single dramatic event. It is a trail of small permissions the adult has granted themselves: a gift, a closed door, a personal message, a lift, a home visit that policy does not allow.`,`A child who is singled out may appear special and may even like the adult. That does not make the relationship appropriate. Favouritism is a sign, not a compliment.`,`Befriending parents and visiting at home can look like 'going the extra mile'. Professionally it is a route to unsupervised access and to making later disclosures less believable. Contact with families should go through setting channels.`]},{heading:`Reporting without investigating`,body:[`Tell the headteacher or DSL the same day (or the chair of governors / LADO if the concern is about the head). Record facts — what was seen, when, who else was present. Do not warn the colleague, search their bag, or question the child repeatedly.`,`Whistleblowing policy protects staff who report in good faith. 'I might be wrong' is a reason to report, not a reason to stay silent. Students on placement have the same duty; placement status does not cancel KCSIE.`,`The LADO (local authority designated officer) coordinates allegations against people who work or volunteer with children. Ofsted and the DBS may become involved. That is the system working, not a betrayal of the staffroom.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Allegations, low-level concerns, staff behaviour and safer working practice.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`LADO role in allegations against people who work with children.`},{name:`Sexual Offences Act`,year:`2003`,point:`Position of trust offences where the relationship is sexual.`},{name:`Safeguarding Vulnerable Groups Act`,year:`2006`,point:`DBS / barred-list framework when an adult may pose a risk.`}],examMoves:[`List specification indicators (gifts, favouritism, time alone, home visits, private texts) — not 'they seemed odd'.`,`Name LADO and the rule: report, do not investigate a colleague.`,`Apply: if the scenario is a popular TA or a 'helpful' volunteer, still put the child first.`,`Mention low-level concerns: small boundary-testing should be written down so a pattern can be seen.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-8-q1`,topicId:`3-8`,stem:`Which of these is a warning sign that an adult in the setting may be forming an inappropriate relationship with a child?`,options:[`Following the intimate-care policy with a second adult in earshot`,`Gifts and favouritism, seeking time alone with one child, visiting the family at home, or private texts and social media with the child`,`Greeting every child at the door`,`Attending statutory safeguarding training`],answer:1,explanation:`The specification lists overly affectionate behaviour, gifts, favouritism, time alone, befriending parents/home visits, and private electronic contact. Ordinary warm, supervised practice is not an indicator.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-8-q2`,topicId:`3-8`,stem:`A student on placement sees a practitioner take the same child into a locked stock room most afternoons and has noticed secret gifts and personal-phone messages. The student should:`,options:[`Report the concern the same day to the DSL or placement manager, following the allegations and whistleblowing procedure, and not investigate the colleague`,`Join in so the child is not alone`,`Ask the child in detail what happens and write it on social media`,`Wait until the placement report is finished to avoid awkwardness`],answer:0,explanation:`Time alone, gifts and private messages are specification indicators. Students have the same duty to report. Delay, amateur investigation and public posts are wrong.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-8-q3`,topicId:`3-8`,stem:`Why does KCSIE expect settings to record low-level concerns about staff as well as formal allegations?`,options:[`To punish staff for minor uniform errors`,`Because every concern must go to the police within the hour`,`Because patterns of boundary-testing (gifts, favouritism, time alone) may only be visible when small concerns are written down over time`,`Because data protection forbids recording staff behaviour`],answer:2,explanation:`Low-level concerns create an audit trail of inappropriate relationships forming. They are not automatic police referrals and they are allowed under data protection for safeguarding.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-8-o1`,topicId:`3-8`,marks:4,command:`Outline`,stem:`Outline two indicators that an adult in a setting may have an inappropriate relationship with a child, and state how staff should respond.`,modelAnswer:`One indicator is seeking regular time alone with the same child, combined with gifts or favouritism that single that child out. Another is private texts or social media with the child, or making friends with parents and visiting at home in a way that bypasses professional channels. Staff should record what they have seen, report to the DSL or head the same day under the allegations procedure, and not investigate the colleague themselves; the LADO coordinates concerns that meet the allegation threshold.`,markScheme:[`Valid specification indicator (1)`,`Second distinct specification indicator (1)`,`Report via DSL/head/allegations procedure (1)`,`Do not investigate / LADO where threshold met (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-9`,code:`3.9`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Responding to suspected abuse, or a child at risk or in need, using setting procedures`,page:51,notes:{gist:`When abuse is suspected, or a child is at risk or in need, the practitioner's job is a sequence, not a hunch. Observe and record as appropriate; follow the setting's child protection policies; follow local safeguarding partnership procedures; use accurate lines of reporting in a timely manner; keep professional boundaries; contact the police if the child is in immediate danger. Do not investigate, do not promise confidentiality, and do not alert the alleged source of harm.`,mustKnow:[`Observe and record as appropriate: date, time, facts, exact words, visible marks described professionally (location, size, colour), who was present. Distinguish observation from opinion. Do not examine the child or photograph on a personal device.`,`Follow organisational child protection policies: named DSL and deputy, cause-for-concern form, what to do if the DSL is absent, whistleblowing if the concern is about a colleague.`,`Follow local safeguarding partnership procedures: thresholds, referral forms, what happens after a referral (Working Together 2026). Education does not run a s.47 enquiry itself.`,`Accurate lines of reporting in a timely manner: the same day, not 'after the weekend' or 'when I have written it up neatly'. Immediate danger = 999.`,`Professional boundaries: no leading questions, no promise of secrecy, no discussion in the staffroom or with other parents, no amateur medical or forensic role.`,`Contact the police if the child is in immediate danger, a crime is in progress, or FGM is known in an under-18 (mandatory reporting under the FGM Act 2003 / Serious Crime Act 2015).`,`If the DSL does not act, the individual still has a duty: follow whistleblowing and, if needed, contact children's social care themselves, then record that they did (KCSIE 2026).`],sections:[{heading:`The sequence on the day`,body:[`Listen and stay calm if the child talks. Do not stop them, do not ask 'did X do this?', do not promise that nobody else will be told. Explain that you will need to tell the person whose job is to keep them safe.`,`Record immediately, in the child's words where there is a disclosure. Use a body map only if policy allows and you are describing what you can see without removing clothes. Then tell the DSL or deputy — in person, not only by leaving a note.`,`The DSL uses local partnership thresholds (Working Together 2026; KCSIE 2026; EYFS for non-school providers) to refer to children's social care, and to the police if a crime may have been committed. Information may be shared without consent where necessary to protect a child (DPA 2018 / UK GDPR).`]},{heading:`Boundaries, delay and allegations`,body:[`Professional boundaries protect evidence and the child: no home visits to 'find out more', no confronting a parent in the doorway about an injury, no searching a colleague's locker. Allegations about staff go to the head/DSL and LADO.`,`Timely means the same working day. Delay so that a parent is not 'upset at pickup', or because the DSL is on a course, is how harm continues. A deputy DSL and an out-of-hours number exist for this reason.`,`In need and at risk still follow the same first steps (observe, record, tell). The threshold conversation happens with the DSL and social care, not as a reason for a practitioner to wait.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Local safeguarding partnership procedures, referral and information sharing.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Staff duties, DSL, managing allegations, and referring if the DSL does not act.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Safeguarding and welfare requirements for registered early years providers, including a child protection policy and a lead practitioner.`},{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Lawful sharing of personal data where necessary to protect a child is not a breach.`},{name:`Female Genital Mutilation Act`,year:`2003`,point:`Mandatory reporting of known FGM in under-18s to the police.`}],examMoves:[`Write a sequence: observe/record → setting CP policy → local partnership procedures → timely reporting line → boundaries → 999 if immediate danger.`,`Name the DSL (or deputy) and say 'same day'. Vague 'tell someone' loses marks.`,`State what you will not do: investigate, examine, promise secrecy, alert the alleged abuser.`,`If the scenario is a colleague, switch to allegations / LADO; if it is immediate danger, start with 999.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-9-q1`,topicId:`3-9`,stem:`A child discloses that they are frightened to go home. The practitioner's first professional sequence is to:`,options:[`Promise secrecy, visit the family that evening, and ask leading questions until a perpetrator is named`,`Wait until the next staff meeting so colleagues can vote on a referral`,`Listen without leading questions, record the child's words, tell the DSL the same day, and not alert the person who may be the source of harm`,`Post a description on the staff WhatsApp group to gather opinions`],answer:2,explanation:`The specification sequence is observe/record, follow CP and local procedures, timely reporting, professional boundaries. Promising secrecy, investigating and gossiping are the opposite.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-9-q2`,topicId:`3-9`,stem:`When should a practitioner contact the police directly rather than only completing a cause-for-concern form?`,options:[`Whenever a child is slightly behind in phonics`,`When the child is in immediate danger, a crime is in progress, or a mandatory report (such as known FGM in an under-18) is required`,`Never — only the local authority may speak to the police`,`Only after a parent has given written consent`],answer:1,explanation:`Immediate danger and specified mandatory reports go to the police. Ordinary concerns go via the DSL. Consent is not required to protect a child at risk.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-9-q3`,topicId:`3-9`,stem:`Why may safeguarding information be shared with children's social care without parental consent?`,options:[`Because the Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR permit lawful, necessary sharing to protect a child from harm`,`Because data protection never applies to schools`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 overrides all privacy rights`,`Because HASAWA 1974 requires all records to be public`],answer:0,explanation:`Data protection is not a barrier to safeguarding. Sharing must still be necessary, proportionate and recorded. It is not a free-for-all.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-9-o1`,topicId:`3-9`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain what a practitioner should do, following setting procedures, when they suspect abuse or think a child may be at risk or in need.`,modelAnswer:`The practitioner should observe and record facts, including the child's exact words, without examining the child or adding speculation. They must follow the setting's child protection policy by telling the named DSL or deputy the same day, and follow local safeguarding partnership procedures under Working Together (2026) so that children's social care, not the practitioner, decide whether the child is in need or at risk. Lines of reporting must be accurate and timely; if the child is in immediate danger the police are contacted on 999. Professional boundaries mean no leading questions, no promise of confidentiality, no staffroom discussion and no alerting the alleged source of harm. If the concern is about a colleague, the allegations procedure and LADO are used. If the DSL does not act, KCSIE (2026) still requires the individual to refer and to record that they did.`,markScheme:[`Observe and record appropriately (1)`,`Follow organisational CP policy / named DSL (1)`,`Follow local safeguarding partnership procedures (1)`,`Accurate, timely lines of reporting (1)`,`Professional boundaries (no investigation / no secrecy promise) (1)`,`Police if immediate danger / whistleblowing if DSL does not act (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-10`,code:`3.10`,elementId:`e3`,title:`How abuse, neglect, bullying, persecution and violence can affect development and behaviour`,page:51,notes:{gist:`Harm does not stay in one domain. Abuse, neglect, bullying, persecution and violence can damage educational attainment, attachments and relationships, physical health, mental health, and behaviour (including, for some young people, substance misuse). Effects vary and are not a life sentence: later safe relationships, predictable care and the right help can still change the trajectory. Behaviour that looks like defiance is often a survival response.`,mustKnow:[`Educational attainment: chronic fear and missed sessions consume working memory and attention; language and curiosity shrink where neglect reduces serve-and-return talk; attainment gaps widen and school refusal can follow.`,`Attachments and relationships: insecure or disorganised attachment; difficulty trusting adults; indiscriminate affection after privation; struggle to make or keep friends; repeating victim or controlling roles in peer groups.`,`Physical health: injuries, poor growth, tiredness, untreated illness, psychosomatic symptoms (stomach aches, headaches), delayed motor skills where play has been limited.`,`Mental health: anxiety, depression, shame, low self-esteem, post-traumatic responses, self-blame. Practitioners notice and refer; they do not diagnose.`,`Behaviour: withdrawal or aggression; regression (toileting, sleep); hypervigilance; controlling play; risk-taking; school refusal; in adolescence, substance misuse as self-medication or peer exploitation.`,`Bullying and persecution (including racism, homophobia, disablism) produce similar mental health, attendance and attainment effects even when there is no caregiver abuse. KCSIE treats bullying and child-on-child abuse as safeguarding.`,`Domestic abuse and violence: children who see, hear or experience the effects are victims under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021. They may copy roles, freeze, or try to protect siblings. Effects are likely, not certain — avoid labelling the child as the problem.`],sections:[{heading:`Impact across the specification domains`,body:[`Attainment falls when a child is in a threat state: attention, memory and the confidence to take learning risks are crowded out. Neglect in the early years also removes the talk and play that build language, so the child presents as 'behind' when the issue is omitted care.`,`Attachments and relationships are the mechanism behind much of the rest. A child who cannot use an adult as a secure base will struggle to play, to be taught and to repair conflict. Bowlby's attachment work and later trauma-informed practice both support putting safety and relationship before curriculum catch-up.`,`Physical and mental health travel together: poor sleep and appetite, medically unexplained pain, anxiety and depression. Behaviour then shows the survival pattern — fight, flight, freeze — which settings may misread as ADHD, 'rudeness' or 'attention-seeking'. Substance misuse in older pupils can be both impact and a further risk.`]},{heading:`Bullying, persecution, violence — and what practitioners do`,body:[`Peer bullying and persecution are not 'character-building'. They predict anxiety, depression, absence and falling attainment, and the child who harms others also needs intervention. Persecution related to a protected characteristic engages the Equality Act 2010 as well as safeguarding.`,`Violence in the home or community can normalise aggression or produce frozen watchfulness. Food-hoarding, inability to sit with a back to the door, or hitting at tidy-up may be protection, not attack.`,`Safeguard first (DSL). Then keep the child in ordinary, predictable nursery or school life as far as possible. Co-regulation, a key person, PACE-informed responses, and partnership with family, virtual school or CAMHS where appropriate, support recovery. Punitive approaches that ignore cause re-traumatise; boundaries still matter, delivered without humiliation.`]}],theorists:[{name:`John Bowlby / Mary Main`,idea:`Insecure or disorganised attachment after frightening or frightened care undermines later relationships, exploration and learning.`},{name:`Bruce Perry (trauma-informed practice)`,idea:`Repeated fear shapes regulation and learning; safety and relationship need to come before curriculum catch-up.`}],legislation:[{name:`Domestic Abuse Act`,year:`2021`,point:`Children who see, hear or experience the effects are victims; impact on mental health and behaviour is recognised in law.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Harm includes impairment of health or development; welfare is paramount.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Persecution related to a protected characteristic is unlawful; settings must act.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,year:`2026`,point:`Bullying, child-on-child abuse and extra-familial harm are safeguarding issues.`}],examMoves:[`Cover more than one specification domain (attainment, attachments, physical health, mental health, behaviour) or you cap the marks.`,`Link a specific harm (neglect vs bullying vs domestic abuse) to a specific effect, then a practitioner response.`,`AO3: effects are likely, not certain; later safe relationships can still help; do not write that every abused child will misuse substances.`,`Name the Domestic Abuse Act 2021 if the scenario is violence at home.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-10-q1`,topicId:`3-10`,stem:`Persistent neglect in the early years is most likely to affect language and attainment because:`,options:[`Children are born with a fixed vocabulary`,`The National Curriculum bans talk in neglected households`,`Language and learning grow through care, talk and play, which are reduced when care is omitted, and chronic stress consumes attention and working memory`,`Neglect only affects bones, never communication or learning`],answer:2,explanation:`Omitted interaction and a persistent threat state are mechanisms for delayed language and lower attainment, not a genetic sentence.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-10-q2`,topicId:`3-10`,stem:`A child who witnesses repeated domestic abuse at home has become anxious, is falling behind, and has started to hit other children. Which statement is most accurate?`,options:[`Under the Domestic Abuse Act 2021 they are a victim; anxiety, attainment and aggression are recognised impacts, and the practitioner should safeguard via the DSL as well as support regulation`,`They are legally unaffected because they were not the direct adult target`,`They must be excluded because they will automatically become a perpetrator`,`No support is needed if they can still complete some worksheets`],answer:0,explanation:`Seeing or hearing domestic abuse is harm in law. Attainment and behaviour impacts are expected. Exclusion is not the first response; safeguarding plus co-regulation is.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-10-q3`,topicId:`3-10`,stem:`Why is it unhelpful to describe a neglected child's food-hoarding only as 'stealing'?`,options:[`Because stealing is never a problem in settings`,`Because the behaviour may be a survival response to hunger or unpredictability; the child still needs a boundary, but also safeguarding, physical care and emotional support`,`Because settings must never have rules about food`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 legalises theft`],answer:1,explanation:`Behaviour as communication does not mean 'no limits'; it means the response must address cause, health and safety as well as the act.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-10-o1`,topicId:`3-10`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how experiences of abuse, neglect, bullying, persecution or violence can change a child's development and behaviour.`,modelAnswer:`Educational attainment often falls because chronic fear and missed sessions reduce attention, working memory and the confidence to learn, while neglect also limits the talk and play that build language. Attachments and relationships can become insecure or disorganised, so the child struggles to trust adults and to make friends. Physical health may show as poor growth, tiredness or untreated illness, and mental health as anxiety, depression or shame. Behaviour may swing between withdrawal and aggression, include regression, or, for some young people, substance misuse. Bullying and persecution can produce the same mental health and attendance effects even when home is safe. A practitioner should first follow safeguarding procedures, then offer predictable routines and co-regulation rather than purely punitive sanctions. Effects are serious but not inevitable if later relationships are safe and support is sustained.`,markScheme:[`Impact on educational attainment (1)`,`Impact on attachments / relationships (1)`,`Impact on physical health (1)`,`Impact on mental health (1)`,`Impact on behaviour (including possible substance misuse) (1)`,`Practitioner response and/or the point that effects are not inevitable (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-11`,code:`3.11`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Why equality, diversity and inclusion matter`,page:51,notes:{gist:`Promoting equality, diversity and inclusion (EDI) is a legal duty and a professional one. It prevents discrimination, creates equality of opportunity, meets individual needs through equity, celebrates difference, recognises varied family circumstances and cultures, and upholds dignity and respect. Done well, it protects children and the setting's reputation; done badly, it becomes tokenism or unlawful practice under the Equality Act 2010.`,mustKnow:[`Legal responsibilities: Equality Act 2010 — nine protected characteristics; unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation; reasonable adjustments for disability; Public Sector Equality Duty for maintained settings. UNCRC 1989 Articles 2 and 12 (non-discrimination and voice). SEND Code of Practice 0–25 for inclusive education.`,`Prevent discrimination: in admissions, curriculum, play, behaviour policy, food, dress, festivals and whose voice is heard. Indirect discrimination (a rule that looks the same for everyone but disadvantages a group) is still unlawful.`,`Equality of opportunity: every child can take part and progress; barriers are removed rather than the child being told to fit in.`,`Individual needs and equity: fairness is not sameness. Extra time, a visual timetable, a quiet space, a prayer room, translated information or an interpreter are equity, not 'special favours'.`,`Celebrate differences and value diversity: books, role play, languages, family structures and festivals that reflect the actual children, not a one-off 'culture day' that stereotypes.`,`Recognise different family circumstances and cultures: kinship care, same-sex parents, faith, Gypsy, Roma and Traveller families, asylum-seeking families, young carers. Do not assume one 'normal' home.`,`Dignity, respect, professional responsibilities and setting reputation: how staff speak about families in the staffroom is practice; Ofsted inspects culture, and a discrimination complaint can become a legal and reputational crisis.`],sections:[{heading:`Law, opportunity and equity`,body:[`The Equality Act 2010 is the backbone: treat a child less favourably because of race, disability, sex, religion or belief, sexual orientation of parents, or another protected characteristic and you are outside the law. Reasonable adjustments are anticipatory — you plan for the wheelchair user and the autistic child before they arrive.`,`Equality of opportunity is the outcome; equity is the method. Giving every child the same worksheet is equal treatment, not equal opportunity, if one child cannot read it, see it or sit still for it. The SEND Code of Practice 0–25 and the EYFS both expect provision that meets individual need.`,`Preventing discrimination includes the hidden curriculum: who is asked to speak, whose names are pronounced, whether boys are sent to fetch and girls to tidy, whether EAL is treated as a deficit.`]},{heading:`Diversity, families, dignity and reputation`,body:[`Valuing diversity is everyday practice: dual-language books, accurate skin tones in paints, dolls that match the children, Ramadan and Christmas both marked without forcing participation, a child's preferred name used. Token displays with no change to practice do not meet the duty.`,`Family circumstances vary. A looked-after child, a child with two mums, a child who translates for parents, a child in a refuge — each needs dignity, not pity or gossip. UNCRC rights belong to the child regardless of the adults' status.`,`Professional responsibility includes calling out discriminatory language from children, colleagues and visitors, following the equality policy, and knowing that the setting's reputation with families, inspectors and the local authority rests on this culture as much as on data.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Protected characteristics, reasonable adjustments, PSED; duty to prevent discrimination and advance equality of opportunity.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Inclusive practice, graduated approach and coproduction so individual needs are met.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Non-discrimination (Article 2), voice (Article 12), education (Article 28) and identity (Article 8).`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Inclusive practice and partnership with parents as a principle of the framework.`}],examMoves:[`Name the Equality Act 2010 and at least one duty (reasonable adjustments, PSED, prevent discrimination) — 'be nice to everyone' is not AO1.`,`Distinguish equality (opportunity) from equity (meeting individual need) from diversity (valuing difference).`,`Apply to the named child: language, disability, faith, family structure — not a generic poster.`,`Finish with dignity/respect and the professional/reputational cost of getting it wrong.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-11-q1`,topicId:`3-11`,stem:`Under the Equality Act 2010, reasonable adjustments for disability are:`,options:[`Optional favours if staffing allows`,`Only required after a parent makes a formal complaint`,`An anticipatory legal duty so disabled children are not substantially disadvantaged`,`Replaced by the Prevent duty`],answer:2,explanation:`Reasonable adjustments are a legal duty and they are anticipatory. They are not a reward for complaining and they are not replaced by Prevent.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-11-q2`,topicId:`3-11`,stem:`A nursery always puts pork on the snack menu and has no alternative. Several children do not eat pork for religious reasons and go hungry. Promoting EDI here means:`,options:[`Telling families to drop their beliefs at the door`,`Recognising different family circumstances and cultures, offering an equitable alternative, and preventing indirect discrimination`,`Making pork compulsory so everyone is treated 'the same'`,`Ignoring food because EDI only applies to displays`],answer:1,explanation:`Sameness is not equality of opportunity. An alternative snack is equity and prevents indirect discrimination linked to religion or belief.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-11-q3`,topicId:`3-11`,stem:`Why is celebrating diversity without meeting individual needs an incomplete approach to EDI?`,options:[`Because displays are illegal under the Equality Act 2010`,`Because settings must never mention culture`,`Because Ofsted only inspects mathematics`,`Because valuing difference must sit alongside legal duties, equality of opportunity, equity and dignity — a culture day does not remove a barrier to access`],answer:3,explanation:`Diversity without equity and anti-discrimination is tokenism. The specification expects all of the elements together.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-11-o1`,topicId:`3-11`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two reasons why it is important to promote equality, diversity and inclusion in an education or early years setting.`,modelAnswer:`It is a legal responsibility under the Equality Act 2010 to prevent discrimination and to make reasonable adjustments, so every child has equality of opportunity to take part and progress. Promoting EDI also means using equity to meet individual needs, celebrating differences, and recognising different family circumstances and cultures with dignity and respect, which supports belonging and the setting's professional reputation. Without this, children are excluded in practice even if a policy file says they are welcome.`,markScheme:[`First valid reason (legal / prevent discrimination / equality of opportunity) (1)`,`Develops with named law or example (1)`,`Second distinct reason (equity / diversity / families / dignity / reputation) (1)`,`Develops the second reason (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-12`,code:`3.12`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Fair expectations and avoiding labels`,page:51,notes:{gist:`Labels stick. Calling a child 'naughty', 'low ability', 'from a problem family' or 'the SEND one' shapes what adults expect and what the child believes they can be. Appropriate expectations, without stigmatising labels, support independence, motivation and confidence, improve academic outcomes, create a culture of achievement regardless of ability, and remove the burden of guilt or blame from the child and their parents.`,mustKnow:[`Not stigmatised: person-first, needs-first language ('a child with an EHCP', 'a child who is hungry') rather than a master-status label. Stigma reduces belonging and is contrary to the Equality Act 2010 and the SEND Code of Practice.`,`Independence: if adults expect a child to be helpless, they over-help and the child never practises the skill. Appropriate challenge plus scaffolding builds independence.`,`Motivation and confidence: Rosenthal and Jacobson (Pygmalion) showed that teacher expectation can become a self-fulfilling prophecy. High, realistic expectation plus specific praise grows confidence; a 'can't' label shrinks it.`,`Academic outcomes: children live up or down to the pitch of work they are offered. 'Bottom group' language and a narrowed curriculum cap attainment.`,`Culture of achievement regardless of ability: every child is entitled to progress and to have success recognised, including children with SEND, EAL and those who are looked after — not only the children who find learning easy.`,`Removes burden of guilt and blame on the individual or parents: poverty, SEND, trauma and language difference are not moral failings. Blame ('if only they tried', 'those parents don't care') damages partnership and the child's self-concept.`,`Appropriate does not mean no standards. Boundaries and ambitious goals still matter; they are matched to stage and need, not to a stereotype.`],sections:[{heading:`How labels harm, and what 'appropriate' looks like`,body:[`A label is a shortcut that hides the child. 'Challenging' may mean hungry, frightened, autistic, bored or abused. Once the label is in the staffroom, observations become confirmation-bias: adults notice the incidents that fit and miss the progress that does not.`,`Appropriate expectations are informed by developmental stage, the child's prior learning, and any SEND or EAL need — not by postcode, family structure, gender or a sibling's reputation. The EYFS and National Curriculum both assume progress for every child.`,`Independence and confidence grow when the child is offered a task they can succeed at with the right scaffold, then the scaffold is faded. Learned helplessness is an adult-created additional need.`]},{heading:`Outcomes, culture and blame`,body:[`Academic outcomes improve when teachers and practitioners refuse a fixed-ability story. Growth-mindset language is useful only if the work is actually accessible; a poster will not undo a permanently low set.`,`A culture of achievement regardless of ability is visible in whose work is displayed, who represents the setting, and whether children with SEND are included in trips as a planning default.`,`Guilt and blame land on children ('you're lazy') and parents ('they don't read at home') when the real issues may be working hours, literacy, housing or an unmet need. The Children and Families Act 2014 and the SEND Code of Practice expect coproduction, not blame. Partnership (Element 6) starts with curiosity, not a label.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Rosenthal and Jacobson`,idea:`Pygmalion effect: teacher expectations can become self-fulfilling prophecies for attainment and behaviour.`},{name:`Carol Dweck`,idea:`A fixed-ability label undermines a growth mindset; effort and strategy language supports motivation — only if tasks are genuinely accessible.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Stigmatising treatment linked to a protected characteristic can be discrimination or harassment.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Focus on outcomes and coproduction; avoid deficit labelling that lowers expectation.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Every child has a right to education and to be treated with dignity, not as a problem to be named.`}],examMoves:[`Pair 'appropriate expectations' with 'not labelling' — the question wants both.`,`Use a named mechanism: self-fulfilling prophecy, learned helplessness, stigma, blame of parents.`,`Apply to the child in the scenario (SEND, poverty, behaviour, EAL) and say what the adult should say and plan instead.`,`Do not argue for having no standards; appropriate means ambitious and matched, not woolly.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-12-q1`,topicId:`3-12`,stem:`Why can labelling a child as 'low ability' harm academic outcomes?`,options:[`Because adults then offer a narrowed curriculum and the child lives down to the expectation (a self-fulfilling prophecy)`,`Because labels are required by the National Curriculum`,`Because children with SEND are not allowed to make progress`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 bans all grouping`],answer:0,explanation:`Expectation shapes provision, which shapes attainment. Grouping is not banned; stigma and a capped offer are the harm.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-12-q2`,topicId:`3-12`,stem:`A practitioner says, in front of a child, 'He's from one of those families — there's no point sending books home.' This is harmful because it:`,options:[`Is an example of high, appropriate expectation`,`Is required by the SEND Code of Practice`,`Stigmatises the child, blames parents, and undermines independence, motivation, partnership and a culture of achievement`,`Protects the setting's reputation`],answer:2,explanation:`The specification links not labelling with removing guilt/blame from the child and parents. This comment does the opposite and is professionally unacceptable.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-12-q3`,topicId:`3-12`,stem:`Appropriate expectations are best described as:`,options:[`Having no standards so that nobody fails`,`The same worksheet for every child regardless of need, so treatment is identical`,`Assuming looked-after children cannot achieve`,`Ambitious goals matched to the child's stage and needs, with scaffolding that builds independence rather than a stigmatising fixed label`],answer:3,explanation:`Appropriate is not 'no bar' and not 'identical treatment'. It is high, matched expectation without stigma — equity rather than a label.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-12-o1`,topicId:`3-12`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two reasons why it is important to have appropriate expectations and not to label children and young people.`,modelAnswer:`Appropriate expectations without labels support independence, motivation and confidence: if adults assume a child 'can't', they over-help or offer a narrowed curriculum and the child lives down to that self-fulfilling prophecy. Not stigmatising also improves academic outcomes and builds a culture of achievement regardless of ability, and it removes the burden of guilt and blame from the child and their parents, which protects self-concept and partnership. Needs can still be named in plans (for example an EHCP) without turning the need into the child's identity.`,markScheme:[`First valid reason (independence / motivation / confidence / not stigmatised) (1)`,`Develops with a because or example (1)`,`Second distinct reason (academic outcomes / culture of achievement / removes guilt or blame) (1)`,`Develops the second reason (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`3-13`,code:`3.13`,elementId:`e3`,title:`Barriers to learning, and ways to help everyone take part`,page:52,notes:{gist:`Participation is not automatic. Barriers can be physical accessibility, mental health, attitudes and expectations, the curriculum, family background and socio-economic circumstances. Strategies include inclusion training, partnership working (including psychological wellbeing), physical environment adaptations, an accessible curriculum and assessment, reviewing EDI policies, information about financial support, and support through transitions. Name the barrier, then the strategy — a list of one without the other is half an answer.`,mustKnow:[`Physical accessibility: steps, narrow doors, inaccessible toilets, poor acoustics and lighting, lack of changing space. Equality Act 2010 reasonable adjustments and the setting's accessibility policy are the legal response.`,`Mental health: anxiety, low mood, trauma responses and unmet emotional need reduce attendance, attention and willingness to join in. Early help, key person, MHST/CAMHS pathways and a mentally healthy environment are strategies.`,`Attitudes and expectations: adult and peer stereotypes, low expectation, bullying, racism, disablism. Inclusion training and a reviewed EDI culture challenge this (see 3.11 and 3.12).`,`Curriculum: inaccessible pitch, language load, assessment that only values one way of showing learning, lack of representation. Accessible curriculum and assessment (multiple means of showing knowledge, scaffolds, EAL-friendly input) open participation.`,`Family background: language, working hours, care history, faith, housing, young carer roles. Partnership working, translated information and flexible contact methods reduce the barrier.`,`Socio-economic: poverty affects uniform, trips, food, data/devices, quiet space to study, and attendance. Information about financial support (FSM, pupil premium, setting hardship funds, trip subsidies) and discreet, non-stigmatising help matter.`,`Transitions (home to nursery, room to room, primary to secondary, into AP) are high-risk moments. Planned visits, information-sharing, key-person handover and coproduced passports support participation through the change.`],sections:[{heading:`Naming barriers without blaming the child`,body:[`Physical and curriculum barriers sit in the environment and the offer, not in the child — that is the social model (Element 4). A child who cannot climb the stairs is not 'refusing PE'; the building is excluding them.`,`Mental health and attitudes are less visible. A child who will not come in at the door may be anxious, not defiant. A child who never puts a hand up may have learned that their voice is not welcome. Low adult expectation is itself a barrier.`,`Family background and socio-economic circumstances are often tangled. Poverty is not a lack of aspiration. Asking for a costly costume, assuming every home has data, or running parents' evening only at 4 p.m. excludes working and low-income families. UNCRC rights do not depend on income.`]},{heading:`Strategies that actually increase participation`,body:[`Inclusion training for all staff, including students and lunchtime teams, changes attitudes and the quality of adjustments. Reviewing EDI policies is not a filing task: it checks whether trips, uniform, food, behaviour and curriculum still create barriers.`,`Physical adaptations (ramps, hearing loops, quiet spaces, accessible toilets) plus an accessible curriculum and assessment (visuals, chunked tasks, extra time, practical ways to show learning) help disabled children and often help everyone.`,`Partnership working includes families, health, educational psychology and wellbeing services, not only a yearly report. Information about FSM, uniform grants and trip support must be offered discreetly. Transitions need a named adult, a visit, and information that travels with the child — especially for SEND, looked-after children and those with mental health needs.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments and accessibility duties; discrimination includes failing to remove barriers.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Identify barriers, use a graduated approach, coproduce, support through transitions.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`EHCPs and the local offer as tools to remove barriers for children with more complex needs.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Right to education (Article 28) and to support for a decent standard of living (Article 27).`}],examMoves:[`Pair each barrier with a matching strategy. Examiners tick both sides of the heading.`,`Use the specification list (physical, mental health, attitudes, curriculum, family, socio-economic) rather than a vague 'some children struggle'.`,`Apply: if the scenario is a trip cost, write socio-economic + financial support + dignity, not only 'talk to parents'.`,`Name the Equality Act 2010 for physical/attitudinal barriers and the SEND Code of Practice for curriculum and transition.`]},quiz:[{id:`3-13-q1`,topicId:`3-13`,stem:`Which of the following is a socio-economic barrier to participation?`,options:[`The cost of uniform, trips, data and food, which can exclude children whose families cannot pay`,`A ramped entrance`,`A visual timetable`,`Inclusion training for staff`],answer:0,explanation:`Socio-economic barriers are about resources. Ramps, visual timetables and training are strategies or physical/curriculum supports, not socio-economic barriers.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`3-13-q2`,topicId:`3-13`,stem:`A Year 6 child with anxiety and a physical disability is refusing the secondary transition days because the site has stairs and they fear getting lost. The most complete strategy is:`,options:[`Tell them to be brave and attend with everyone else, with no adjustments`,`Remove them from the roll so the secondary school is not inconvenienced`,`Delay all transition indefinitely without a plan`,`Physical environment adaptations, support through the transition (visits, a map, a named adult), partnership with family and wellbeing services, and a review of accessibility/EDI practice`],answer:3,explanation:`The scenario combines physical, mental health and transition barriers. The specification strategies address all three; 'be brave' and exclusion do not.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`3-13-q3`,topicId:`3-13`,stem:`Why is reviewing EDI policies a strategy for participation, not just an admin task?`,options:[`Because policies replace the need for reasonable adjustments`,`Because a review tests whether uniform, trips, curriculum and behaviour rules still create barriers, and leads to change rather than a laminated intention`,`Because Ofsted only reads policies and never watches practice`,`Because the UNCRC bans written policies`],answer:1,explanation:`Policy review is only a strategy if it changes the barriers children actually meet. Adjustments and practice still matter; inspectors look at both.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`3-13-o1`,topicId:`3-13`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Describe three barriers that can hold back a learner's education, with one strategy for each that helps them take part.`,modelAnswer:`Physical accessibility (steps, inaccessible toilets, poor acoustics) can exclude disabled children; the strategy is environment adaptations and Equality Act 2010 reasonable adjustments. Mental health (anxiety, trauma) reduces attendance and concentration; partnership working with families and wellbeing services, plus a key adult and planned transitions, supports participation. Socio-economic barriers (uniform, trip costs, food, data) exclude children in poverty; settings should share information about financial support discreetly and review EDI policies so optional extras do not become compulsory. Attitudes, curriculum pitch and family background are further barriers; inclusion training and an accessible curriculum and assessment address those. In each case the barrier is named and the strategy is matched, rather than blaming the child.`,markScheme:[`Barrier 1 named (1) + matching strategy (1)`,`Barrier 2 named (1) + matching strategy (1)`,`Barrier 3 named (1) + matching strategy (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]}],i=[{id:`4-1`,code:`4.1`,elementId:`e4`,title:`Why SEND policies and processes exist`,page:53,notes:{gist:`SEND provision is not a kindness: it is structured by named policies and by two key processes. Settings need a SEND policy, an equality policy, an accessibility policy and, where relevant, an alternative provision policy. Early Help Assessment brings early support and partnership from the early years through the school years. An Education, Health and Care Plan (EHCP) is for complex needs that go beyond the SEND provision normally available. Both sit under the Children and Families Act 2014 Part 3 and the SEND Code of Practice 0–25.`,mustKnow:[`SEND policy: how the setting identifies need, uses the graduated approach (assess, plan, do, review), names the SENCO, works with families and other agencies, and uses its notional SEND budget. It is the operational document Ofsted and parents will ask to see.`,`Equality policy: Equality Act 2010 duties — no unlawful discrimination, harassment or victimisation; reasonable adjustments; Public Sector Equality Duty where it applies. SEND is a disability issue as well as an educational one.`,`Accessibility policy (accessibility plan in schools): how premises, curriculum and information will be made accessible over time. Anticipatory, not a reaction to one child.`,`Alternative provision policy: when and how a child who cannot be in the usual classroom (medical need, exclusion, specialist placement) still receives suitable full-time education, with safeguarding, review and a plan to return or move on.`,`Early Help Assessment: a process, not a place. It organises early support, partnership with the family, and a Team Around the Child from the early years through school, so needs do not wait for crisis or for an EHCP.`,`EHCP: a statutory plan for children and young people 0–25 whose needs cannot be met from normally available SEND provision. It specifies education, health and social care outcomes and provision. Local authority maintains it; settings must deliver the education section.`,`Purpose of the whole set: lawful, consistent, coproduced support so children with SEND participate, progress and are kept safe — not a folder of policies that never change practice.`],sections:[{heading:`Four policies that must actually drive practice`,body:[`The SEND policy tells every adult how to notice need, talk to the SENCO, involve parents and review support. Without it, provision depends on who happens to be on duty. The SENCO coordinates; class teachers and key persons remain responsible for the child's day-to-day progress (SEND Code of Practice).`,`Equality and accessibility policies stop SEND being treated as an optional extra. A child who cannot access the hall, the worksheet or the website is being discriminated against if reasonable adjustments have not been made. The accessibility plan is a three-year school duty; early years settings meet the same idea through the EYFS and Equality Act 2010.`,`An alternative provision policy matters when the usual setting cannot meet need: medical tuition, a PRU, a specialist unit, a reduced timetable that is time-limited and reviewed. It must not become unofficial exclusion. Safeguarding, attendance and the child's voice still apply.`]},{heading:`Two processes: Early Help and the EHCP`,body:[`Early Help Assessment is the right first process for many children: speech delay, emerging behaviour that is communication, a family under strain, a health need that education must understand. It is partnership-based, travels with the child from nursery into school, and can close if support works. It does not replace child protection if the child is at risk.`,`An EHCP is not a gold star and not automatic. It is for complex needs beyond normally available provision after the graduated approach has been used. Parents, the young person (where old enough) and the setting can request an EHC needs assessment. The plan is legally enforceable for the education provision it specifies.`,`Purpose: policies set the local rules; processes put the right intensity of help around the child at the right time. Confusing Early Help with an EHCP, or writing an accessibility policy that never changes a doorway, is how children wait.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Part 3: SEN system, EHCPs 0–25, local offer, coproduction with parents and young people.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Graduated approach, SENCO role, Early Help, EHC needs assessment and plan, duties on early years and schools.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments and accessibility; disability includes many SEND needs.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Providers must have arrangements for supporting children with SEND and a named SENCO in group provision.`}],examMoves:[`Name the policy or process and its purpose — 'they have a SEND policy' without saying what it does is not enough.`,`Contrast Early Help Assessment (early, partnership, normally available plus extra help) with EHCP (statutory, complex needs beyond normally available provision).`,`Apply: a child with mild speech delay is not automatically an EHCP candidate; a child whose needs cannot be met after the graduated approach may be.`,`Link equality/accessibility to the Equality Act 2010 so SEND is not only a 'special needs' story.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-1-q1`,topicId:`4-1`,stem:`An Education, Health and Care Plan is best described as:`,options:[`A voluntary sticker chart used in every Reception class`,`A replacement for the Equality Act 2010`,`A process that settings may use instead of safeguarding`,`A statutory plan for children and young people 0–25 whose complex needs cannot be met from the SEND provision normally available`],answer:3,explanation:`An EHCP is statutory, 0–25, and for needs beyond normally available provision. It does not replace equality law or safeguarding.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-1-q2`,topicId:`4-1`,stem:`A three-year-old's speech is delayed and the family is under strain, but there is no evidence of complex multi-agency need beyond what nursery and speech and language therapy can offer. The most appropriate process to start is:`,options:[`An immediate EHCP with no assessment`,`An Early Help Assessment to organise early support and partnership from the early years, reviewed as the child moves on`,`Permanent alternative provision in a PRU`,`Doing nothing until Year 6`],answer:1,explanation:`Early Help Assessment is the specification process for early, partnership-based support. An EHCP is for complex needs beyond normally available provision; delay until Year 6 wastes the early years.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-1-q3`,topicId:`4-1`,stem:`Why must a SEND policy sit alongside equality and accessibility policies rather than replacing them?`,options:[`Because SEND is never a disability issue`,`Because accessibility plans are optional posters`,`Because identification and the graduated approach (SEND policy) still leave a legal duty to prevent discrimination and to adapt premises, curriculum and information (Equality Act 2010)`,`Because alternative provision abolishes the need for reasonable adjustments in the main setting`],answer:2,explanation:`SEND policy is the educational process; equality and accessibility are legal duties that stop the child being excluded by the building or the offer. AP is additional, not a way to avoid adjustments.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-1-o1`,topicId:`4-1`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Choose two SEND policies or processes and explain what each one is for.`,modelAnswer:`A SEND policy sets out how the setting identifies need, uses the graduated approach, names the SENCO and works with families, so support is consistent rather than dependent on who is on duty. An Early Help Assessment is a process that organises early support and partnership from the early years through school, so emerging needs are met before they escalate. By contrast, an EHCP under the Children and Families Act 2014 is for complex needs that cannot be met from normally available SEND provision, and equality and accessibility policies exist so reasonable adjustments required by the Equality Act 2010 actually happen. Purpose in every case is lawful, coproduced provision that lets the child participate and progress.`,markScheme:[`Names policy or process 1 (1)`,`Explains its purpose (1)`,`Links to practice or legislation (1)`,`Names a distinct policy or process 2 (1)`,`Explains its purpose (1)`,`Links to practice, legislation or a contrast (e.g. Early Help vs EHCP) (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-2`,code:`4.2`,elementId:`e4`,title:`How multi-agency teams give joined-up support to learners with SEND`,page:53,notes:{gist:`No single profession holds the whole child. A multi-agency team shares information, allocates tasks and responsibilities, brings assessments from multiple perspectives, and creates an effective approach to diverse needs so that early prevention and intervention actually happen. Integrated support means the family tells their story once and the plan is joined up — education, health and care pulling in the same direction under the SEND Code of Practice and Working Together (2026).`,mustKnow:[`Sharing information: with consent where possible, and without consent where necessary to protect a child (DPA 2018 / UK GDPR). Each agency holds a piece (attendance, health, speech, home) that others cannot see unless it is shared.`,`Allocating tasks and responsibilities: a Team Around the Child or EHCP review names who does what by when — SENCO, key person, SALT, EP, OT, paediatrician, social worker, parent — so work is not duplicated or dropped.`,`Assessments from multiple perspectives: education sees learning and peer relationships; health sees hearing, vision, neurology, mental health; care sees family capacity. One assessment in isolation misreads the child.`,`Effective approach to diverse needs: autism, ADHD, physical disability, SEMH and MLD do not need the same plan. The team matches provision to the actual profile.`,`Early prevention and intervention: the point of the team is to act before exclusion, before language fully stalls, before a parent reaches crisis. Early Help is multi-agency work, not a waiting list for an EHCP.`,`Coproduction with parents, carers and the young person is not optional (Children and Families Act 2014). The family is part of the team, not a guest at it.`,`Barriers: jargon, missed meetings, data-protection myths, professional hierarchy. A named lead professional and a single plan reduce those.`],sections:[{heading:`What 'integrated' actually means`,body:[`Integrated is more than a meeting. If speech and language therapy sets targets the classroom never uses, or the paediatrician changes medication the school is not told about, support is parallel, not integrated. Shared outcomes, shared language and shared review dates are the test.`,`Information sharing is the hinge. A nursery that does not tell the receiving school about an Early Help plan, or a school that will not tell CAMHS about attendance, forces the family to carry the file in their head. Lawful sharing is a duty, not a favour.`,`Allocating tasks prevents the 'someone should' problem. The educational psychologist assesses cognition; the SENCO writes the school provision map; the parent practises the agreed signs at home; the TA does not become an untrained therapist by default.`]},{heading:`Diverse needs, early help, and the child's day`,body:[`Multiple perspectives stop a behaviour being treated only as a behaviour: a hearing loss, hungry child, traumatised child and autistic child can look similar at 9 a.m. Assessment from health, education and care distinguishes them.`,`Early prevention is cheaper in human terms than a late EHCP after years of failure. Health visitors, SALT in the early years, and a TAC in Reception are multi-agency working at its most useful.`,`On placement, the practitioner's role is to observe, record, contribute the education view, keep professional boundaries, and not wait for a specialist to 'fix' the child before teaching them.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Joint commissioning, EHCPs that include education, health and care, coproduction.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Multi-agency working, lead professional, Early Help and annual reviews.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,year:`2026`,point:`Agencies must share information and work together; SEND and safeguarding often overlap.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`2004`,point:`Duty to cooperate to improve children's wellbeing.`},{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Lawful basis for sharing; not a barrier to integrated support or safeguarding.`}],examMoves:[`Use the five specification functions (share information, allocate tasks, multiple assessments, diverse needs, early prevention) — not just 'they work together'.`,`Name two agencies and what each uniquely contributes in the scenario.`,`Mention coproduction: the family is in the team.`,`AO3: integration fails when meetings happen but classroom practice does not change.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-2-q1`,topicId:`4-2`,stem:`Which of the following is a core role of a multi-agency team supporting a child with SEND?`,options:[`Keeping each profession's assessment secret so that parents are not confused`,`Replacing the SENCO so schools no longer need a SEND policy`,`Waiting until the child is excluded before meeting`,`Sharing information, allocating tasks and responsibilities, and bringing assessments from multiple perspectives so intervention is early and matched to diverse needs`],answer:3,explanation:`The specification lists sharing, allocating, multiple perspectives, diverse needs and early prevention. Secrecy and delay are the opposite of integrated support.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-2-q2`,topicId:`4-2`,stem:`A Reception child has delayed speech, possible hearing loss and housing insecurity. Why is a multi-agency team more effective than the teacher acting alone?`,options:[`Because teachers are not allowed to speak to parents`,`Because SALT, audiology, housing/Early Help and education each hold a different assessment and task; together they prevent the delay being treated only as 'naughty' or only as a curriculum issue`,`Because an EHCP is automatic as soon as three agencies are named`,`Because data protection bans the teacher from recording observations`],answer:1,explanation:`Multiple perspectives and allocated tasks match diverse needs and enable early intervention. An EHCP is not automatic; teachers can and must work with families and record.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-2-q3`,topicId:`4-2`,stem:`Why can a myth about data protection undermine integrated SEND support?`,options:[`Because staff may refuse to share necessary information, so agencies duplicate assessments and the family repeats their story, delaying early intervention`,`Because the Data Protection Act 2018 requires all files to be public`,`Because GDPR banned EHCPs`,`Because only Ofsted may share information`],answer:0,explanation:`DPA 2018 / UK GDPR allow lawful, necessary sharing. Treating data protection as a blanket ban fragments the team and delays help.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-2-o1`,topicId:`4-2`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two ways a team from different agencies can give joined-up support to a learner with SEND.`,modelAnswer:`The team shares information so that education, health and care each see the whole child rather than one slice, which allows assessments from multiple perspectives and stops a need being misread (for example treating hearing loss only as behaviour). It also allocates tasks and responsibilities, with the family as partners, so speech targets, classroom strategies and home support match and early prevention actually happens rather than each agency waiting for another. Integration is the joined-up plan, not the meeting itself.`,markScheme:[`First valid function (share / allocate / multiple assessments / diverse needs / early intervention) (1)`,`Develops how that integrates support (1)`,`Second distinct function (1)`,`Develops the second / names agencies or coproduction (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-3`,code:`4.3`,elementId:`e4`,title:`Integration, equity and inclusion: what each means and how they differ`,page:53,notes:{gist:`These three words are not synonyms, and examiners expect the difference. Integration adds separate support so the child can access the existing curriculum; success depends on the child's ability to adapt, and extra support benefits only those with SEN. Equity starts with the educator's own culture, values and biases, builds parent partnerships and shared aspirations, and offers a fair curriculum and holistic development so social cohesion can grow. Inclusion gives equal rights, access and choices; the environment changes so each child can succeed, and extra adaptations benefit everyone.`,mustKnow:[`Integration: the child is physically present in the mainstream room or setting, but the curriculum, environment and routines stay as they were. Extra resources (a TA at the child's side, a withdrawal group) are bolted on so the child can cope with the existing offer.`,`Under integration, success depends on the child's ability to adapt. If they cannot keep up, the conclusion is that they 'are not ready' for mainstream, rather than that the offer is too rigid.`,`Extra support under integration benefits only those identified with SEN. Peers do not gain from the adaptation because the adaptation is attached to the child, not to the environment.`,`Equity: the educator understands their own culture, values and biases and how those shape who they notice, praise and challenge. Strong parent partnerships and shared aspirations sit at the centre.`,`Equity also means a fair curriculum and holistic development — health, social and emotional as well as academic — and it aims at social cohesion, not just individual scores.`,`Inclusion: equal rights, access and choices. The environment, curriculum and attitudes change so each child can succeed. Universal Design for Learning thinking: extra adaptations (visuals, flexible seating, captions, extra time) benefit everyone, not only the child with a label.`,`The Equality Act 2010, UNCRC 1989 and the SEND Code of Practice 0–25 all pull towards inclusion and equity, not mere presence.`],sections:[{heading:`Integration is presence; inclusion is belonging`,body:[`A child sitting at the edge of the carpet with a TA repeating the same unadapted input is integrated, not included. They are in the room; the room has not changed.`,`Integration can be a step on the way, and specialist teaching still has a place. The exam risk is treating a full-time TA as the definition of good SEND practice. If success depends on the child adapting, the model will fail children with more complex profiles and will not improve the offer for anyone else.`,`Inclusion redesigns the lesson, the space and the social climate: now-and-next boards the whole class uses, mixed seating, playground equipment that a child with a physical disability can use with friends. The adaptation is environmental, so extra support benefits everyone.`]},{heading:`Equity sits between sameness and belonging`,body:[`Equity is not 'treat everyone identically' (that is a crude version of equality as sameness) and not only 'put them in the room' (integration). It asks what this child needs in order to have a fair chance, and it asks the adult to notice their own bias — for example expecting less of a child on FSM or over-identifying SEMH in some groups.`,`Parent partnerships and shared aspirations stop professionals setting a lower bar than the family holds, or the reverse. Holistic development (health, social, emotional) stops a narrow academic curriculum being treated as the only fair offer.`,`Social cohesion is the community outcome: children who learn together, with fair access, are less likely to grow up seeing disability as 'other'. That is why inclusion is a values position as well as a teaching method.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments and non-discrimination support inclusion; failure to adapt is not excused by 'they are in the room'.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`High-quality teaching for all, then targeted support; the child should not have to earn access.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Equal rights to education and to a voice; inclusion as a rights position.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Preference for mainstream where it is compatible with needs and the efficient education of others, with the right support.`}],examMoves:[`Define all three, then contrast: who has to change — the child (integration) or the environment (inclusion) — and what equity adds (bias, partnership, fair holistic offer).`,`Use a concrete example (carpet time, trip, PE) and show how the three models would handle it differently.`,`Do not write that integration is 'illegal' — it exists; the specification wants you to know why inclusion and equity go further.`,`Phrase 'extra support benefits only those with SEN' vs 'adaptations benefit everyone' — that contrast is in the spec.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-3-q1`,topicId:`4-3`,stem:`Integration is best described as:`,options:[`Separate support and extra resources so the child can access the existing curriculum, with success depending on the child's ability to adapt`,`Changing the environment so extra adaptations benefit everyone`,`The educator examining their own biases and building parent partnerships`,`Excluding all children with SEND from mainstream`],answer:0,explanation:`Integration bolts support onto an unchanged offer. Inclusion changes the environment; equity centres bias, partnership and a fair holistic curriculum.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-3-q2`,topicId:`4-3`,stem:`A teacher puts a now-and-next board and flexible seating on every table, not only beside the autistic child, and children without SEND also settle faster. This is an example of:`,options:[`Integration, because only the child with SEN may use the board`,`Exclusion, because visuals are banned in the National Curriculum`,`A medical model that locates the problem only in the child`,`Inclusion, because the environment has changed and the adaptation benefits everyone`],answer:3,explanation:`Inclusion changes the environment; extra adaptations benefit everyone. Integration would have given the board only to the identified child.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-3-q3`,topicId:`4-3`,stem:`Why does equity require the educator to understand their own culture, values and biases?`,options:[`Because bias never affects who is identified for SEND or who is challenged`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 bans self-reflection`,`Because unnoticed bias lowers or skews expectation, partnership and the fairness of the curriculum, so some children never receive a genuinely fair offer`,`Because parents must not be involved in aspirations`],answer:2,explanation:`The specification places the educator's self-knowledge inside equity, alongside parent partnerships, a fair holistic curriculum and social cohesion.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-3-o1`,topicId:`4-3`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the differences between integration, equity and inclusion in educational provision for children and young people.`,modelAnswer:`Integration means providing separate support or extra resources so a child with SEN can access the existing curriculum. Success depends on the child's ability to adapt, and the extra support benefits only those with SEN. Equity goes further: the educator understands their own culture, values and biases, builds strong parent partnerships and shared aspirations, and offers a fair curriculum that supports holistic development (health, social and emotional as well as academic) so social cohesion can grow. Inclusion is a rights position: equal rights, access and choices, with the environment changed so each child can succeed, and with extra adaptations designed to benefit everyone. The same child at carpet time is integrated if a TA repeats an unadapted input beside them, and included if the input, seating and visuals change for the whole class.`,markScheme:[`Integration: extra support to access existing curriculum / child adapts / benefits only SEN (2)`,`Equity: educator bias, parent partnership, fair holistic curriculum, social cohesion (2)`,`Inclusion: equal rights and access; environment changes; adaptations benefit everyone (2)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`4-4`,code:`4.4`,elementId:`e4`,title:`Using respectful, accurate language about SEND`,page:54,notes:{gist:`Words teach children who they are. Appropriate terminology complies with policy and guidance, avoids stereotyping and labelling, shows value and respect, and is a mark of professionalism. 'The SEND child', 'suffers from', 'is confined to', 'low ability' and playground diagnoses are not professional language. Needs can be named clearly ('a child with a hearing impairment', 'a young person with an EHCP') without reducing the person to the need.`,mustKnow:[`Compliance with policies and guidance: Equality Act 2010, SEND Code of Practice 0–25, setting equality and SEND policies, and professional standards all expect respectful, accurate language. Inspection and complaints notice staffroom talk as well as reports.`,`Avoids stereotyping and labelling: one child with autism is not all children with autism; 'those families' and 'the naughty group' set a master status that hides strengths and other needs (see 3.12).`,`Value and respect: person-first or identity-first language as the child and family prefer; identity-first ('autistic person') is preferred by many autistic people — ask, do not assume. Never use outdated slurs or medicalised pity.`,`Professionalism: reports, emails, EHCP contributions and conversations with parents are legal documents and relationships. Casual labels undermine coproduction and can be discriminatory.`,`Accuracy is part of appropriateness: 'learning difficulty' is not interchangeable with 'disability'; 'behaviour' is not a diagnosis; do not name a condition you are not qualified to diagnose.`,`Language shapes provision: if the file says 'can't cope in mainstream', adults stop adapting the environment. Enabling language ('needs a quieter start', 'uses AAC') points to the adjustment.`,`UNCRC Article 12: the child has a voice in how they are described as they get older. Using their preferred terms is part of empowerment.`],sections:[{heading:`What appropriate terminology looks like`,body:[`Name the child, then the need if the need is relevant: 'Aisha uses a walker and a symbol board', not 'the disabled one'. Avoid 'suffers from', 'normal children' as a contrast, and jokes about diagnoses.`,`Stereotypes hide diversity within SEND: some children with Down's syndrome will learn to read fluently; some children without a diagnosis will not. Terminology that implies a single story ('they're all loving', 'they'll never…') is both inaccurate and disrespectful.`,`Written language matters as much as spoken. An EHCP contribution, a learning journal and a handover note that say 'refuses to try' set up the next adult to see defiance rather than an unmet communication or sensory need.`]},{heading:`Why it is a professional, not cosmetic, duty`,body:[`Compliance: discriminatory language can be harassment under the Equality Act 2010. Settings' codes of conduct and KCSIE-related staff behaviour expectations apply to how children are spoken about, including on social media.`,`Partnership: parents who hear their child reduced to a label at the door will not share the information the team needs. Coproduction under the Children and Families Act 2014 starts with respect.`,`The child's self-concept is listening. Repeated deficit language becomes the story they tell about themselves, which then affects independence, motivation and outcomes (3.12). Professionalism is choosing words that tell a truer, more hopeful, still honest story.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Harassment and discrimination can include language that violates dignity or creates a hostile environment.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Focus on outcomes and the child's strengths and needs, not on deficit labels.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Dignity, identity and the child's voice in matters that affect them, including how they are described.`}],examMoves:[`Give the four specification reasons: compliance, avoids stereotyping/labelling, value and respect, professionalism.`,`Offer a rewritten phrase (from 'the SEND kid' to 'a child with… / a child who uses…').`,`Link to 3.12 (labelling) and 4.9 (enabling language / social model) if the question opens that door.`,`Do not spend the answer on a debate about person-first vs identity-first without applying it to respect and preference.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-4-q1`,topicId:`4-4`,stem:`Which statement best explains why appropriate terminology matters when discussing SEND?`,options:[`It is only about sounding clever in an interview`,`It means never naming a need, even in an EHCP`,`It replaces the need for reasonable adjustments`,`It complies with policy and guidance, avoids stereotyping and labelling, shows value and respect, and is part of professionalism`],answer:3,explanation:`The specification lists compliance, avoiding labels/stereotypes, value and respect, and professionalism. Needs can still be named accurately; language does not replace adjustments.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-4-q2`,topicId:`4-4`,stem:`A TA writes in a learning journal: 'The autistic one kicked off again — typical.' Why is this inappropriate?`,options:[`Because it labels and stereotypes, reduces the child to a diagnosis, lacks respect, and is unprofessional in a document parents and other agencies may read`,`Because journals are not allowed to record behaviour`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 bans all mention of autism`,`Because only doctors may write in journals`],answer:0,explanation:`The sentence uses a master-status label, a stereotype ('typical') and casual deficit language in a professional record. Autism can be named respectfully; this is not that.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-4-q3`,topicId:`4-4`,stem:`Why can deficit language in files change a child's outcomes, not just their feelings?`,options:[`Because words have no link to adult expectation`,`Because the next teacher or practitioner reads the label, expects less, adapts less, and the child lives down to the story — language shapes provision`,`Because the UNCRC requires deficit language`,`Because EHCPs must be written only in slang`],answer:1,explanation:`Terminology is not cosmetic: it sets expectation and therefore the offer. That is why professionalism and the SEND Code of Practice focus on needs and outcomes, not stigma.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-4-o1`,topicId:`4-4`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Give two reasons why the words practitioners choose matter when they talk about a learner's SEND.`,modelAnswer:`Appropriate terminology complies with the Equality Act 2010, the SEND Code of Practice and setting policies, and it is a mark of professionalism in reports, meetings and daily talk. It also avoids stereotyping and labelling, which shows value and respect and stops a child being reduced to a diagnosis. Language such as 'a child who uses AAC' points to the adjustment needed, whereas 'the SEND one' lowers expectation and can damage partnership with families and the child's self-concept.`,markScheme:[`First valid reason (compliance / professionalism) (1)`,`Develops with named guidance or example (1)`,`Second distinct reason (avoid labels / value and respect) (1)`,`Develops the second reason (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-5`,code:`4.5`,elementId:`e4`,title:`How a primary disability can affect social, emotional and physical development`,page:54,notes:{gist:`A primary disability is the main identified need; it can still ripple into other domains. Social and emotional development may be affected through impulse control, language development, and mood and emotion. Physical development may be affected through attention and concentration, memory, sensory processing and motor control. Effects are potential, not certain: the environment, relationships and adjustments (the social model) change how far a disability becomes a barrier.`,mustKnow:[`Social/emotional — impulse control: some neurological and developmental disabilities make stopping, waiting and thinking before acting harder, which then affects friendships and behaviour systems that expect instant compliance.`,`Social/emotional — language development: hearing impairment, DLD, autism and some learning disabilities can delay or change how language is used to share feelings, negotiate play and repair conflict.`,`Social/emotional — mood and emotion: chronic pain, frustration at not being understood, fatigue, and other people's attitudes can raise anxiety, low mood or emotional outbursts. These are effects, not 'naughtiness'.`,`Physical — attention and concentration: sensory load, pain, medication, fatigue and some cognitive profiles shorten the time a child can use a lesson. The primary disability is not laziness.`,`Physical — memory: working memory and long-term memory can be affected by a range of neurological, learning and health conditions, which then hits sequences, instructions and learning from experience.`,`Physical — sensory processing: over- or under-responsiveness to sound, touch, light, movement or interoception changes how the child uses the room, clothes, food and play.`,`Physical — motor control: fine and gross motor difficulties affect writing, dressing, PE, feeding and independence, which then loops back into social confidence.`,`Do not assume one disability produces one personality. Two children with the same primary diagnosis can present very differently depending on support, pain, sleep and whether peers include them.`],sections:[{heading:`Social and emotional ripples`,body:[`If language is hard, so is friendship. A child who cannot find the words to join play may snatch, withdraw or become the adult's 'helper' instead of a peer. Impulse control difficulties are often punished as defiance unless adults teach the skill and adapt waiting times.`,`Mood and emotion are shaped by the disability and by other people's responses. Repeated failure, pain, or being left out of a trip will dysregulate a child who might have coped in an adapted environment. Mental health is a developmental domain, not an add-on.`,`Attachments can be strained if care is painful (medical procedures) or if adults rotate. A key person and predictable intimate care protect emotional development.`]},{heading:`Physical ripples, and why 'potential' matters`,body:[`Attention, memory, sensory processing and motor control are listed as physical-development effects because they live in the body and brain. A child who cannot sit still may be seeking sensory input; a child who 'forgets' the routine may have a working-memory load that five-part instructions have exceeded.`,`Motor control difficulties do not only affect PE. They affect drawing, using AAC devices, speed of eating, and whether the child can keep up with coat-and-bag routines that gate the next activity.`,`Potential is the AO3 word. A primary disability does not automatically flatten social, emotional or physical development if inclusion, AAC, therapy, rest, and peer education are in place. Locating every difficulty in the child (medical model) misses the barriers the setting can remove.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Duty to anticipate and adjust so a primary disability does not become substantial disadvantage in social, emotional or physical access.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Broad areas of need interact; provision should address the whole profile, not a single label.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`EHCPs can specify education, health and care together because development is not siloed.`}],examMoves:[`Use the specification lists: social/emotional = impulse control, language, mood/emotion; physical = attention, memory, sensory processing, motor control.`,`Apply to a named primary disability in the scenario (cerebral palsy, autism, hearing impairment) without inventing a single story for all children with that label.`,`Say 'may' and then name an adjustment — effects are potential.`,`Show a loop: motor difficulty → left out of play → mood and friendships suffer.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-5-q1`,topicId:`4-5`,stem:`Which set of effects belongs under social and emotional development?`,options:[`Attention, memory, sensory processing and motor control`,`Only broken bones`,`Impulse control, language development, and mood and emotion`,`GCSE option choices in Year 9`],answer:2,explanation:`Social/emotional: impulse control, language, mood and emotion. Physical: attention, memory, sensory processing, motor control.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-5-q2`,topicId:`4-5`,stem:`A child with a primary motor disability is often last to finish getting ready and is then left out of playground games. How might this affect social and emotional development?`,options:[`Missed play and repeated public struggle can affect mood, confidence and peer relationships, so physical effects ripple into social and emotional development`,`It cannot affect mood or friendships because motor needs are only physical`,`The child should be kept in at every playtime to hide the difficulty`,`Language development is legally unaffected by exclusion from play`],answer:0,explanation:`The specification wants interaction between domains. Motor control is physical; the consequence for mood and relationships is social-emotional. Exclusion is not an adjustment.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-5-q3`,topicId:`4-5`,stem:`Why does the specification describe these as potential effects rather than inevitable ones?`,options:[`Because disability never affects development`,`Because the environment, relationships and reasonable adjustments change how far a primary disability becomes a barrier — two children with the same diagnosis can develop very differently`,`Because practitioners are not allowed to notice effects`,`Because the medical model is the only lawful model`],answer:1,explanation:`Potential leaves room for the social model and for individual difference. Denying any effect, or treating effects as fixed fate, both miss the point.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-5-o1`,topicId:`4-5`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a primary disability may affect a child's social and emotional development and their physical development.`,modelAnswer:`Socially and emotionally, a primary disability may affect impulse control, so waiting and turn-taking are harder and friendships come under strain. It may also affect language development, which makes it harder to express feelings and join play, and mood and emotion, especially if the child is frustrated, in pain or left out. Physically, attention and concentration, memory, sensory processing and motor control may all be affected, so the child tires faster, misses instructions, finds the sensory environment overwhelming, or struggles with writing, PE and self-care. These effects are potential: key-person support, AAC, sensory adjustments and motor-friendly routines can reduce the barrier. Practitioners should plan for the ripple, not treat each domain as separate.`,markScheme:[`Impulse control and/or language linked to social-emotional development (2)`,`Mood and emotion linked to disability or others' responses (1)`,`Attention/memory (1)`,`Sensory processing and/or motor control (1)`,`Effects are potential / adjustment can reduce the barrier (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-6`,code:`4.6`,elementId:`e4`,title:`Thinking skills that learners need to make good progress`,page:54,notes:{gist:`Cognition is the set of mental processes children use to take in, hold, make sense of and use information. The specification skills are attention, short- and long-term memory, perception, logic and reasoning, auditory processing and visual processing. If any one of these is weak — because of developmental stage, SEND, fatigue, language or the way the lesson is taught — educational development stalls even when the child is trying. Good teaching makes these skills visible and supports them; it does not assume they are already in place.`,mustKnow:[`Attention: the ability to select and sustain focus, and to shift it when needed. Without attention, nothing else in the list can start. ADHD, anxiety, sensory overload, hunger and a dull or overloaded lesson all disrupt it.`,`Short-term / working memory: holding and manipulating information in the moment (a three-part instruction, a mental calculation). Long-term memory: storing and retrieving knowledge and skills over time so learning builds.`,`Perception: making meaning from sensory input — recognising a phoneme, a numeral, a facial expression, depth on the stairs. Perception is not the same as 20/20 vision or typical hearing; the brain has to interpret the signal.`,`Logic and reasoning: seeing patterns, cause and effect, 'if… then…', problem-solving. Needed from block play through to GCSE science. Some learning disabilities and some language profiles slow this; good scaffolding (concrete → pictorial → abstract) supports it.`,`Auditory processing: making sense of what is heard (not only whether the ears work). A child may have typical hearing tests and still miss instruction in a noisy classroom.`,`Visual processing: making sense of what is seen — tracking print, discriminating shapes, organising a worksheet, judging space in PE. Distinct from visual acuity.`,`These skills interact. Poor auditory processing looks like inattention; weak working memory looks like non-compliance with instructions; weak visual processing looks like 'careless' writing. Assess before you sanction.`],sections:[{heading:`What each skill does in a real session`,body:[`Carpet input asks for attention plus auditory processing plus working memory. A child who calls out, fiddles or cannot repeat the instruction may have a cognitive bottleneck, not a behaviour plan problem.`,`Reading and writing load visual processing, long-term memory (letter-sound knowledge), working memory (holding a sentence) and attention. Maths adds logic and reasoning and, for word problems, language.`,`Play and outdoor learning still use the full set: perceiving depth on a climbing frame, reasoning how to make a den stand up, attending long enough to finish a game. Cognition is not only 'table work'.`]},{heading:`Supporting the skills rather than assuming them`,body:[`Reduce load: one instruction at a time, visual alongside spoken, quiet space, movement breaks, extra time, concrete resources. That is high-quality teaching in the SEND Code of Practice, not a favour.`,`Teach the skill: attention games, memory supports (rehearsal, retrieval practice), sorting and matching for perception, talk-aloud for reasoning. Metacognition (Element 2) helps children notice which skill they are using.`,`Multi-agency view: an educational psychologist, SALT or occupational therapist may assess processing and memory when classroom strategies are not enough. Early identification prevents years of 'not trying'.`]}],examMoves:[`Name the six skills. Examiners look for the specification list, not a vague 'thinking skills'.`,`Apply two skills to the activity in the scenario (phonics, PE, a recipe, a science investigation).`,`Show interaction: what looks like behaviour may be memory or processing.`,`Finish with a teaching adjustment, not only a definition.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-6-q1`,topicId:`4-6`,stem:`Which list gives the six cognitive skills?`,options:[`Handwriting speed, shoe-tying and lining up`,`Only IQ as a single number`,`Perception; attention; memory (short- and long-term); logical reasoning; processing sounds; processing visual information`,`Prevent, Protect and Prepare`],answer:2,explanation:`Six thinking skills to know: paying attention, remembering (short- and long-term memory), perceiving, reasoning logically, processing what you hear and processing what you see.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-6-q2`,topicId:`4-6`,stem:`A child with typical hearing tests still misses three-part instructions in a noisy classroom but copes in a quiet room with a visual list. Which skills are most clearly involved?`,options:[`Only fine motor control`,`Long-term memory of last year's topics only`,`A refusal that cannot have a cognitive explanation`,`Auditory processing, attention and working memory, which are overloaded by noise and spoken length`],answer:3,explanation:`Typical hearing is not the same as auditory processing. Noise and long spoken sequences also load attention and working memory. The quiet visual list is the adjustment that shows the mechanism.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-6-q3`,topicId:`4-6`,stem:`Why is it professionally risky to treat weak working memory only as 'not listening'?`,options:[`Because working memory is unrelated to following instructions`,`Because the child may have attended but cannot hold and use the steps; a sanction will not install the skill, whereas chunked instructions and visuals will`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 bans instructions`,`Because only secondary pupils use memory`],answer:1,explanation:`Cognitive skills explain some 'behaviour'. Support the skill; do not only punish the symptom. Working memory is in use from the early years up.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-6-o1`,topicId:`4-6`,marks:4,command:`Outline`,stem:`Outline two cognitive skills necessary for effective educational development and why each matters.`,modelAnswer:`Attention matters because the child must select and sustain focus before they can take in teaching; without it, memory and reasoning never start. Short- and long-term memory matter because the child must hold instructions in the moment and store learning over time so new knowledge can build on prior knowledge. (Other valid skills: perception, logic and reasoning, auditory processing, visual processing — each linked to a classroom demand such as reading, problem-solving or following talk.) Practitioners support these skills by reducing load and by teaching them, not by assuming they are already in place.`,markScheme:[`Names a specification cognitive skill (1)`,`Explains why it is needed for learning (1)`,`Names a second distinct specification skill (1)`,`Explains the second (1)`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`4-7`,code:`4.7`,elementId:`e4`,title:`How disability and cognitive impairment affect language, communication, function and emotions`,page:54,notes:{gist:`Disabilities and cognitive impairments can change how a child understands and uses language, how they function day to day, and how they feel. The specification influences are: comprehension of reading, writing and maths; vocabulary and communication; attention span; coordination; logical reasoning; memory and building on prior knowledge; perception; motor skills and mobility. Each academic or functional hit can then feed anxiety, frustration or withdrawal. Influence is not destiny: AAC, scaffolding and environmental change reduce the impact.`,mustKnow:[`Comprehension of reading, writing and maths: decoding, comprehension, recording and number sense all load language, memory, visual processing and reasoning. Impairment in any of those skills changes access to the curriculum.`,`Vocabulary and communication: a smaller or differently organised vocabulary, delayed expressive language, or unclear speech affects learning and friendships. Communication includes non-verbal and AAC, not only talk.`,`Attention span: shorter or more fluctuating attention reduces how much teaching sticks and how long the child can stay in play or a task.`,`Coordination: planning and executing movement (dyspraxia / DCD and other profiles) affects writing, dressing, PE and using classroom tools.`,`Logical reasoning: difficulty with sequences, inference and 'if… then…' hits problem-solving, behaviour choices and maths.`,`Memory and building on prior knowledge: if yesterday's learning cannot be retrieved, every lesson starts at zero. That is exhausting and emotionally flattening.`,`Perception: mis-seeing or mis-hearing the room, the print or the social cue (a facial expression) changes function and relationships.`,`Motor skills and mobility: getting to the lesson, sitting, writing, accessing the toilet and joining play. Function is independence in the real building, not only test scores.`],sections:[{heading:`Language, communication and the curriculum`,body:[`Reading, writing and maths are language-heavy. A child with DLD, a hearing impairment, or a learning disability may have the idea and not the sentence, or the calculation and not the word problem. Assessment must separate 'does not know' from 'cannot show it in this format'.`,`Vocabulary and communication difficulties isolate. Peers move fast; adults over-question; the child stops offering ideas. AAC, extra time, and talking partners are functional supports, not extras.`,`Attention, memory and reasoning sit underneath comprehension. A child who cannot hold a sentence in working memory will look as if they cannot read for meaning. Teach the cognitive skill and adapt the task.`]},{heading:`Function, mobility and emotional development`,body:[`Coordination, motor skills and mobility decide whether the child can keep up with the hidden curriculum: coat, bag, lining up, getting to the next room, sitting on the carpet, using the toilets that happen to be upstairs. Fatigue from this work leaves less for learning.`,`Perception and mobility also affect safety and confidence outdoors. A child who cannot judge depth or who uses a walker needs the environment changed (inclusion), not a constant 'be careful' that shrinks their world.`,`Emotional development: repeated public struggle, not being understood, and being left out produce frustration, anxiety and sometimes 'behaviour'. Name the influence, adapt, and keep expectation. The Equality Act 2010 requires that these influences are not allowed to become substantial disadvantage.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments so impairments do not substantially disadvantage curriculum access, communication or mobility.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Cognition and learning, communication and interaction, sensory and/or physical, SEMH — needs overlap; provision should too.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`EHCP provision can include therapies and equipment that support function and communication.`}],examMoves:[`Pick three specification influences and apply them to language/communication, function (daily skills) and emotional development — the title has all three.`,`Do not write a medical essay. Stay with educational impact and what the practitioner does.`,`Show a chain: memory difficulty → cannot build on prior knowledge → falls behind → anxiety.`,`Mention AAC or environmental change so the answer is not deficit-only.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-7-q1`,topicId:`4-7`,stem:`Which of these is one way a disability or cognitive impairment can affect a learner?`,options:[`Guaranteed giftedness in one area`,`Automatic exclusion from PE`,`Comprehension of reading, writing and maths; vocabulary and communication; attention span; coordination; logical reasoning; memory; perception; motor skills and mobility`,`The end of all emotional development`],answer:2,explanation:`The specification lists those educational and functional influences. Giftedness is not guaranteed; exclusion is not a requirement; emotional development continues, though it may be affected.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-7-q2`,topicId:`4-7`,stem:`A child with a cognitive impairment understands a science idea in practical work but cannot read the worksheet or hold a three-step write-up in mind, and has started to refuse the subject. The influence is mainly on:`,options:[`Mobility only`,`Comprehension of reading and writing, memory, and then emotional development (frustration and avoidance)`,`A lack of curiosity that cannot be linked to impairment`,`Coordination of the left ankle only`],answer:1,explanation:`Practical understanding shows the idea is there; reading, writing and memory are the barriers; refusal is the emotional consequence. That is the specification chain.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-7-q3`,topicId:`4-7`,stem:`Why must function (motor skills, mobility, coordination) be considered alongside language when planning for a child with a disability?`,options:[`Because getting to the lesson, sitting, writing and joining play are part of educational access; ignoring them leaves the child too tired or excluded to use any language support`,`Because the National Curriculum is only about running`,`Because language never interacts with movement`,`Because the Equality Act 2010 only covers speech`],answer:0,explanation:`Function is daily access. The Equality Act covers disability broadly, including mobility. Language support fails if the child cannot be in the room or on the page.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-7-o1`,topicId:`4-7`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how disabilities or cognitive impairments may influence a child's language and communication, daily function, and emotional development.`,modelAnswer:`Language and communication may be affected through vocabulary, comprehension of reading, writing and maths, and through attention, memory and reasoning, so the child cannot take in or show learning in the usual format. Daily function may be affected through coordination, motor skills, mobility and perception, so dressing, writing, moving between rooms and joining PE take more effort and time. Those academic and functional barriers then influence emotional development: frustration at not being understood, anxiety about looking 'slow', and withdrawal from tasks or friends. Practitioners should adapt input (visuals, AAC, chunked steps), the physical environment and time, so the impairment does not become substantial disadvantage under the Equality Act 2010. Influence is real but can be reduced.`,markScheme:[`Language/communication influence (vocabulary, comprehension, attention, memory, reasoning) (2)`,`Function influence (coordination, motor, mobility, perception) (2)`,`Emotional influence (frustration, anxiety, withdrawal) (1)`,`Adjustment / effects can be reduced (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-8`,code:`4.8`,elementId:`e4`,title:`How long-term health conditions can affect children and young people`,page:55,notes:{gist:`A chronic condition is a long-term health need — for example asthma, diabetes, epilepsy, eczema, sickle cell disease, cancer treatment, juvenile arthritis, ME/CFS — that may be stable, fluctuating or progressive. Impact is wider than medical appointments. The specification domains are emotional state, education access and attainment, behavioural changes, quality of life, mental health and social development. Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) requires an individual healthcare plan so the condition is planned for, not improvised.`,mustKnow:[`Emotional state: worry about symptoms, needles, seizures or being 'different'; embarrassment about medication or toilets; grief at lost activities. Feelings often fluctuate with the condition.`,`Education access and attainment: missed sessions for appointments, fatigue, pain, hospital stays, and exclusion from PE, trips or the full day. Gaps compound. Home tuition or a time-limited reduced timetable may be needed; unofficial exclusion is not.`,`Behavioural changes: irritability when in pain or when blood glucose is low; withdrawal; risk-taking in adolescence (skipping medication). Behaviour is often the condition talking.`,`Quality of life: sleep, food, play, independence and the ordinary pleasures of childhood. A good plan protects quality of life, not only oxygen saturations.`,`Mental health: anxiety, depression, medical trauma, identity questions ('will I always be the ill one?'). Refer; do not minimise as 'they're used to it'.`,`Social development: missing the informal curriculum of friendship, being left out of sleepovers and sport, peer curiosity that becomes bullying. Peers may need accurate, consented information.`,`Staff duties: individual healthcare plan, trained staff, medicines policy, equality adjustments, partnership with health and family. Equality Act 2010 treats many chronic conditions as disabilities.`],sections:[{heading:`Access, attainment and the school day`,body:[`A child who is always at clinic on phonics day, or who cannot complete a full afternoon because of fatigue, will fall behind unless catch-up is planned and the curriculum is adapted. Attainment impact is often the setting's organisation, not the child's ability.`,`Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) is statutory for maintained schools and academies: children should be properly supported so they have full access, and they should not be penalised for their medical needs (for example attendance codes, trips, exams).`,`Alternative provision and hospital schools exist for some treatment periods. The child's own setting remains responsible for a coherent plan, including reintegration.`]},{heading:`Emotional, social and quality-of-life impact`,body:[`Chronic conditions steal spontaneity. A child who cannot join the puddle walk, the birthday pizza, or the residential without a risk assessment and a trained adult needs those things planned in, not quietly dropped.`,`Social development suffers when peers are not helped to include, or when the child is defined by the condition. Confidentiality still applies: share on a need-to-know basis with the family's agreement unless safeguarding requires otherwise.`,`Mental health and emotional state are not vanity extras. Pain, steroids, sleep disruption and fear of a seizure in assembly are cognitive events — they use up the attention and memory 4.6 described. Holistic support (key adult, counselling pathways, flexible demand) is educational provision.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions`,year:`2015`,point:`Statutory guidance: individual healthcare plans, trained staff, full access, no penalty for medical needs.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Many chronic conditions meet the definition of disability; reasonable adjustments are required.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Medicines, health and wellbeing duties for early years providers; partnership with parents.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`EHCPs can include health provision where education is affected; joint commissioning.`}],examMoves:[`Use at least three specification domains (emotional state, access/attainment, behaviour, quality of life, mental health, social development).`,`Name Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) and the individual healthcare plan.`,`Apply to the named condition in the scenario without graphic medical detail.`,`AO3: impact fluctuates; good planning and inclusion change quality of life as much as medicine does.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-8-q1`,topicId:`4-8`,stem:`Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) requires schools to:`,options:[`Send every child with a chronic condition home until they are cured`,`Share every medical detail in the school newsletter`,`Replace first aiders with parents on site all day`,`Have arrangements, including individual healthcare plans where appropriate, so children are properly supported and can access education`],answer:3,explanation:`The 2015 guidance is about support and access, not exclusion or public disclosure. IHPs and trained staff are the mechanism.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-8-q2`,topicId:`4-8`,stem:`A child with a fluctuating chronic condition has missed two residentials, is irritable in the afternoons, and has stopped joining clubs. Which specification impacts are most clearly shown?`,options:[`Only spelling age`,`A legal requirement to remove them from roll`,`Education access, behavioural changes, quality of life and social development`,`Proof that chronic conditions never affect mental health`],answer:2,explanation:`Missed residentials = access and quality of life; afternoon irritability = behavioural change linked to fatigue/pain; clubs = social development. Mental health may also be affected; removal from roll is not the response.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-8-q3`,topicId:`4-8`,stem:`Why is unofficially reducing a child's hours because 'it is easier' an inadequate response to a chronic condition?`,options:[`Because reduced timetables are always illegal in every circumstance`,`Because it can cut education access and social development without a reviewed plan, and may amount to unlawful discrimination if it is the default instead of adjustments and an IHP`,`Because the EYFS bans all part-time attendance in Reception`,`Because parents must never be consulted`],answer:1,explanation:`Time-limited, reviewed reduced timetables can be part of a plan. A quiet, indefinite cut in hours to suit the setting is not support and risks discrimination.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-8-o1`,topicId:`4-8`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain three ways in which a chronic condition may impact on a child or young person in an education or early years setting.`,modelAnswer:`Education access and attainment may fall because of missed sessions, fatigue, pain or being left out of PE and trips if an individual healthcare plan under Supporting pupils at school with medical conditions (2015) is not in place. Emotional state and mental health may be affected by worry, embarrassment about medication, or medical trauma, which then reduces attention and belonging. Social development and quality of life suffer when the child misses the informal curriculum of friendship and play, and behaviour may change (irritability, withdrawal) when they are unwell. Practitioners should plan adjustments, keep the child included, work with family and health, and not treat the condition as a reason to lower aspiration.`,markScheme:[`Access / attainment with a because (2)`,`Emotional state or mental health with a because (2)`,`Behaviour / quality of life / social development with a because (2)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-9`,code:`4.9`,elementId:`e4`,title:`Medical and social models of disability, and removing barriers so learners feel valued`,page:55,notes:{gist:`The medical model locates disability in the child's impairment or difference: the child is the problem to be treated or fixed. The social model locates disability in the environment: the child is disabled by stairs, attitudes, inaccessible communication and rigid curriculum, not by the impairment alone. Removing barriers — accessible secure environments, value and respect, involving the individual in planning learning and health care, context and relevance, enabling language, work with family and professionals, implementing setting policies — is how settings empower and value children. UK law (Equality Act 2010) is closer to the social model.`,mustKnow:[`Social model: disabled by the environment rather than by the impairment. Change the environment and attitudes, and the same child can participate.`,`Medical model: disabled by impairment or differences. The response is diagnosis, therapy and the child adapting to an unchanged world. Diagnosis still matters for health; it must not be the whole educational story.`,`Remove barriers — accessible, secure environment: ramps, acoustics, lighting, quiet space, accessible toilets, safe storage of equipment, fire evacuation plans that include every child.`,`Promote value and respect; enabling language: see 4.4. Talk about barriers and adjustments, not 'what's wrong with them'.`,`Involve the individual in planning learning and health care: voice, choice, coproduction (UNCRC Article 12; Children and Families Act 2014). Even young children can show what they like, find hard, and want next.`,`Context and relevance: tasks that connect to the child's life, interests and culture so learning is meaningful, not a test of sitting still.`,`Work with family and professionals; implement setting policies: SEND, equality, accessibility, medicines, IHP, EHCP. A model that stays in an essay but never changes the doorway is not practice.`],sections:[{heading:`Two models, one child`,body:[`Medical-model thinking: 'He can't join the trip because he uses a wheelchair.' Social-model thinking: 'The coach and the museum are the barrier; book accessible transport and a venue that already works.' The impairment has not changed; the plan has.`,`The medical model is not 'bad' in hospital. Epilepsy needs medicine; a hearing impairment may need audiology. The educational error is using only that model, so the child becomes a diagnosis and the setting never changes.`,`Mike Oliver and the disabled people's movement in the UK are the usual AO3 names for the social model. The Equality Act 2010's reasonable-adjustments duty is social-model law: the setting must change.`]},{heading:`How barriers are actually removed`,body:[`Environment and policy: accessibility plan, fire-evacuation buddy, height-adjustable tables, AAC always charged and within reach, medicines policy that does not send the child home at the first symptom.`,`Relationships and language: peers are taught to include; staff use enabling language; the child is in the planning meeting, not talked over. Value and respect are visible in whose work is displayed and who represents the setting.`,`Curriculum: context and relevance, plus involving the child in targets, so they experience competence. Work with family and the multi-agency team (4.2) so health care and learning plans do not fight each other. Implementation is the test — laminated values are not barrier removal.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Mike Oliver`,idea:`Social model of disability: people are disabled by barriers in society and the environment, not by impairment alone.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments and anticipatory duty — social-model law in practice.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Remove barriers through high-quality teaching and targeted support; coproduce.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Voice (Article 12) and non-discrimination (Article 2) when planning learning and care.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Young person and parent involvement in EHC assessment and planning.`}],examMoves:[`Define both models in a sentence each, then apply to the scenario's barrier (stairs, language, attitude, worksheet).`,`Use the specification removal list — environment, value/respect, involve the individual, context, enabling language, family/professionals, implement policies.`,`Do not dump the medical model as useless; say when diagnosis and treatment matter, and why they are not enough in education.`,`Name the Equality Act 2010 as the legal hook for removing barriers.`]},quiz:[{id:`4-9-q1`,topicId:`4-9`,stem:`Which statement correctly contrasts the two models?`,options:[`The social model says people are disabled by the environment; the medical model says they are disabled by impairment or difference`,`The medical model only applies to buildings; the social model only applies to medicine`,`Both models ban reasonable adjustments`,`The social model forbids any health treatment`],answer:0,explanation:`That is the specification contrast. The social model does not ban healthcare; the medical model is not a buildings policy.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-9-q2`,topicId:`4-9`,stem:`A child who uses a wheelchair is told they cannot go on the farm trip because 'it is not for children like you'. A social-model response is to:`,options:[`Agree that the impairment makes trips impossible`,`Write a medical report instead of booking a ramp-access vehicle`,`Keep the child in school to colour in a farm picture every year`,`Treat the inaccessible transport and the adult's attitude as the barriers, involve the child and family in planning, and change the arrangement so they can go`],answer:3,explanation:`Social model + specification barrier-removal: environment, attitudes, involving the individual, implementing equality/accessibility policy. Exclusion is medical-model practice dressed up as care.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-9-q3`,topicId:`4-9`,stem:`Why is involving the child in planning learning and health care a way of empowering them?`,options:[`Because children must write their own prescriptions`,`Because parents are legally banned from meetings`,`Because voice and choice (UNCRC Article 12; coproduction) treat them as experts on their own lives, which is the opposite of a medical model that does to them`,`Because Ofsted only interviews children who are silent`],answer:2,explanation:`Empowerment is the child's participation in decisions. It does not replace clinical roles or family partnership.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-9-o1`,topicId:`4-9`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Compare the medical and social models of disability, and give two ways a setting can remove barriers so disabled learners feel valued and in control.`,modelAnswer:`The medical model locates disability in the child's impairment or difference, so the child is expected to be treated or to adapt to an unchanged setting. The social model locates disability in the environment and attitudes, so the child is disabled by barriers rather than by the impairment alone. Barriers are removed by creating an accessible, secure environment (ramps, quiet space, accessible communication) and by using enabling language that shows value and respect. They are also removed by involving the child in planning learning and health care, making tasks relevant, working with family and professionals, and actually implementing SEND, equality and accessibility policies. The Equality Act 2010 requires reasonable adjustments, which is social-model practice. Diagnosis and healthcare still matter; they are not the whole educational response.`,markScheme:[`Medical model defined (impairment as the disabling factor) (1)`,`Social model defined (environment/attitudes as the disabling factor) (1)`,`First barrier-removal strategy (1)`,`Develops how it empowers or values (1)`,`Second distinct strategy (1)`,`Develops the second / names Equality Act or coproduction (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`4-10`,code:`4.10`,elementId:`e4`,title:`AAC: ways to add to or replace speech, and how to use them`,page:55,notes:{gist:`AAC is every way of communicating that supplements or replaces spoken words. It is not a last resort and it does not stop speech developing — for many children it supports language. The specification groups it as no-tech, low-tech and high-tech. Application means matching the method to the child, the context and the communication partner, keeping the system available all day, and treating it as the child's voice, not a toy.`,mustKnow:[`No-tech: body language, pointing, objects of reference, vocalisations, signing, and the communication partner (who interprets, waits, and offers choices). No equipment beyond the body and everyday objects.`,`Low-tech: pen and paper, pictures, photographs, symbols (including PECS-style exchange, communication books, choice boards). Not powered; portable; needs consistent filing and updating.`,`High-tech: mobile devices, laptops, tablets, speech-generating devices (speech synthesis), eye-tracking systems. Powered, often individualised, need charging, programming and a plan for breakdown.`,`AAC can be unaided (signing, gesture) or aided (everything that uses an object or device). Many children use a mix across the day.`,`Application: model the system as you talk; wait; honour every message; do not remove AAC as a sanction; have a low-tech backup for high-tech; train every adult including lunchtime staff and cover.`,`Matching: a child with physical disability may need eye-tracking or switch access; a child with autism may use symbols and signing; a child with unclear speech may use a tablet to repair misunderstanding. SALT leads assessment; education uses it all day.`,`Rights: communication is a UNCRC and Equality Act issue. Withholding a child's AAC is like taking someone else's voice.`],sections:[{heading:`No-tech, low-tech, high-tech — know the examples`,body:[`No-tech is easy to underestimate. Objects of reference (a nappy meaning 'toilet', a cup meaning 'drink') give meaning before pictures. Signing (Makaton, BSL) and a tuned-in communication partner who offers two real choices are AAC. Vocalisations are communication if adults respond rather than hush them.`,`Low-tech is the workhorse: a symbol strip for now-and-next, a photograph of the child's nan, a whiteboard to write a word the child cannot say. It fails when symbols are out of date, left on a high shelf, or only used at 'communication time'.`,`High-tech includes a mainstream tablet with a symbol app, a dedicated speech-generating device, and eye-tracking for children who cannot point or speak. High-tech fails when it is in a cupboard charging during snack — the moment the child needed to say 'I don't want that' — or when only one TA knows the password.`]},{heading:`Application in early years and school`,body:[`Use AAC across the curriculum and care routines, not only in SALT sessions. The communication partner's job is to pause, model, and not over-guess so the child never needs to communicate.`,`Combine levels: a child with a tablet still needs signing in the pool and a laminated choice board when the battery dies. Fire drills and trips need a planned system.`,`Family and multi-agency consistency matters. If nursery uses one symbol set and home another, load increases. Involve the child in choosing vocabulary that is relevant (4.9 context) — favourite people, protest words, jokes — not only 'please' and colour names.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`AAC can be a reasonable adjustment so a disabled child is not substantially disadvantaged in communication.`},{name:`SEND Code of Practice 0–25`,point:`Communication and interaction needs; settings must enable children to express themselves.`},{name:`UNCRC`,year:`1989`,point:`Article 12 (voice) and Article 13 (freedom of expression) apply whether or not the child uses speech.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`EHCPs can specify AAC equipment and training as educational provision.`}],examMoves:[`Give at least one example under each heading: no-tech, low-tech, high-tech. That is the easy AO1.`,`Application is AO2: who uses it, when, communication partner, backup, not using it as a reward.`,`State that AAC supplements or replaces speech and does not 'make them lazy'.`,`Link to inclusion (environment changes so everyone can communicate) and to enabling language (4.4 / 4.9).`]},quiz:[{id:`4-10-q1`,topicId:`4-10`,stem:`Which example is correctly grouped?`,options:[`Eye-tracking is no-tech because it uses the body`,`Pen and paper are high-tech because they use tools`,`Signing, pointing, objects of reference and a communication partner are no-tech; symbols and photographs are low-tech; tablets, speech synthesis and eye-tracking are high-tech`,`Vocalisations are not communication`],answer:2,explanation:`No-tech = body, objects of reference, vocalisations, signing, partner. Low-tech = pen and paper, pictures, photographs, symbols. High-tech = powered devices including speech synthesis and eye-tracking.`,ao:`AO1`},{id:`4-10-q2`,topicId:`4-10`,stem:`A child who uses a speech-generating tablet is about to go swimming. The most appropriate application of AAC is to:`,options:[`Leave the tablet in the classroom because water is messy, and manage without any system`,`Take the unsecured tablet into the water`,`Remove AAC for the term as a behaviour sanction`,`Plan a no-tech or low-tech backup (signs, laminated symbols) and a communication partner in the pool, then return the high-tech device afterwards`],answer:3,explanation:`Application means matching the system to the context and always having a way to communicate. High-tech is not used in the pool; no-tech/low-tech is. AAC is never a sanction.`,ao:`AO2`},{id:`4-10-q3`,topicId:`4-10`,stem:`Why is it inaccurate to delay AAC until a child 'has tried harder to talk'?`,options:[`Because AAC supplements or replaces speech and often supports language development; withholding it denies a voice and can be a failure to make a reasonable adjustment`,`Because AAC is only for adults`,`Because the UNCRC bans speech`,`Because high-tech devices must be used before any signing`],answer:0,explanation:`AAC is not a last resort and does not prevent speech. No-tech methods can start immediately. Equality and rights arguments support early use.`,ao:`AO3`}],open:[{id:`4-10-o1`,topicId:`4-10`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the three types of AAC and how one of them might be applied to support a child or young person in an educational setting.`,modelAnswer:`No-tech AAC uses the body and everyday objects: body language, pointing, objects of reference, vocalisations, signing and a communication partner who waits and offers choices. Low-tech AAC uses non-powered tools such as pen and paper, pictures, photographs and symbols on choice boards or in a communication book. High-tech AAC uses powered systems — mobile devices, laptops, tablets, speech synthesis and eye-tracking. Application must match the child and the context: for example a child with unclear speech might use a tablet with speech synthesis in class, while staff model the language, keep the device charged and within reach, never remove it as a sanction, and have a low-tech symbol board as backup for the playground or a battery failure. AAC supplements or replaces speech; it is the child's voice and a reasonable adjustment under the Equality Act 2010.`,markScheme:[`No-tech defined with an example (1)`,`Low-tech defined with an example (1)`,`High-tech defined with an example (1)`,`Applied use matched to a child or context (1)`,`Communication partner / available all day / not a sanction / backup (1)`,`Purpose: supplement or replace speech / rights or reasonable adjustment (1)`],ao:`AO2`}]}];function a(e,t,n,r,i,a,o=`AO1`){return{id:e,topicId:t,stem:n,options:r,answer:i,explanation:a,ao:o}}var o=[{id:`5-1`,code:`5.1`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Typical cognitive, physical, social and emotional development from birth to 19`,page:56,notes:{gist:`Typical development from birth to 19 is holistic: cognitive, physical, and social and emotional learning interact, and the rate varies even when the sequence is similar. Practitioners use expected patterns to set realistic expectations, identify delay, and plan the next step — they do not treat a missed month as failure.`,mustKnow:[`Cognitive development includes information processing (attention, encoding, storage, retrieval), memory (sensory, working and long-term), problem solving, and sensory perception.`,`Physical development includes gross motor skills (large, whole-body movements such as rolling, walking, running and throwing) and fine motor skills (smaller, precise movements such as grasping, cutting and writing).`,`Puberty typically begins around 8–13 in girls and 9–14 in boys: growth spurt, primary and secondary sexual characteristics, skin and sweat changes, later sleep onset, and body-image pressure.`,`Social and emotional development includes bonding and attachments, expressing feelings, following instructions, co-operation, self-control/self-regulation, self-concept, and establishing friendships and peer groups.`,`Infancy (0–2): from reflexes to walking; palmar then pincer grasp; object permanence; first words; attachment to a key person.`,`Early childhood (2–7): running, climbing, drawing then letter formation; symbolic play; vocabulary explosion; turn-taking taught through play.`,`Middle childhood (7–11): team games and joined handwriting; more logical thought; best friendships; growing capacity to delay gratification.`,`Adolescence (11–19): puberty and identity; abstract thought becomes available; peer intensity; prefrontal cortex still maturing, so impulse control is a work in progress.`,`Sequences are typical ranges, not fixed dates. Delay in one domain (for example language) often ripples into others (behaviour, friendships, self-concept).`],sections:[{heading:`Cognitive: processing, memory, problem solving and perception`,body:[`Information processing speeds up with myelination and practice: younger children need shorter inputs, visuals and repetition; older children can hold more in working memory and use strategies such as rehearsal and organisation.`,`Memory is not one skill. Infants recognise familiar faces and routines; preschoolers recall events with adult prompting; school-age children start to use memory strategies deliberately; adolescents can plan revision but still need teaching, sleep and reduced cognitive load.`,`Problem solving moves from trial and error (a toddler stacking cups) to more systematic checking (a Year 4 pupil testing a science idea) to hypothetical reasoning (a sixth-former weighing options). Sensory perception becomes more selective: a baby is flooded by noise and light; a secondary pupil should be able to filter a busy classroom, but SEND or anxiety can keep the load high.`]},{heading:`Physical: gross motor, fine motor and puberty`,body:[`Gross motor: head control, rolling, sitting, crawling or bottom-shuffling, walking, running, hopping, catching, then stamina for team sport. Fine motor: palmar grasp to pincer, stacking, scribbling, snipping, dressing, handwriting and tool use.`,`Physical skill underpins independence and status. A Reception child who cannot yet hop needs PE adapted, not labelled lazy. A Year 8 pupil whose fine motor skill is weak may avoid written work that looks 'babyish'.`,`Puberty is a physical, not only a social, change. Growth, sexual characteristics, tiredness and a biological shift towards later sleep change daytime behaviour. Early or late puberty can affect self-esteem and peer standing. RSHE and sensitive PE changing-room practice matter.`]},{heading:`Social and emotional learning from bonding to peer groups`,body:[`Bonding and attachment in infancy create the secure base from which children explore. Expressing feelings starts as crying and movement and becomes named emotion with adult co-regulation.`,`Following instructions and co-operation depend on language, working memory and trust in the adult. Self-control and self-regulation are taught, not assumed: toddlers snatch; primary pupils can wait with a timer; adolescents still need routines when tired or in a peer group.`,`Self-concept becomes more comparative in middle childhood ('I am good at football, not spelling') and identity work intensifies in adolescence. Friendships move from proximity playmates to loyal best friends to intimate, identity-shaping peer groups.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Jean Piaget`,idea:`Cognitive stages as a map, not a prison: sensorimotor (0–2), pre-operational (2–7), concrete operational (7–11), formal operational (11+).`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Learning is social; adults and more skilled peers scaffold within the zone of proximal development.`},{name:`Erik Erikson`,idea:`Psychosocial crises across the lifespan, including trust vs mistrust in infancy, industry vs inferiority in middle childhood, and identity vs role confusion in adolescence.`},{name:`Atkinson and Shiffrin`,idea:`Multi-store model of memory (sensory, short-term/working, long-term) used to explain why attention and rehearsal matter in the classroom.`}],examMoves:[`Name the domain (cognitive, physical, or social-emotional) and the age band before you give an example.`,`Use a typical sequence then say how the practitioner adapts (shorten the carpet; teach the pincer; coach turn-taking).`,`Puberty is physical development — do not write only about mood or 'hormones' without the bodily changes.`,`Holistic answers score: a language delay can look like 'behaviour' because the child cannot follow a three-part instruction.`]},quiz:[a(`5-1-q1`,`5-1`,`Which pair best matches gross motor and fine motor development?`,[`Gross motor is handwriting; fine motor is running.`,`Gross motor is large whole-body movement; fine motor is smaller, precise movement.`,`Gross motor only occurs after puberty; fine motor only occurs in infancy.`,`Gross motor is sensory perception; fine motor is memory.`],1,`The specification defines gross motor as large whole-body skills and fine motor as smaller precise skills.`),a(`5-1-q2`,`5-1`,`A Year 8 pupil is late most mornings, irritable on the first lesson, and self-conscious in PE changing rooms. Which typical development best explains this pattern?`,[`Loss of all working memory at the start of Key Stage 3.`,`The end of attachment needs at age 11.`,`Completion of Piaget's sensorimotor stage.`,`Physical changes of puberty, including a shift towards later sleep and heightened body awareness.`],3,`Adolescent sleep shift and body-image awareness are typical physical/social effects of puberty, not a sign that attachment or memory has vanished.`,`AO2`),a(`5-1-q3`,`5-1`,`Why is it professionally risky to treat typical toddler snatching as 'defiance' in the same way as a planned Year 10 refusal?`,[`Impulse control, language and self-regulation are still emerging at toddler stage, so the first response is to teach the skill, not only to punish.`,`Toddlers never need boundaries.`,`Year 10 pupils cannot co-operate.`,`The EYFS forbids any adult intervention.`],0,`Stage informs the response: toddlers lack the neural and language equipment for long sanctions; older pupils have more capacity and still need teaching.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-1-o1`,topicId:`5-1`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how cognitive, physical and social-emotional development typically change from early childhood to adolescence, and why practitioners must use this knowledge.`,modelAnswer:`Cognitively, early childhood is marked by growing language, symbolic play and limited working memory, so children need short, concrete, scaffolded tasks; by adolescence abstract and hypothetical thought is available but still needs teaching, sleep and manageable load. Physically, early childhood refines running, climbing and the fine motor skills for drawing and early writing, whereas adolescence brings puberty, a growth spurt, later sleep and body-image pressure that affect PE, attendance and concentration. Socially and emotionally, young children are learning to name feelings, take turns and follow simple instructions with adult co-regulation; adolescents are forming identity and intense peer groups while self-regulation is still developing. Practitioners use these typical patterns to set age-appropriate expectations, adapt provision (movement breaks, later start conversations, RSHE, key adult) and to notice when development is unexpected so that support can be planned.`,markScheme:[`Cognitive change with an age contrast (up to 2).`,`Physical change, including puberty for adolescence (up to 2).`,`Social-emotional change (feelings, co-operation, self-regulation, friendships/identity) (up to 2).`,`Credit application: why the practitioner adapts expectations or provision.`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-2`,code:`5.2`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Attachment theory and how it helps friendships and relationships grow`,page:56,notes:{gist:`Attachment is the lasting emotional bond a child forms with a caregiver who is a secure base. Bowlby, Ainsworth, and Schaffer and Emerson explain why sensitive, responsive care — not simply hours spent — shapes later friendships, trust and how children use adults in a setting. The EYFS key-person role is attachment theory put into daily practice.`,mustKnow:[`Bowlby: attachment is innate; infants are biologically primed to form a bond with a primary figure; that internal working model influences later relationships.`,`Bowlby’s maternal deprivation hypothesis: prolonged separation from the mother/primary carer in the early years can harm emotional development — later research shows quality of substitute care and later relationships also matter.`,`Ainsworth’s Strange Situation classified infant responses to a carer leaving and returning: secure; insecure avoidant; insecure ambivalent (resistant).`,`Disorganised-disoriented attachment was identified later (Main and Solomon): contradictory, freeze or fear behaviours often linked with frightened or frightening care.`,`Schaffer and Emerson: babies attach to the people who respond most sensitively, not automatically to whoever spends the most time with them.`,`Schaffer and Emerson stages: asocial (0–6 weeks); indiscriminate (6 weeks–6 months); specific (7 months+, with separation anxiety and fear of strangers); multiple (from about 10–11 months: grandparents, friends, childminders, nursery).`,`Secure attachment supports exploration, emotion regulation and later friendships; insecure patterns can look like clinginess, apparent independence, or push–pull with peers and staff.`,`In settings, a named key person, predictable greetings and goodbyes, and working with families put attachment theory into practice.`],sections:[{heading:`Bowlby: innate bonds and later relationships`,body:[`Bowlby argued that infants seek proximity to a primary attachment figure for survival. The child builds an internal working model of 'are people reliable?' and 'am I worthy of care?'. That model is carried into friendships, teacher relationships and, later, intimate relationships.`,`Maternal deprivation is not the same as short, well-supported nursery days. Harm is associated with prolonged, unmitigated loss or grossly inadequate care. High-quality key-person care and a second attachment figure can protect development.`,`Monotropy (one primary figure) does not mean only mothers, or that other bonds do not count. Fathers, grandparents, foster carers and key persons can be primary or secondary attachment figures.`]},{heading:`Ainsworth: the Strange Situation types`,body:[`Secure: the child is distressed by separation but is comforted on reunion and returns to play — they use the adult as a secure base. In a setting this looks like a child who protests at drop-off, settles with the key person, and can then explore.`,`Insecure avoidant: little overt distress, limited contact on return; the child has learned that bids for comfort are unwelcome. They may look 'easy' and play alone, then struggle to use help or to trust friends.`,`Insecure ambivalent/resistant: intense distress, difficulty settling, mix of seeking and resisting contact. In class this can look like clinginess, jealousy of peers, or anger when the adult attends to someone else.`,`Disorganised-disoriented: no coherent strategy — approaching then freezing, or fear of the carer. This is a safeguarding alert as well as a developmental one; the child needs highly predictable, non-frightening adults.`]},{heading:`Schaffer and Emerson: stages and sensitive care`,body:[`Asocial (0–6 weeks): babies respond similarly to people and objects. Indiscriminate (6 weeks–6 months): they enjoy people generally. Specific (from about 7 months): one or more preferred figures, with separation anxiety and stranger wariness. Multiple (from about 10–11 months): additional attachments to grandparents, childminders, nursery staff and family friends.`,`The Glasgow babies study showed that the most sensitive, responsive adult — the one who answers cues promptly and accurately — became the attachment figure even if they were not the person present for the most hours. That is why a nursery key person who is attuned can become a genuine attachment figure, and why an unresponsive parent who is 'always there' may not.`,`Multiple attachments support friendships: a child who has learned that several people can be safe is more able to trust peers, recover from conflict and use a range of adults in school.`]}],theorists:[{name:`John Bowlby`,idea:`Innate attachment to a primary figure; internal working models; maternal deprivation as a risk to later relationships.`},{name:`Mary Ainsworth`,idea:`Strange Situation: secure, insecure avoidant and insecure ambivalent attachment types.`},{name:`Main and Solomon`,idea:`Identified the later disorganised-disoriented classification from contradictory reunion behaviour.`},{name:`Schaffer and Emerson`,idea:`Attachments form with the most sensitive, responsive carers; stages from asocial to multiple attachments.`}],examMoves:[`Name the theorist with the concept: Bowlby = innate/deprivation/internal working model; Ainsworth = Strange Situation types; Schaffer and Emerson = stages and sensitive care.`,`Do not write 'Bowlby said mothers must stay at home'. The exam wants sensitive primary care and later relationship effects.`,`Apply types to behaviour in the setting (clingy, 'too independent', freeze) then say what the key person should do.`,`Disorganised attachment is later than Ainsworth’s original three — say so if you name it.`]},quiz:[a(`5-2-q1`,`5-2`,`According to Schaffer and Emerson, attachments are most likely to form with:`,[`Whoever spends the most hours in the same room, regardless of responsiveness.`,`Carers who respond most sensitively to the baby's cues.`,`Only biological mothers.`,`Any adult who provides food.`],1,`Schaffer and Emerson found quality of sensitive responsiveness predicted attachment more than quantity of time.`),a(`5-2-q2`,`5-2`,`In the Strange Situation a child is very distressed when the carer leaves, is not easily comforted on return, and both seeks and resists contact. This is most typical of:`,[`Secure attachment.`,`Insecure avoidant attachment.`,`Insecure ambivalent (resistant) attachment.`,`Asocial stage (0–6 weeks).`],2,`Ambivalent/resistant attachment is high distress plus difficulty using the carer as a calm base. Avoidant children show little overt need; secure children settle and play.`,`AO2`),a(`5-2-q3`,`5-2`,`Why does a secure attachment in the early years support later friendships?`,[`Securely attached children never disagree with peers.`,`Bowlby proved that friendships are genetically fixed at birth.`,`Multiple attachments prevent all peer conflict.`,`The internal working model that people are reliable and the self is worthy makes it easier to trust, repair conflict and use others as a safe base.`],3,`Bowlby's internal working model is the AO3 link from early bonds to later relationships. Secure does not mean conflict-free.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-2-o1`,topicId:`5-2`,marks:6,command:`Discuss`,stem:`Discuss how the key concepts of Bowlby, Ainsworth, and Schaffer and Emerson help practitioners support the development of friendships and relationships.`,scenario:`Noah, aged 14 months, cries at drop-off, settles with his key person, then plays near other children. A new bank staff member tries to take him straight to the sand tray without greeting him.`,modelAnswer:`Bowlby’s idea of an innate bond and an internal working model means Noah needs a reliable figure before he can explore or join others; skipping the greeting threatens that model. Ainsworth’s secure pattern fits a child who protests, is comforted and then plays — the key person should greet, hold, then scaffold play alongside peers rather than force instant independence. Schaffer and Emerson show that at this age Noah is likely in the specific and emerging multiple-attachment stages (separation anxiety is typical) and that he will attach to the most sensitive adult, so a consistent key person and attuned bank staff matter more than hours on a rota. Practitioners support later friendships by providing that secure base, co-regulating goodbye, and then creating low-pressure parallel play so peer relationships grow from safety rather than from being left to 'get on with it'.`,markScheme:[`Bowlby applied (innate bond / internal working model / later relationships) (up to 2).`,`Ainsworth type recognised and used (up to 2).`,`Schaffer and Emerson stages and/or sensitive care applied to age/key person (up to 2).`,`Credit a link from secure base to friendships/peer play.`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`5-3`,code:`5.3`,elementId:`e5`,title:`How friendships and relationships support wellbeing`,page:57,notes:{gist:`Warm, reciprocal friendships and relationships are a protective factor, not a luxury. They support mental health, resilience, social skills, self-esteem and confidence, and a more positive self-concept. Isolation and chronic conflict do the reverse, so practitioners plan for belonging as deliberately as they plan for phonics.`,mustKnow:[`Positive friendships and relationships support mental health: belonging reduces anxiety and low mood; someone to talk to buffers stress.`,`Resilience is strengthened when a child has at least one trusted person and a peer who helps them recover after a setback.`,`Social skills (turn-taking, reading cues, repairing conflict, joining a group) are rehearsed in real friendships, not only in adult-led circle time.`,`Self-esteem and confidence grow when peers choose you, laugh with you and notice what you can do.`,`Self-concept ('I am likeable / I am a good friend / I belong in this class') is built from repeated social evidence.`,`A single trusted adult relationship (key person, tutor, mentor) can offset a difficult peer week; both adult and peer relationships matter.`,`The same mechanisms can harm: exclusion, bullying and controlling friendships damage the same wellbeing factors — exam answers can show this balance.`],sections:[{heading:`Mental health, resilience and social skills`,body:[`A child with a playmate at playtime has a reason to come in; attendance and mood often lift together. For adolescents, a trusted friend is frequently the first person they tell about worry, identity or home stress — a protective factor if adults also remain available.`,`Resilience is not grit in isolation. It is being able to try again because someone sits with you after the fall. Group games, shared projects and restorative conversations after a falling-out all build that recovery muscle.`,`Social skills need an audience. Turn-taking, waiting, compromising and reading facial expression are practised in pairs and small groups. Children with SEND or EAL may need the unwritten rules taught explicitly so they can access those wellbeing benefits.`]},{heading:`Self-esteem, confidence and self-concept`,body:[`Being chosen for a game, having a birthday invitation or being asked to help a peer is data for self-esteem. Repeated social success ('they wanted me') is more powerful than a sticker from an adult alone.`,`Confidence to speak, try a new club or walk into the dinner hall often follows a friendship, not the other way round. Practitioners who only coach 'be more confident' without structuring buddy systems miss the mechanism.`,`Self-concept in middle childhood becomes comparative. Friendships that value effort, kindness and shared interests protect a child whose academic self-image is shaky. Cliques that value appearance or 'banter' can distort self-concept quickly.`]}],theorists:[{name:`John Bowlby`,idea:`Early attachment relationships provide the template for later friendships; a secure base supports exploration towards peers.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Social learning: children copy the social behaviours they see rewarded in friendship groups; self-efficacy grows through successful social experiences.`},{name:`Robert Selman`,idea:`Friendship understanding deepens with age from momentary playmates to intimate, interdependent relationships — wellbeing effects intensify as friendship becomes about trust.`}],examMoves:[`Name one wellbeing factor (such as resilience, self-esteem, self-concept, social skills or mental health), then explain how the friendship affects it.`,`Apply to age: a toddler’s parallel playmate is not the same as a Year 10 confidant, but both can protect wellbeing.`,`Stretch: friendships are protective and they can be the vehicle for bullying — show you can weigh both if the command is discuss/evaluate.`]},quiz:[a(`5-3-q1`,`5-3`,`Which wellbeing factor is most directly strengthened when a child has a peer who helps them try again after failing a task?`,[`Handwriting speed.`,`Fine motor control.`,`Chronological age.`,`Resilience.`],3,`Resilience is the capacity to recover from setbacks; a supportive relationship is a classic protective mechanism.`),a(`5-3-q2`,`5-3`,`A quiet Year 5 pupil joins a lunchtime board-games club, makes two friends, and starts contributing in class. The most accurate explanation is that friendships have:`,[`Replaced the need for any adult relationship.`,`Improved social skills, belonging and self-esteem, which in turn support participation and mental health.`,`Guaranteed they will never be bullied.`,`Completed Piaget's formal operational stage.`],1,`The spec link is friendships → social skills, self-esteem, mental health → more confident participation. Adult relationships still matter.`,`AO2`),a(`5-3-q3`,`5-3`,`Why might a practitioner argue that planning for friendship is as much a wellbeing intervention as a social activity?`,[`Ofsted inspects the number of best friends each child has.`,`Friendship is entertainment only and has no developmental function.`,`Positive relationships are a mechanism for mental health, resilience, social skills, self-esteem and self-concept, so isolation is a welfare issue, not a personality trait.`,`Children legally require three named friends.`],2,`AO3 is the causal chain from relationships to wellbeing factors, and the professional implication that isolation needs planning.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-3-o1`,topicId:`5-3`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two ways in which friendships and relationships can positively impact a child or young person's wellbeing.`,modelAnswer:`Friendships support mental health because belonging and having someone to talk to reduce anxiety and low mood, so the child is more likely to attend and engage. They also build self-esteem and a more positive self-concept: being chosen, included and valued by peers provides evidence that 'I am likeable', which increases confidence to try new learning and social situations.`,markScheme:[`1 mark per wellbeing factor named, up to 2 (for example self-esteem or confidence, resilience, mental health, social skills, self-concept).`,`1 mark for each developed mechanism of how friendship/relationship produces that impact (max 2).`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`5-4`,code:`5.4`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Receptive and expressive language, and what affects learning to talk`,page:57,notes:{gist:`Receptive language is understanding what is said or signed; expressive language is using vocabulary and sentences to convey meaning. They do not develop at the same pace — many children understand more than they can say. Age and stage, personality, cognition, bilingualism, culture, SEND, the learning environment and available support all affect acquisition.`,mustKnow:[`Receptive language: comprehend and understand what is said (or signed/seen) — following instructions, answering questions, enjoying stories.`,`Expressive language: choosing words and building sentences to get meaning across — asking for things, commenting, telling stories, making an argument.`,`A gap is common: a toddler may follow 'get your shoes' (receptive) before they can say the sentence (expressive). Do not assume a quiet child understands nothing, or that a talkative child understands everything.`,`Age and stage: babbling, first words, two-word combinations, then increasingly complex grammar; adolescents still add subject-specific vocabulary.`,`Personality: a confident extravert may practise speech more; a shy child may have strong receptive language that is hidden.`,`Cognition: attention, memory and conceptual understanding underpin both comprehension and sentence-building.`,`Bilingualism is not a delay. Children may mix languages (code-switching) and show uneven profiles; the home language is an asset.`,`Cultural background shapes who talks to children, narrative styles, and whether children are expected to speak up to adults.`,`SEND (for example DLD, hearing impairment, autism, learning disability) can affect one or both channels; AAC may be needed.`,`Learning environment and available support: serve-and-return talk, reading, wait time, visual support, SALT, EAL strategies and family involvement accelerate acquisition; noise, rushing and 'quiz' talk slow it.`],sections:[{heading:`Receptive versus expressive in practice`,body:[`Check receptive language by asking the child to show, fetch or point — not only to talk. A child who cannot answer 'what did you do at the weekend?' may still understand a two-part instruction with a visual.`,`Support expressive language with choices ('water or milk?'), commenting rather than testing, expanding the child's utterance ('car' → 'yes, the red car is going fast'), and time to formulate a sentence.`,`Behaviour is often language. A child who pushes to get a bike may not yet have the expressive sentence 'my turn please'. Teach the words at the moment of need, then practise them in calm play.`]},{heading:`Factors that affect acquisition`,body:[`Age and stage set the ceiling for complexity, but experience sets the rate. A language-rich home and setting produce more vocabulary than age alone.`,`Personality and cognition interact: a child with ADHD may miss the instruction (receptive) even with a large vocabulary (expressive). A child with a learning disability may need concepts teaching before the words stick.`,`Bilingualism and culture: assess in all languages where possible; never tell families to drop the home language. Silence in the silent/receptive period of EAL is typical, not a disorder.`,`SEND and support: hearing checks, SALT programmes, visual timetables, Makaton or other AAC, and a calm acoustic environment are professional responses, not optional extras. The Equality Act 2010 expects reasonable adjustments.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Noam Chomsky`,idea:`Language Acquisition Device — humans are biologically prepared to acquire grammar; environment still supplies the language to acquire.`},{name:`B. F. Skinner`,idea:`Behaviourist view: language is learned through imitation and reinforcement — useful for explaining why modelling and expanding utterances work, incomplete as a full theory.`},{name:`Jerome Bruner`,idea:`Language Acquisition Support System — caregivers scaffold language through routines, joint attention and formats such as peekaboo and shared books.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Language and thought develop through social interaction; private speech supports problem solving.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments for disability-related communication needs, including hearing impairment and language disorders.`}],examMoves:[`Define both terms in a short sentence before you apply them — many candidates mix them up.`,`If the scenario is a quiet EAL child, say receptive may be ahead of expressive and that bilingualism is not SEND.`,`Name a factor from the spec list and the mechanism (for example cognition: working memory limits following a three-part instruction).`]},quiz:[a(`5-4-q1`,`5-4`,`Receptive language is best defined as:`,[`Using vocabulary and forming sentences to express meaning.`,`Only the ability to write.`,`Comprehending and understanding what is said or signed.`,`A delay caused automatically by bilingualism.`],2,`Receptive = understand. Expressive = produce. Bilingualism is a factor, not a definition of receptive language.`),a(`5-4-q2`,`5-4`,`A three-year-old follows 'coat on, then bag' but answers most questions with one word. This most clearly shows:`,[`No language at all.`,`Receptive language currently stronger than expressive language, which is typical at this stage.`,`Expressive language ahead of receptive language.`,`That the family must stop speaking a home language.`],1,`Understanding a two-part instruction with limited spoken output is a classic receptive > expressive profile in early childhood.`,`AO2`),a(`5-4-q3`,`5-4`,`Which statement about factors affecting language acquisition is most accurate?`,[`Personality, cognition, bilingualism, culture, SEND, environment and support can all raise or lower the rate of acquisition; bilingualism itself is not a disorder.`,`Only age matters; the environment cannot change language.`,`SEND always prevents any expressive language.`,`Cultural background is irrelevant in English settings.`],0,`The specification lists multiple factors. Treating bilingualism or culture as deficits loses AO3 marks.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-4-o1`,topicId:`5-4`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the difference between receptive and expressive language and how two factors might affect language acquisition.`,scenario:`Mei, aged 4, has recently arrived from overseas. She watches stories closely, fetches her coat when asked, but rarely speaks in English. At home she chats in Mandarin. Staff wonder if she has SEND.`,modelAnswer:`Receptive language is Mei’s ability to understand what is said — shown when she follows 'get your coat' and attends to stories. Expressive language is using vocabulary and sentences to express meaning; her limited English talk does not prove she has nothing to say. Age and stage plus bilingualism are both relevant: a silent/receptive period is typical when acquiring an additional language, and her home-language chatter shows expressive skill already exists. Cultural background and the learning environment also matter: if staff only quiz her in English, she may stay silent, whereas visuals, a buddy, continued home-language use and wait time will support acquisition. Jumping to a SEND label without considering EAL, environment and support would be inaccurate.`,markScheme:[`Clear definition of receptive language with application (up to 2).`,`Clear definition of expressive language with application (up to 2).`,`Two factors (age/stage, personality, cognition, bilingualism, culture, SEND, environment, support) explained with mechanism (up to 2).`,`Do not credit 'bilingualism causes SEND'.`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-5`,code:`5.5`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Personal, environmental and educational influences on behaviour`,page:57,notes:{gist:`Behaviour is never only 'inside' the child. Personal factors (age and stage, self-esteem, SEND, PSED), environmental factors (culture and religious beliefs, care history, family and societal influences) and educational factors (bullying and discrimination, peer relationships, relationships with practitioners, levels of expectations) stack. Changing the context is often more ethical and effective than only changing the child.`,mustKnow:[`Personal: age/stage (impulse control, language, puberty); self-esteem; SEND (including SLCN, ADHD, autism, learning disability); PSED (personal, social and emotional development).`,`Environmental: culture and religious beliefs (what counts as respectful behaviour); care history (looked-after, kinship, multiple moves); family and societal influences (poverty, conflict, media, community norms).`,`Educational: bullying and discrimination; peer relationships; the relationship with practitioners; levels of expectations (too low = boredom; too high = threat).`,`The same behaviour (withdrawal, hitting, refusal) can have different drivers — observe before you intervene.`,`Positive versions exist: secure PSED, a valued culture, a trusted adult and well-pitched work all protect behaviour.`,`An ABC approach (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) helps locate whether the driver is personal, environmental or educational.`],sections:[{heading:`Personal factors`,body:[`A two-year-old who bites is often at a typical stage of limited language and impulse control. A fourteen-year-old who does the same thing needs a different analysis (peer status, SEND, trauma, sleep) even though the behaviour looks similar.`,`Low self-esteem can look like avoidance, clowning or aggression that masks shame. SEND and gaps in PSED (naming feelings, waiting, empathy) make the hidden rules of the classroom harder, so behaviour communicates unmet need.`,`Personal factors are not excuses. They tell you which skill to teach and which adjustment to make under the Equality Act 2010.`]},{heading:`Environmental and educational factors`,body:[`Culture and faith shape eye contact, adult–child talk, food, dress and what 'defiance' looks like. Misreading a cultural norm as rudeness is an adult error. Care history (loss, neglect, many carers) can produce hypervigilance, hoarding or rejection of help.`,`Family stress, housing and community violence raise baseline arousal. Children may be 'fine' at school and collapse at home, or the reverse.`,`Educationally, bullying and discrimination make the setting itself the threat. Peer relationships can pull behaviour towards a group norm. A warm, boundaried practitioner relationship is one of the strongest protective factors. Expectations that are chronically too high or too low both generate disruption: one from threat, one from boredom.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Urie Bronfenbrenner`,idea:`Ecological systems: behaviour is shaped by nested contexts (family, setting, culture, policy), not only by the individual child.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Behaviour is learned through observing peers and adults; what is modelled and reinforced in the educational setting matters.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Disability, race, religion and other protected characteristics: behaviour responses must not discriminate; reasonable adjustments are required.`}],examMoves:[`In 6-mark analyse questions, take one factor from each heading (personal, environmental, educational) and show the mechanism.`,`Always say whether the influence is positive or negative in this scenario.`,`Do not treat family structure or faith as a problem in themselves — specify the mechanism (conflict, discrimination, mismatch of expectations).`]},quiz:[a(`5-5-q1`,`5-5`,`Which is an educational factor that can influence behaviour?`,[`A child's age and stage.`,`Levels of expectation and the quality of relationships with practitioners.`,`Family religious beliefs.`,`Care history before starting school.`],1,`Age/stage and SEND are personal; culture, faith and care history are environmental; expectations and practitioner relationships are educational.`),a(`5-5-q2`,`5-5`,`Marcus, aged 6, has a hearing impairment and pushes peers during a long phonics carpet. Which analysis is most accurate?`,[`Personal SEND plus an educational factor (length, acoustics, seating) are likely combining; changing the carpet may be more effective than only sanctioning.`,`This can only be a parenting problem.`,`Hearing impairment never affects behaviour.`,`High expectations are always harmful.`],0,`Personal (SEND/sensory load) and educational (task design) stack. That is the spec's three-factor model in action.`,`AO2`),a(`5-5-q3`,`5-5`,`Why is it professionally important to consider environmental factors such as care history rather than only the incident?`,[`Care history always excuses harm to others.`,`Ofsted forbids sanctions.`,`Environmental factors are irrelevant after age five.`,`Without the context, adults may punish a survival response (hypervigilance, hoarding, rejection of help) and miss safeguarding and attachment needs.`],3,`AO3: context changes the meaning of the behaviour and the ethical response; it does not remove the duty to keep others safe.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-5-o1`,topicId:`5-5`,marks:6,command:`Analyse`,stem:`Analyse how personal, environmental and educational factors might influence behaviour in this scenario.`,scenario:`Aisha, aged 9, has ADHD and low self-esteem. She has had three house moves this year. In class she calls out, then rips up her work when it is not immediately praised. A supply teacher has told her she is 'lazy'.`,modelAnswer:`Personally, ADHD affects impulse control and waiting, so calling out is partly a stage-and-SEND profile; low self-esteem makes any delay in praise feel like failure, so ripping work protects her from being seen as 'not good enough'. Environmentally, three house moves mean disrupted routines, possible tiredness and a care history of instability — she may not trust that adults or classrooms stay. Educationally, a supply teacher’s 'lazy' label is discrimination-adjacent and a low, shaming expectation that confirms the negative self-concept; peer laughter would amplify it. A more effective response would adjust the educational factor (shorter tasks, immediate specific feedback, a known adult, ADHD reasonable adjustments) while remaining curious about home stress, rather than only punishing the call-outs.`,markScheme:[`Personal factor with mechanism (ADHD, self-esteem, PSED, age/stage) (up to 2).`,`Environmental factor with mechanism (moves, family/care, culture) (up to 2).`,`Educational factor with mechanism (label, expectations, relationships, peers/bullying) (up to 2).`],ao:`AO3`}]},{id:`5-6`,code:`5.6`,elementId:`e5`,title:`How self-concept forms, and what shapes behaviour, thinking and social development`,page:58,notes:{gist:`Self-concept is the picture a child holds of themselves. It is built from self-image (how you see yourself), self-esteem (how you value yourself) and the ideal self (how you wish you could be). Experiences, interactions, environment, reflection and feedback all shape it — and a threatened or inflated self-concept shows in behaviour, thinking and relationships.`,mustKnow:[`Self-image: how you see yourself (appearance, roles, abilities — 'I am a big sister', 'I am bad at writing').`,`Self-esteem: how you value yourself (worth, pride, shame).`,`Ideal self: the person you would like to be. If it is far from your self-image, this can cause distress (Rogers called this incongruence).`,`Self-concept develops through experiences (success, failure, trauma, achievement), interactions (family, peers, practitioners), environment (inclusion or discrimination), reflection, and feedback (specific vs global; public vs private).`,`Positive, specific feedback and genuine success experiences strengthen self-esteem and willingness to try cognitively demanding tasks.`,`Global criticism ('you’re lazy'), comparison, bullying and exclusion shrink self-concept and can produce withdrawal or acting out.`,`Self-concept becomes more comparative in middle childhood and more identity-focused in adolescence; social media intensifies the ideal self.`],sections:[{heading:`The three parts of self-concept`,body:[`Self-image is descriptive. A child may see themselves as 'the funny one', 'the child who can't sit still', or 'a Muslim girl who wears hijab'. Labels from adults become self-image surprisingly fast.`,`Self-esteem is evaluative. Two children can share a self-image ('I find reading hard') with very different self-esteem depending on whether they still feel valued.`,`The ideal self is the target. If the ideal is realistic and adults help close the gap (scaffolding, practice), motivation rises. If the ideal is impossible (a body, a grade, a popularity) or adults only criticise the gap, anxiety and giving-up follow. Carl Rogers called a close match congruence.`]},{heading:`Influences on behaviour, cognition and social development`,body:[`Behaviour: children protect a fragile self. They may refuse the writing task, clown, blame others, or people-please. Over-confidence can be a mask: interrupting and dismissing help keeps the ideal self intact.`,`Cognition: 'I am not a maths person' becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy — less practice, more working-memory load from anxiety, poorer performance (link to Bandura’s self-efficacy and Dweck’s mindset).`,`Social development: children who believe they are unlikeable withdraw or become controlling. Inclusive environments, mixed grouping, and feedback that names effort and kindness (not only attainment) give new data for self-concept.`,`Experiences of racism, ableism, homophobia or care-identity stigma attack self-image at group level. Practitioner feedback cannot be 'neutral' if the environment is hostile.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Carl Rogers`,idea:`Self-concept as self-image, self-esteem and ideal self; congruence when they align; unconditional positive regard supports healthy self-concept.`},{name:`Charles Horton Cooley`,idea:`Looking-glass self — we see ourselves in how others respond to us.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Self-efficacy: belief in one's capacity to succeed shapes which tasks children attempt.`},{name:`Carol Dweck`,idea:`Growth versus fixed mindset: feedback about effort and strategy protects self-concept when work is hard.`}],examMoves:[`Define self-image, self-esteem and ideal self as three separate terms — do not treat self-concept as only self-esteem.`,`Name a factor from the spec (experiences, interactions, environment, reflection, feedback) and link it to behaviour or cognition.`,`Apply: quote the adult’s feedback in the scenario and show how it becomes self-image.`]},quiz:[a(`5-6-q1`,`5-6`,`In Rogers' model, self-esteem is:`,[`How you see yourself (roles and appearance).`,`How you wish you could be.`,`How you value yourself.`,`A statutory National Curriculum level.`],2,`Self-image = see; self-esteem = value; ideal self = wish to be. All three make up self-concept.`),a(`5-6-q2`,`5-6`,`A teacher writes 'you’re so lazy' on a Year 6 book. How is this most likely to influence self-concept?`,[`It is specific strategy feedback that raises self-efficacy.`,`It only affects handwriting, not self-concept.`,`It closes the gap between self-image and ideal self.`,`Global negative feedback can become self-image and lower self-esteem, reducing effort on later cognitive tasks.`],3,`Global labels are taken into self-image. Specific, task-focused feedback would have been the professional alternative.`,`AO2`),a(`5-6-q3`,`5-6`,`Why can a large gap between self-image and ideal self affect social development?`,[`Incongruence can produce shame, withdrawal or bravado, which makes authentic friendships and help-seeking harder.`,`It always produces higher attainment.`,`It is legally required in adolescence.`,`Ideal self is unrelated to peers.`],0,`Rogers' incongruence is the AO3 mechanism: the child hides the real self from peers and adults.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-6-o1`,topicId:`5-6`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how self-concept is developed and how one factor can influence a child or young person's behaviour or cognition.`,modelAnswer:`Self-concept is made up of self-image (how you see yourself), self-esteem (how you value yourself) and the ideal self (how you wish you could be), and it is built through experiences, interactions, environment, reflection and feedback. For example, repeated specific praise for strategy and genuine success experiences raise self-efficacy, so the child is more willing to attempt harder cognitive tasks; global criticism or exclusion does the reverse and can show as refusal or withdrawal.`,markScheme:[`Identifies self-image, self-esteem and/or ideal self (1–2).`,`Names a valid influence (experiences, interactions, environment, reflection, feedback) and links it to behaviour, cognition or social development (2–3).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-7`,code:`5.7`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Why learners need to adapt their behaviour to different social situations`,page:58,notes:{gist:`The same action can be right in one place and wrong in another: shouting on the pitch, not in assembly. Children and young people need to adapt so they can focus on learning, control impulses, conform to social norms where it is safe and fair to do so, and make and keep friends. Adults must teach the hidden rules, not assume they are obvious.`,mustKnow:[`Focusing on learning in educational settings: lowering voice, attending, storing a disagreement until an appropriate time.`,`Impulse control: stopping the snatch, the shout-out, the phone-out; pausing before acting.`,`Conforming to social norms: lining up, personal space, greetings, online etiquette, reading a room.`,`Making friends and maintaining relationships: matching humour, repairing after conflict, different behaviour with a best friend versus a teacher versus a visitor.`,`Context-switching is a developmental skill linked to age, language, SEND (especially autism and ADHD) and what has been explicitly taught.`,`Not every 'norm' should be obeyed: children also need to recognise unsafe or discriminatory contexts and seek help.`,`Importance: safety, inclusion, learning time, reputation with peers, and later workplace/community life.`],sections:[{heading:`Why adaptation matters`,body:[`Educational settings only work if most children can hold an impulse long enough for someone else to speak. Without that, learning time collapses and anxious children cannot think.`,`Friendships depend on reading context: teasing that is funny with a close friend is bullying with a new peer. Young people who cannot switch are at risk of isolation or of being labelled aggressive.`,`Social norms keep people safe (road, kitchen, lab, online). They also carry bias: 'be quiet and make eye contact' may clash with culture, autism or trauma. Fair practice teaches the rule and offers a reasonable adjustment, rather than shaming the child.`]},{heading:`How practitioners teach context-switching`,body:[`Name the context and the behaviour: 'Football voice outside; library voice inside.' Visual cues, social stories, now/next, and role play make hidden rules visible.`,`Rehearse impulse control in games (freeze, turn-taking, waiting for a timer) when the child is calm, not only at the point of crisis.`,`Model the switch yourself: adults who shout 'be quiet' teach that shouting is how power works. Co-regulation first, then self-regulation.`,`For SEND, teach explicitly and adjust the demand (a movement break, a fidget, a quieter entry) so the child can succeed in the context rather than being repeatedly failed by it.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Social contexts and more knowledgeable others teach the cultural tools of behaviour; what is 'appropriate' is learned, not innate.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Children imitate the behaviour that is modelled and reinforced in each setting.`}],examMoves:[`Give two contrasting contexts (pitch/library, home/school, friends/teacher) — that is the heart of this point.`,`Link to inclusion: failure to adapt may be a skill gap or SEND, not 'naughtiness'.`,`Mention impulse control and friendships if the marks allow — they are named in the spec.`]},quiz:[a(`5-7-q1`,`5-7`,`Running in the hall but running in PE is an example of:`,[`Defiance in both places.`,`The same behaviour being appropriate in one social context and not another.`,`A safeguarding disclosure.`,`Summative assessment.`],1,`Context decides whether the behaviour meets the social rule. The child needs the switch taught, not a global ban on running.`),a(`5-7-q2`,`5-7`,`A Year 10 pupil uses the same sarcastic 'banter' with a teacher that they use with close friends, and friendships are starting to fracture too. The practitioner should mainly:`,[`Teach that relationships and settings require different registers, and coach repair with peers as well as respectful challenge with adults.`,`Ignore it because sarcasm is a social norm everywhere.`,`Exclude them permanently for one comment.`,`Ban all friendships.`],0,`Adapting behaviour to maintain relationships and to meet educational norms is the specification point. Teaching the skill is the professional response.`,`AO2`),a(`5-7-q3`,`5-7`,`Why is impulse control central to adapting behaviour in educational settings?`,[`Impulse control is only relevant in sport.`,`The law requires children to have no impulses.`,`Without a pause, children cannot inhibit the first action long enough to follow the context rule, so learning and peer relationships both suffer.`,`Impulse control replaces the need for adult modelling.`],2,`Impulse control is the mechanism that makes context-switching possible. It develops with age and can be taught.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-7-o1`,topicId:`5-7`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain why it is important for children and young people to adapt their behaviour to meet different social contexts.`,modelAnswer:`Different contexts have different safety and social rules: shouting is acceptable on the football pitch and not in assembly, so adaptation protects learning time and keeps people safe. Adaptation also supports friendships and inclusion — humour, personal space and tone that work with a close friend can damage a new peer relationship or a lesson. Children who cannot yet switch, including some with SEND, need the hidden rules taught rather than only being sanctioned.`,markScheme:[`Two different contexts with different expectations (2).`,`Why it matters: learning/safety and/or friendships/inclusion/impulse control (2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-8`,code:`5.8`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Behaviour policies and procedures, and strategies for setting expectations`,page:58,notes:{gist:`A behaviour policy makes the expected standard visible for staff, children and young people, and parents/carers. Followed consistently, it is fair, safe and motivating. Strategies then turn the policy into daily practice: structure, age-appropriate boundaries, modelling, positive reinforcement, feedback, reflection, co-regulation and relationships.`,mustKnow:[`Importance: clarifies the expected standard for staff, CYP and parents/carers so everyone knows the rules of the community.`,`CYP input and ownership make rules more likely to be followed; tokenistic 'class rules' that children did not shape are weaker.`,`Expectations must be realistic for age and stage (and adjusted for SEND).`,`Fairness and a consistent approach stop children being at the mercy of adult mood and stop them testing every new adult.`,`Safety: policy covers harmful behaviour, bullying, child-on-child abuse and, where relevant, reasonable force — staff do not improvise.`,`Celebrate and reflect on success; this reinforces motivation for attainment, not only compliance.`,`Strategies: structured approach; age/stage-appropriate boundaries; modelling; positive reinforcement; formative feedback; self-reflection; mistakes as learning; co-regulation then self-regulation; positive relationships and communication; clarifying expectations ongoing; opportunities to test and stretch skills.`],sections:[{heading:`Why the policy matters`,body:[`Written policy is how a setting shows Ofsted, governors and families that behaviour is not left to chance. It should align with the EYFS or the school’s behaviour policy, KCSIE (behaviour can be a safeguarding indicator) and the Equality Act 2010 (reasonable adjustments).`,`Consistency is fairness. If one TA laughs off swearing and another issues a sanction, children learn that rules are about adult personality. New and supply staff need an induction into the actual procedures (scripts, recording, when to call the DSL).`,`Ownership: children who help draft playground rules understand them and will remind peers. Parents who have had the policy explained at induction are partners, not opponents, when a difficult conversation comes.`,`Motivation: catching children getting it right, reviewing success in assemblies or key-person time, and linking behaviour to learning goals ('we can do science when equipment is safe') beats a list of punishments.`]},{heading:`Strategies that set and encourage expectations`,body:[`Structure: visual timetables, predictable transitions, seating, calm corners. Chaos manufactures behaviour incidents.`,`Boundaries that match the stage: a two-year-old needs distraction and a short 'stop, it hurts'; a Year 9 needs a clear consequence they have already heard about, plus a chance to repair.`,`Modelling and positive reinforcement: adults use the tone they want back; specific praise and earned privileges beat vague 'good boy'. Formative feedback tells the child what to do next time.`,`Mistakes as learning, self-reflection, and co-regulation: after a rupture, the adult lends their calm, then helps the child name the trigger and plan a different action. Relationships and ongoing clarification of expectations make stretch possible — children will take a supported risk in a skill (a speaking part, a match, a debate) if they trust the adult response to error.`]}],theorists:[{name:`B. F. Skinner`,idea:`Operant conditioning: behaviour followed by reinforcement is more likely to be repeated — the basis of positive reinforcement strategies.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Social learning: children copy modelled behaviour; staff conduct is part of the behaviour policy in action.`}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Behaviour policy and sanctions must not discriminate; reasonable adjustments for disability are required.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,point:`Behaviour, bullying and child-on-child abuse are safeguarding issues; policy must join up with the DSL.`},{name:`Education and Inspections Act`,year:`2006`,point:`School staff have a statutory power to discipline pupils; policy should set out how that power is used fairly.`}],examMoves:[`Split the answer: why policy (consistency, fairness, safety, ownership, motivation) then which strategies.`,`Always mention age/stage and SEND adjustments or you miss a specification bullet.`,`Apply: what happens if one adult ignores the policy this afternoon?`]},quiz:[a(`5-8-q1`,`5-8`,`A core reason for all staff to follow the same behaviour policy is that it:`,[`Saves writing lesson plans.`,`Makes the expected standard consistent and fair for staff, children and families, and safer to apply.`,`Removes the need for professional judgement in safeguarding.`,`Means parents are not allowed to know the rules.`],1,`The specification stresses a clarified standard, fairness, consistency and safety — not secrecy from parents.`),a(`5-8-q2`,`5-8`,`Which strategy best matches 'mistakes as learning' after a Reception child snatches a bike?`,[`A week of exclusion with no explanation.`,`Public humiliation so others learn.`,`Co-regulate, give the language for 'my turn', practise waiting with a timer, and later reflect on what to do next time.`,`Ignore the other child's distress so natural consequences teach both children.`],2,`Age-appropriate boundary plus teaching the skill, reflection and co-regulation are named strategies. Exclusion and humiliation are not.`,`AO2`),a(`5-8-q3`,`5-8`,`Why should children and young people have input into behaviour expectations?`,[`It removes adult duty of care.`,`It is a statutory KS4 examination.`,`It replaces the written policy and Equality Act duties.`,`Ownership and understanding make rules more likely to be followed and keep expectations realistic for age and stage.`],3,`The spec lists CYP input and ownership. Adults still hold safeguarding and legal duties.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-8-o1`,topicId:`5-8`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain why settings need a behaviour policy and procedures, and describe two strategies that help learners understand and meet expectations.`,modelAnswer:`Policies clarify the expected standard for staff, children and parents so responses are consistent, fair and safe rather than dependent on adult mood; they also allow children a voice, keep expectations realistic for age and stage, and create space to celebrate success so motivation for learning is reinforced. One strategy is modelling combined with positive reinforcement: adults use the tone and language they want back and specifically praise the behaviour they want repeated. A second is co-regulation moving towards self-regulation: a calm adult lends their regulation, then uses formative feedback and reflection so the child can name the trigger and try a better strategy, with boundaries matched to age and SEND.`,markScheme:[`Importance of policy: consistency/fairness/safety/ownership/age-stage/motivation (up to 3).`,`Two named strategies from the spec with a brief how (up to 3).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-9`,code:`5.9`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Using what you know about home, family and care to support behaviour`,page:59,notes:{gist:`Home, family and care information is not gossip and it is not an excuse. Used properly, it helps practitioners work with parents to find support, share with colleagues for multi-agency or early intervention, plan transitions, write a behaviour plan that actually fits the child, and set and monitor individual behaviour targets.`,mustKnow:[`Work with parents/carers to find support and advice (sleep, food, routines, parenting programmes, Early Help).`,`Share with colleagues on a need-to-know basis so multi-agency working and early intervention can start (SENCO, DSL, key person, pastoral lead).`,`Support planned transitions (new class, new carer, secondary transfer) and unplanned ones (bereavement, becoming looked after, sudden house move).`,`Inform a behaviour management plan: triggers at collection, hunger on Mondays, hypervigilance after contact sessions.`,`Set and monitor individual behaviour targets that are realistic given the child's circumstances, then review with family.`,`Care history (looked-after, kinship, adoption, special guardianship) often includes loss and change; behaviour may be communication of an internal working model, not a character flaw.`,`Confidentiality and data protection apply: share to protect and support, not in the staff-room as a story. Safeguarding overrides consent where a child is at risk.`],sections:[{heading:`What the information is for`,body:[`A child who hoards snack after a weekend of inconsistent meals needs food and a plan, not a lecture on greed. A child who deteriorates on Fridays before contact with a birth parent needs a key adult and a quieter afternoon, not a reputation as 'always worse at the end of the week'.`,`Parents often already hold the explanation (a new baby, night shifts, a relative dying). Partnership turns that knowledge into a shared strategy: the same language for waiting, the same sleep routine, the same target.`,`When needs exceed the setting, information enables Early Help, a TAF, or a SEND/safeguarding referral. Delay because 'we don’t like to interfere' is not respectful — it leaves the child managing adult problems alone.`]},{heading:`Plans, targets and transitions`,body:[`Behaviour plans should name known triggers from home and care (transitions between houses, noisy lunchtimes after a chaotic morning) and the adult response that works. Copying a generic 'three strikes' sheet will fail a looked-after child who experiences sanctions as further rejection.`,`Individual targets must be small, observable and reviewed: 'greet the key person and put my bag away' beats 'be good'. Monitor with the child and carer so success is shared.`,`Planned transitions: extra visits, social stories, information to the new setting. Unplanned transitions: keep ordinary routines as an island of safety, one trusted adult, honest age-appropriate information, and do not punish regression (wetting, clinginess, silence).`]}],legislation:[{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,point:`Early identification and sharing of information with families and agencies to protect welfare.`},{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Home and care information is personal data; share on a lawful, need-to-know basis. Safeguarding sharing is permitted where necessary.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Welfare is paramount; looked-after children and children in need require coordinated planning.`}],examMoves:[`List the spec uses: parental support, multi-agency/early intervention, transitions, behaviour plan, individual targets.`,`Stay non-judgemental. Analyse the mechanism (sleep, fear, loyalty), not the family's morality.`,`Mention confidentiality/need-to-know so you do not sound as if every adult should hear everything.`]},quiz:[a(`5-9-q1`,`5-9`,`Practitioners use home, family and care information mainly to:`,[`Entertain colleagues with family stories.`,`Work with parents, inform plans and targets, support transitions, and enable early intervention.`,`Lower all expectations permanently.`,`Replace safeguarding procedures.`],1,`The specification lists partnership, multi-agency/early intervention, transitions, behaviour plans and individual targets.`),a(`5-9-q2`,`5-9`,`A child in foster care hoards snack and becomes distressed at home-time after a contact session. The best use of this information is to:`,[`Sanction the hoarding as theft.`,`Tell the whole playground so peers can 'help'.`,`Ignore it so the child learns independence.`,`Adapt the behaviour plan (predictable key person, snack planned, calmer collection) and share need-to-know with the carer and social worker.`],3,`Care-history information should change the plan and the multi-agency conversation, not become gossip or a character judgement.`,`AO2`),a(`5-9-q3`,`5-9`,`Why must individual behaviour targets be set with family and care context in mind?`,[`Targets that ignore home triggers will fail and can shame a child who is already managing instability; realistic, monitored targets create shared success.`,`Parents must write all school rules.`,`Looked-after children are exempt from any targets.`,`Data protection forbids all target-setting.`],0,`AO3 links fairness and effectiveness: context-blind targets set the child up to fail.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-9-o1`,topicId:`5-9`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two ways a practitioner can use what they know about a learner's home, family or care situation to support positive behaviour.`,modelAnswer:`Practitioners can work with parents or carers to agree shared strategies and find extra support (for example Early Help with sleep or routines), so the child hears the same expectations in both places. They can also use the information to write a realistic behaviour plan and individual targets — for example planning a calm collection after contact — and share need-to-know detail with colleagues so transitions and early intervention are joined up.`,markScheme:[`1 mark for each valid use (parental support/advice; multi-agency/early intervention; planned or unplanned transitions; behaviour plan; individual targets) (max 2).`,`1 mark each for explaining how that use helps manage behaviour (max 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-10`,code:`5.10`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Verbal and non-verbal communication with children and young people`,page:59,notes:{gist:`Children read the whole message: words, tone, proximity, gesture and body language. Warm, congruent communication invites co-operation. A harsh tone, looming over a child, pointing or closed body language can trigger threat — especially for children with trauma, SEND or EAL — and the behaviour you wanted to stop gets worse.`,mustKnow:[`Verbal factors include the words chosen, volume, tone, pace, wait time and whether the language matches the child's stage.`,`Tone can turn a neutral sentence into a threat or a comfort; sarcasm is easily missed by younger children, EAL learners and some autistic pupils.`,`Proximity: getting down to the child's level versus crowding; cultural and trauma-related differences in comfortable distance.`,`Gesture: pointing, beckoning, thumbs-up, Makaton or visual cues that support understanding.`,`Body language: open versus closed posture, facial expression, eye contact (not universally comfortable), turning towards or away.`,`Congruence matters: kind words with an angry face teach children not to trust adults. Mixed messages are especially confusing for toddlers and for children with SLCN.`,`Positive practice: calm voice, child's name first, short instructions, time to process, side-by-side stance rather than toe-to-toe, smile or neutral face, gestures that clarify.`],sections:[{heading:`Verbal: words and tone`,body:[`Say the behaviour you want ('walking feet') rather than only the one you do not ('don't run'). Give one instruction at a time for younger children and for anyone with a language or attention need.`,`Tone carries more than content in conflict. A raised, sarcastic or shaming tone activates fight, flight or freeze. A low, slow, specific voice supports co-regulation.`,`Public verbal correction can humiliate and inflame peer audience effects, especially in adolescence. Private, brief, respectful language protects dignity and is more likely to change the behaviour.`]},{heading:`Non-verbal: proximity, gesture and body language`,body:[`Standing over a seated child, finger-pointing, or blocking the exit reads as threat. Crouch to the side, keep an exit visible, and use an open palm. In a crisis, extra proximity can escalate; give space if it is safe to do so.`,`Gesture and visuals (a stop hand, a visual timetable, a point to the pegs) carry meaning when language has been lost to emotion or is not the child's strongest channel.`,`Body language is modelled constantly. Adults who roll their eyes in the staff room and then smile at the door teach two curricula. Children with attachment needs scan faces first; your face is an intervention.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Albert Mehrabian`,idea:`Often cited for the weight of non-verbal cues in conveying attitude — a reminder that tone and body can override words, even if the original percentages are over-quoted.`},{name:`Carl Rogers`,idea:`Congruence: words, tone and body language need to match for the relationship to feel trustworthy.`}],examMoves:[`Name at least two of tone, proximity, gesture and body language — they are the spec list.`,`Apply: describe what the adult should do with their body, not only what they should say.`,`Link to SEND, EAL or trauma if the scenario offers it: non-verbal load is higher when language is weaker.`]},quiz:[a(`5-10-q1`,`5-10`,`Which of these is a non-verbal communication factor?`,[`The written behaviour policy.`,`Proximity, gesture or body language.`,`A child's SAT result.`,`The setting's Ofsted grade.`],1,`The spec highlights tone (verbal) and proximity, gesture and body language (non-verbal).`),a(`5-10-q2`,`5-10`,`A practitioner stands over a distressed five-year-old, points and says 'I'm not angry' in a sharp tone. The child covers their ears. What is the most likely issue?`,[`The child cannot hear kind words.`,`Five-year-olds never use body language.`,`The child is legally required to make eye contact.`,`Verbal and non-verbal messages are incongruent: tone, proximity and gesture communicate threat, so the words are not trusted.`],3,`Congruence: the non-verbal channel is contradicting the words. Get down, soften tone, reduce pointing.`,`AO2`),a(`5-10-q3`,`5-10`,`Why is considering communication factors especially important for children with EAL or SLCN?`,[`They rely more on tone, gesture, visuals and predictable body language to understand intent; mixed or threatening non-verbals remove the only clear channel.`,`They do not need any spoken language.`,`They always prefer shouting.`,`Non-verbal communication is irrelevant if an interpreter is booked once a year.`],0,`When language is limited, non-verbal cues do more of the work — for better or worse.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-10-o1`,topicId:`5-10`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain why the way practitioners speak, look and move matters when they communicate with children and young people.`,modelAnswer:`Tone can make the same words feel safe or threatening, so a calm, age-appropriate voice supports co-regulation and understanding. Non-verbal factors — proximity, gesture and body language — are read instantly: looming, pointing or a closed posture can trigger fight-or-flight, especially for children with trauma, SEND or EAL, whereas getting to the child's level with open gestures clarifies the message and invites co-operation. If words and body language do not match, children learn not to trust the adult.`,markScheme:[`Verbal factor (tone, words, pace) with why it matters (up to 2).`,`Non-verbal factor (proximity, gesture, body language) with why it matters (up to 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-11`,code:`5.11`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Building self-regulation and resilience in a setting`,page:59,notes:{gist:`Self-regulation is the ability to manage attention, impulses and emotions; resilience is the ability to recover and keep going after setbacks. Neither is a personality trait you either have or lack — both are taught through relationships, practice and a thoughtfully planned environment.`,mustKnow:[`Games and interactive sessions for turn-taking and impulse control (stop/go, board games, waiting for a timer, parachute).`,`Stories for reflection on emotions (naming feelings, talking about characters' choices, social stories).`,`Modelling coping skills: adults narrate 'I'm frustrated, I'm going to breathe and try again'.`,`Physical exercise to discharge arousal and improve mood and sleep.`,`Problem solving: 'what could we try next?' rather than rescuing or punishing only.`,`Socialisation opportunities so children practise recovery with peers, not only with adults.`,`Reframing challenges positively (a growth-mindset commentary: 'you can't do it yet').`,`Mindfulness and calm-down tools (breathing, grounding, quiet corners) taught before they are needed.`,`Supported risks: climbing, performing, trying a harder task with an adult as safety net — this is how resilience is earned.`,`Co-regulation comes first, especially for younger children and those with trauma: the adult lends their calm, then gradually hands regulation over.`],sections:[{heading:`Building self-regulation in the setting`,body:[`Impulse control is a muscle. Short, playful practice (freeze games, passing a cup of water, 'first/then') in calm moments transfers better than a lecture after a bite.`,`Stories, emotion coaching ('you wanted the bike; snatching hurt Sam; let's try 'my turn'') and visual supports (feelings fans, 5-point scales) give children a language for states they can then manage.`,`Physical exercise, outdoor play and movement breaks are regulation strategies, not rewards to be withdrawn when behaviour is difficult — withdrawing them often makes regulation harder.`,`Mindfulness is brief and concrete in early years (blowing a feather, listening for a sound) and can be more structured in secondary (timed breathing before an exam). It is not a substitute for changing an overwhelming environment.`]},{heading:`Building resilience`,body:[`Resilience grows when a challenge is real but survivable. Supported risks — a new climbing route, a speaking part, a test paper — with an adult who will help the child regroup afterwards, teach 'I can cope'.`,`Reframing ('that went wrong; what did we learn?') and problem solving stop children seeing a setback as proof they are the problem. Dweck’s growth commentary is useful if it is honest, not toxic positivity.`,`Socialisation opportunities (group challenges, clubs, restorative conversations) mean peers become a resilience resource. A child who never gets to repair a friendship does not learn that relationships can recover.`,`Model coping: if staff hide all adult difficulty, children never see repair. If staff panic, children borrow that too. Bandura’s modelling applies to emotional skills as much as to painting.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Coping skills are learned by watching others; self-efficacy grows through successful, supported challenges.`},{name:`Carol Dweck`,idea:`Reframing challenge as a chance to learn ('yet') supports resilience if feedback is specific and honest.`},{name:`Lev Vygotsky`,idea:`Co-regulation is social: adults and peers scaffold emotional control within the zone of proximal development until it becomes self-regulation.`}],examMoves:[`Name approaches from the spec list (games, stories, modelling, exercise, problem solving, socialisation, reframing, mindfulness, supported risks) — do not invent a generic 'be kind'.`,`Apply each approach to a setting (carpet, playground, exam hall, forest school).`,`Sequence: co-regulation then self-regulation; supported risk then reflection.`]},quiz:[a(`5-11-q1`,`5-11`,`Which approach specifically targets impulse control and turn-taking?`,[`Games and interactive sessions such as stop/go or board games.`,`Removing all play as a sanction.`,`Publishing SAT results.`,`Ignoring all peer conflict.`],0,`The specification names games and interactive sessions for turn-taking and impulse control.`),a(`5-11-q2`,`5-11`,`A Year 2 child panics when their model collapses. Which combination best builds self-regulation and resilience?`,[`Finish the model for them and avoid all future making.`,`Shame them in front of the class.`,`Send them home immediately.`,`Co-regulate (calm voice, breathe), reframe ('we can problem-solve'), and try a supported next step so they experience recovery.`],3,`Modelling coping, problem solving, reframing and a supported risk/retry match the spec approaches.`,`AO2`),a(`5-11-q3`,`5-11`,`Why should mindfulness and calm corners be taught before they are needed in a crisis?`,[`Mindfulness replaces safeguarding.`,`Children legally cannot use breathing techniques after a trigger.`,`Skills practised in a calm state are available under stress; introducing a new strategy at the point of explosion usually fails.`,`Calm corners are only for staff.`],2,`Self-regulation strategies have to be in muscle memory. Teaching at the point of crisis is too late.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-11-o1`,topicId:`5-11`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how practitioners can apply approaches that develop self-regulation and resilience in an educational setting.`,modelAnswer:`Self-regulation can be practised in games that demand turn-taking and impulse control, and through stories and emotion coaching that help children name feelings before they act. Adults should model coping aloud, use physical exercise and movement breaks to reduce arousal, and teach simple mindfulness or calm-down tools in ordinary time so they are available under stress. Resilience is built when children meet supported risks — a harder climbing step, a performance, a tricky maths problem — then reframe the setback and problem-solve with a calm adult and with peers. Co-regulation comes first: the practitioner lends their calm, then gradually hands control back so the child learns 'I can cope here'.`,markScheme:[`Two or more self-regulation approaches applied to a setting (games, stories, modelling, exercise, mindfulness, co-regulation) (up to 3).`,`Two or more resilience approaches applied (problem solving, socialisation, reframing, supported risks) (up to 3).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`5-12`,code:`5.12`,elementId:`e5`,title:`Keeping yourself and others safe when behaviour challenges`,page:59,notes:{gist:`Behaviour that challenges is behaviour that risks harm, not merely behaviour adults dislike. Risk assessment is dynamic: know the child's history, read triggers and early warning signs, judge the likelihood of harm to self or others, remove hazards, and follow the setting's policies. Heroics that ignore procedure put everyone in more danger.`,mustKnow:[`Use prior history: previous incidents, known weapons or spitting, medical needs, what has worked, what has escalated.`,`Identify triggers and early warning signs (change of face colour, pacing, silence, a particular demand, a noisy transition) so you can intervene early.`,`Assess likelihood of harm to self and others (children, staff, visitors) and how serious that harm could be.`,`Remove hazards and reduce risk: move other children, pocket scissors, open a door, reduce audience, get a second adult.`,`Follow setting policies and procedures: call for help, recording, informing parents, DSL if it is also safeguarding, agreed scripts, and any trained use of reasonable force only as a last resort.`,`Protect your own safety: do not block a doorway alone, do not chase into a road, wait for trained colleagues if the policy says so.`,`After the incident: first aid, regulation time, record while fresh, debrief, review the plan — assessment is not only in the moment.`],sections:[{heading:`Assessing in the moment`,body:[`Start with what you already know. A child with a history of throwing chairs when demands are public needs a different first move (reduce audience, offer an exit) from a child who has never been physical.`,`Early warning signs are the professional's chance to change the ending: swap the adult, change the demand, move to a pre-agreed calm space, use the co-regulation script. Waiting until someone is hit is a failed assessment.`,`Likelihood and severity: a small child kicking under a table is not the same risk as an adolescent with a chair near a glass door. Dynamic assessment asks who could be hurt, how badly, and how quickly you can lower that chance.`]},{heading:`Reduce risk and follow policy`,body:[`Environment first: remove the audience and the hazard rather than wrestling the child. Guide other children out. Do not leave yourself between the child and a hard edge if you can change the space.`,`Policy exists so individuals are not making it up under adrenaline. Know the call for help, who is trained, when reasonable force is permitted (to prevent harm, not to gain compliance with a task), and how to record. HASAWA 1974 requires employers to protect staff; staff must follow the controls they have been trained in.`,`Your safety is part of the child's safety. An injured or frightened adult cannot co-regulate anyone. Standing aside, keeping a barrier, and waiting for a second person is professional, not weak.`,`Unexplained, sudden or extreme behaviour is also a safeguarding and possible medical issue (pain, seizure, disclosure). Risk assessment includes 'what else could this be?'`]}],legislation:[{name:`Health and Safety at Work etc. Act`,year:`1974`,point:`Employers must protect staff and others so far as reasonably practicable; staff must follow training and report incidents.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,point:`Behaviour that harms may be a safeguarding issue, including child-on-child abuse; record and tell the DSL.`},{name:`DfE Use of Reasonable Force`,point:`Force only when necessary to prevent harm or serious damage, last resort, recorded, and never as punishment.`}],examMoves:[`Walk through the spec order: history → triggers/early signs → likelihood of harm → remove hazards → follow policy.`,`Include the practitioner's own safety — the question names it.`,`Do not write 'just restrain them'. Force is last resort, trained, and policy-led.`]},quiz:[a(`5-12-q1`,`5-12`,`When assessing risk around behaviour that challenges, 'prior history' is used to:`,[`Identify known patterns, what has escalated harm before, and what has reduced it.`,`Prove the child is permanently dangerous.`,`Share every past incident with other parents.`,`Avoid writing any new records.`],0,`History informs prediction and planning; it is not a life sentence or gossip.`),a(`5-12-q2`,`5-12`,`A Year 8 pupil is pacing, clenching fists, and moving towards a tray of scissors after a public reprimand. The priority risk-reduction move is to:`,[`Film the incident on a personal phone.`,`Stand in the doorway alone and shout.`,`Remove the scissors and the audience, give space, call for trained help, and follow the setting's policy.`,`Ignore the early warning signs until someone is cut.`],2,`Remove hazards, reduce audience, protect self and others, follow policy. Early warning signs are there to be used.`,`AO2`),a(`5-12-q3`,`5-12`,`Why is following setting policy part of risk assessment rather than paperwork after the event?`,[`Policy exists only for Ofsted displays.`,`Policy tells staff how to call for help, who is trained, when force is permitted and how to record, which reduces harm to self and others under stress.`,`HASAWA 1974 bans all behaviour procedures.`,`Children must write the risk assessment during the incident.`],1,`Under adrenaline, agreed procedures are a safety control. Improvisation increases likelihood of harm and allegations.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`5-12-o1`,topicId:`5-12`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how a practitioner judges the risk to themselves and others when a learner's behaviour becomes unsafe.`,scenario:`In a nursery, a four-year-old who has previously thrown objects when overwhelmed begins to shake, knock chairs, and move towards a glass patio door as the rest of the group lines up for outdoor play.`,modelAnswer:`Staff should use prior history (throwing when overwhelmed) and early warning signs (shaking, knocking chairs) to act before the glass door becomes a hazard, rather than waiting for injury. They assess likelihood of harm to the child, to peers in the line, and to themselves if they stand in the path of thrown furniture. Immediate reduction means moving the other children away, blocking or closing access to glass if it is safe, removing objects that can be thrown, and calling a second adult — not crowding or chasing. They follow the setting's policy on who leads, whether a calm space is used, recording, and informing parents or the DSL. The practitioner's own safety is part of the plan: keep a barrier, do not block the door alone, and wait for trained colleagues rather than using untrained force.`,markScheme:[`Prior history and/or triggers and early warning signs (up to 2).`,`Likelihood of harm to self/others and removing hazards (up to 2).`,`Follow setting policies/procedures and own safety (up to 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]}];function s(e,t,n,r,i,a,o=`AO1`){return{id:e,topicId:t,stem:n,options:r,answer:i,explanation:a,ao:o}}var c=[{id:`6-1`,code:`6.1`,elementId:`e6`,title:`Benefits of working with parents, carers and families`,page:61,notes:{gist:`Parents, carers and wider families are children's first and most enduring educators. Working with them produces shared expectations, smoother transitions, richer learning at home, family contributions to the setting, better information, stronger wellbeing, and decisions that advocate for the child. Tokenistic contact delivers none of these advantages.`,mustKnow:[`Shared expectations: home and setting use similar language for behaviour, sleep, food and learning so the child is not caught between two maps.`,`Smoother transitions: families prepare the child for nursery, school, class changes and exams when they know what is coming and why.`,`Learning at home: talking, reading, playing and the home learning environment multiply the setting's impact (EPPE/EPPSE: Sylva and colleagues).`,`Family contributions: volunteering, sharing jobs and hobbies, supporting events — families are a resource, not only a recipient of letters.`,`Sharing information: health, language, culture, SEND, changes at home — practitioners cannot see this from a baseline assessment alone.`,`Wellbeing: trusted adults at home and in the setting form a protective network; families who feel respected disclose worries earlier.`,`Making decisions and advocating: coproduction on support plans, EHCPs and everyday choices means the child has adults pulling in the same direction.`,`Wider family (grandparents, siblings, kinship and foster carers) often hold history and practical care; excluding them loses the advantage.`],sections:[{heading:`Consistency, transitions and learning`,body:[`When a parent and a key person both say 'stop, it hurts' and both read the same bedtime story structure, the child's nervous system spends less energy decoding adults and more on play and learning.`,`Transitions — starting Reception, moving year group, going into care, sitting GCSEs — are smoother when families have visited, seen the uniform, practised the walk, and know who the trusted adult is. Surprise is the enemy of a smooth start.`,`Learning at home is not a pack of worksheets. Serve-and-return talk, sharing books, cooking and counting the stairs are high-impact. Settings that coach this respectfully, and that send home what the child is excited about, extend the school day without burdening families in poverty with expensive projects.`]},{heading:`Contribution, information, wellbeing and advocacy`,body:[`Inviting a grandparent who sews, a dad who is a mechanic, or a parent to cook a festival dish does more than fill a diary date: it tells the child their family is valued and gives other children a wider view of the world.`,`Information sharing is two-way. Families notice the limp, the missing word, the new fear. Practitioners notice peer issues families cannot see. Together they identify need earlier.`,`Wellbeing and advocacy sit together. A family who trusts the setting will let you help them seek Early Help, a hearing test or a school-place appeal. The Children and Families Act 2014 expects coproduction in SEND; the EYFS requires partnership with parents and carers. Advantage is lost if contact only happens when there is a problem.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Kathy Sylva (EPPE/EPPSE)`,idea:`The quality of the home learning environment predicts outcomes more strongly than social class alone — a research basis for working with families on learning at home.`},{name:`Urie Bronfenbrenner`,idea:`Mesosystem: when home and setting work together, the child's nested contexts support rather than pull against each other.`}],legislation:[{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Settings must work closely with parents and carers and keep them informed about their child's progress.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Coproduction with parents and young people in SEND assessment, planning and review.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,point:`Practitioners should listen to families and share information appropriately to protect welfare.`}],examMoves:[`Do not list 'better communication' as an advantage on its own — say what it achieves (shared expectations, earlier identification, advocacy).`,`Use the spec list: shared expectations, transitions, learning at home, contributions, information, wellbeing, decisions/advocating.`,`Apply: if the child is looked after, name the carer, social worker and virtual school, not a generic 'parent'.`]},quiz:[s(`6-1-q1`,`6-1`,`Which of these is a benefit of working closely with parents, carers and the wider family?`,[`Settings can avoid all record-keeping.`,`Parents take over Ofsted inspections.`,`Shared expectations and smoother transitions between home and setting.`,`Practitioners no longer need a key-person system.`],2,`Shared expectations and smoother transitions are specification advantages. Partnership does not remove professional duties.`),s(`6-1-q2`,`6-1`,`A nursery invites a child's grandmother, who is a gardener, to plant bulbs with the group after a house move. This mainly illustrates which advantage?`,[`Family contributions (skills, jobs, hobbies, events) that support belonging, learning and wellbeing after a transition.`,`A way to avoid sharing any information with the family.`,`Proof that only grandparents should volunteer.`,`A legal requirement to replace the Early Years Educator.`],0,`Contributions from wider family are a named advantage; here they also support wellbeing after a move.`,`AO2`),s(`6-1-q3`,`6-1`,`Why is 'inviting parents in only when there is a problem' a weak form of partnership?`,[`It is the model required by the EYFS.`,`Families must never hear difficult news.`,`Ofsted forbids celebration of success.`,`It teaches families that contact means trouble, so they are less likely to share information, contribute, or advocate with the setting when wellbeing is at stake.`],3,`The advantages (information, wellbeing, advocacy) depend on trust. Problem-only contact destroys that trust.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`6-1-o1`,topicId:`6-1`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain three benefits for children and young people when practitioners work closely with parents, carers and the wider family.`,modelAnswer:`Shared expectations mean the child hears similar messages about behaviour, routines and learning at home and in the setting, which reduces anxiety and supports self-regulation. Smoother transitions follow when families are prepared for new classes, settings or exams and can rehearse what is coming. Learning at home — talk, books, play — extends the setting's impact, and family contributions (skills, volunteering, events) as well as two-way information-sharing help practitioners plan accurately and advocate with the family for the child's wellbeing.`,markScheme:[`1 mark for each valid advantage from the spec (shared expectations; transitions; learning at home; contributions; sharing information; wellbeing; decisions/advocating) (max 3).`,`1 further mark for each developed explanation of how it supports the child or setting (max 3).`,`Do not credit vague 'better communication' without a consequence.`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`6-2`,code:`6.2`,elementId:`e6`,title:`Different family set-ups, and why practitioners should be aware of them`,page:61,notes:{gist:`Children in the UK are raised in many family dynamics: nuclear, single-parent, extended, foster and adoptive, blended or step-families. Being mindful means valuing and respecting families, including them in planning, using family context to understand behaviour and development, and practising fairly. Structure is a description of who provides care, not a judgement of quality.`,mustKnow:[`Nuclear: two parents raising children in one household — common, not a gold standard.`,`Single parent: one adult with main day-to-day care; the child may still have two involved parents living apart.`,`Extended: multiple family members co-raising — grandparents, aunts, older siblings sharing housing or daily care.`,`Foster and adoptive: legal care by adults who were not the birth parents; contact, life-story work and identity remain live issues.`,`Blended/step: a parent re-partners; step-parents and step- or half-siblings, often with two homes and two sets of rules.`,`Importance: valuing and respecting families; inclusion in planning and provision; behavioural context; understanding possible developmental delay or regression after change; agreeing strategies; fair, inclusive practice.`,`Operational detail still matters: who has parental responsibility, who may collect, who consents, who receives reports.`,`Do not treat single-parent, same-sex, blended, foster or extended families as risk factors in themselves. Risk sits in conflict, neglect, poverty and instability.`],sections:[{heading:`Reading dynamics without ranking them`,body:[`A nuclear family may be highly conflicted; an extended household may offer a buffer of warm grandparents. Outcomes follow the quality of care, not the headcount.`,`Single-parent families are diverse. Barriers are often structural (time, money, stigma, shift work), not the absence of a second parent in the house. Forms and events that assume 'both mums and dads will attend' exclude.`,`Foster and adoptive families need accurate language ('first family', not 'real parents'), sensitivity around Mother's Day and family trees, and awareness that a settled presentation at school can sit alongside grief. Blended families involve loyalty binds and different surnames — 'different at Dad's' is a context, not defiance.`]},{heading:`Why mindfulness changes practice`,body:[`Valuing families: displays, books, forms and pronouns that show more than one family type. Children scan the walls for people like them.`,`Inclusion in planning: who needs the letter, the interpreter, the later meeting, the social-worker copy? A special guardian and a birth parent with PR may both need information.`,`Behaviour and development: regression after a new step-sibling, hypervigilance after a placement move, tiredness from living across two homes. Strategies agreed with the actual carers will work; generic 'tell mum' advice may not.`,`Fair inclusive practice: the same high expectations, without deficit talk ('no wonder, coming from that family'). Mindfulness is curiosity plus respect, not lowering the bar.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Diana Baumrind`,idea:`Parenting style (warmth and control) predicts self-regulation better than household structure alone — useful when contrasting dynamics with quality of care.`}],legislation:[{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`Parental responsibility and the child's welfare as paramount — know who holds PR in foster, adoptive and separated families.`},{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Discrimination related to sex, sexual orientation, race, religion or disability has no place in how families are treated.`}],examMoves:[`If asked for dynamics, characterise how care is organised — nuclear, single, extended, foster/adoptive, blended/step.`,`Never imply that a single-parent or blended family is automatically a safeguarding concern.`,`Importance answers need practice: forms, collection lists, family-tree activities, who is invited to the review.`]},quiz:[s(`6-2-q1`,`6-2`,`An extended family, in the specification's sense, is one in which:`,[`Two parents and their children live in one household with no other relatives involved.`,`A child lives only in residential children's homes.`,`Multiple family members co-raise the child, such as grandparents sharing daily care.`,`Parents are legally forbidden from working.`],2,`Extended = multiple family members co-raising. Nuclear is two parents in one household.`),s(`6-2-q2`,`6-2`,`A child lives with their mother, her new partner and two older step-siblings, and spends alternate weekends with their father. Which dynamic is this?`,[`Nuclear family only.`,`Adoptive family.`,`A family structure that must be reported as neglect.`,`Blended/step-family with shared care.`],3,`Re-partnering plus step-siblings is blended/step. Shared care with a birth parent does not make it nuclear or a safeguarding issue in itself.`,`AO2`),s(`6-2-q3`,`6-2`,`Why must practitioners be mindful of family dynamics when planning a 'family tree' activity?`,[`All children have identical households.`,`The activity can exclude or shame children in foster, adoptive, blended or single-parent families unless it is designed to value every dynamic; inclusive planning is fair practice.`,`Family trees are banned by the Children Act 1989.`,`Only nuclear families should take part in curriculum.`],1,`Mindfulness is about inclusion in provision. A poorly designed task becomes discrimination in effect.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`6-2-o1`,topicId:`6-2`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two different family dynamics in which children may be raised, and why practitioners must be mindful of them.`,modelAnswer:`A single-parent family is one in which one adult has main day-to-day care, although the child may still have contact with another parent; practitioners must not assume deficit and should offer meeting times and forms that do not require two household adults. A blended or step-family forms when a parent re-partners, so there may be step-siblings and two homes with different rules; being mindful means using this as behavioural context (tiredness, loyalty binds) and including the right adults in planning rather than addressing only 'mum'. In both cases, valuing the family and practising inclusively supports development better than ranking structures.`,markScheme:[`1 mark for each correctly characterised dynamic (nuclear, single parent, extended, foster/adoptive, blended/step) (max 2).`,`1 mark for each linked reason to be mindful (respect, inclusion in planning, behaviour context, development, strategies, fair practice) (max 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`6-3`,code:`6.3`,elementId:`e6`,title:`Barriers to working with families, and how to overcome them`,page:61,notes:{gist:`Partnership fails for practical and emotional reasons, not because families 'do not care'. Time, work commitments, limited resources, EAL, SEND and parents' own negative educational experiences are common barriers. Each has a matching professional strategy: relationships, open-door practice, two-way communication, events, translators or advocates, home visits and secure technology.`,mustKnow:[`Barriers: time — meetings and workshops only offered in the school day exclude many families.`,`Work commitments: shifts, zero-hours contracts and multiple jobs make daytime, on-site partnership impossible.`,`Limited resources: poverty, no phone credit or data, no transport, no childcare for siblings.`,`EAL: jargon-heavy English, untranslated letters, and using the child as interpreter.`,`SEND: the child's or the parent's needs (literacy, mental health, an EHCP process) can make forms and meetings inaccessible.`,`Family members' negative educational experiences: exclusion, being spoken down to, or social-care involvement make the gate feel like a threat.`,`Strategies: build a relationship (named key person, start with strengths); open-door policy; two-way communication (not only newsletters out).`,`Further strategies: open days and evenings including early/late options; professional translators or child advocates — never the child for sensitive talk; home visits where safe; secure technology (forums and apps) alongside paper and face-to-face.`,`'Hard to reach' often means 'our offer is hard to access'. Locate the barrier in the system as well as in the family.`],sections:[{heading:`Barriers you must be able to name`,body:[`Time and work: a 2 p.m. workshop systematically excludes shift workers, then labels them uninterested. Limited resources: no credit on a phone, no data for the journal app, no bus fare, no clean uniform for a celebration — digital-only contact is a barrier, not a modernisation.`,`EAL: letters in dense English, jargon (ELG, EHCP, PSED), and using the child to interpret invert family roles and hide safeguarding or SEND conversations.`,`SEND: a parent with literacy needs, mental ill-health, or who is navigating their child's EHCP may miss a form, not a cue of apathy. Negative educational experiences (exclusion, being spoken down to, social-care involvement) make the school gate feel like a threat.`]},{heading:`Strategies that match the barrier`,body:[`Build the relationship first: daily door contact, a named person, asking what is going well at home. Open-door policy means families can come without a crisis, and staff are visibly available.`,`Two-way communication: phone calls, paper copies, translated key documents, and time to talk — not a one-way app feed. Open days and evenings need more than one time slot.`,`Translators and advocates: book a professional interpreter; some families also benefit from an independent advocate (for example SENDIASS). Home visits, where policy and safety allow, meet families on their territory and can reveal strengths invisible at the gate.`,`Secure technology (closed forums, setting apps, encrypted email) helps working parents if it is optional, GDPR-compliant, and paired with face-to-face and paper. It is not secure if staff use personal phones or open social media.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Equality Act`,year:`2010`,point:`Reasonable adjustments and no discrimination related to disability, race or other protected characteristics — including how families are communicated with.`},{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Any app, forum or message system must be a lawful, secure processing of personal data.`}],examMoves:[`Pair each barrier with a strategy — that is how 6-mark answers are built.`,`Never use a child as the interpreter in an exam answer you want to pass.`,`Challenge the phrase 'hard to reach' if the command is discuss/evaluate.`]},quiz:[s(`6-3-q1`,`6-3`,`Which of the following is a barrier to partnership named in the specification?`,[`Family members' negative educational experiences.`,`The existence of a key-person system.`,`Too many translated documents.`,`Open-door policies.`],0,`Negative prior experience of education is a named barrier. Key person, translation and open-door are strategies.`),s(`6-3-q2`,`6-3`,`A parent works night shifts, has limited data on their phone, and did not attend a 2 p.m. phonics workshop. The most appropriate first strategy is:`,[`Record them as 'not interested in learning'.`,`Offer two-way contact at a workable time, a paper phonics guide, and a relationship via the key person rather than digital-only, daytime events.`,`Fine them under the Equality Act.`,`Ask their five-year-old to teach them phonics from memory.`],1,`Time, work and limited resources are the barriers; flexible two-way communication and non-digital options are the matching strategies.`,`AO2`),s(`6-3-q3`,`6-3`,`Why should children not be used as translators in meetings about behaviour or SEND?`,[`It is always faster.`,`EAL is not a real barrier.`,`GDPR requires children to process all family data.`,`It inverts family roles, risks inaccurate translation, and can expose the child to adult information they should not carry — professional interpreters or advocates are the appropriate strategy.`],3,`The spec lists translators or child advocates as the strategy — meaning professionals/advocates, not the child interpreting their own case.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`6-3-o1`,topicId:`6-3`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Give two things that can get in the way of working with families and carers, and one way to overcome each.`,modelAnswer:`Work commitments and time are a barrier when events are only offered in the school day, so shift-working families appear disengaged; the matching strategy is two-way communication at usable times, an open-door offer, and early or late open evenings. EAL is a barrier when letters are jargon-heavy and children are asked to interpret; the matching strategy is professional translators, translated key documents, and a relationship with a named person so the family can ask questions without losing face. In both cases the setting changes its offer rather than blaming the family.`,markScheme:[`Two barriers from: time, work, limited resources, EAL, SEND, negative educational experiences (up to 2).`,`A matching strategy for each from: relationship, open-door, two-way communication, open days/evenings, translators/advocates, home visits, secure technology (up to 4).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`6-4`,code:`6.4`,elementId:`e6`,title:`How agencies and services help children and families`,page:62,notes:{gist:`No setting holds every specialism. Charities, public services and local education authority teams wrap around families so that housing, mental health, SEND, safeguarding and advice sit alongside education. Practitioners need to know who does what so they can signpost accurately and refer with consent (or without, if safeguarding requires it).`,mustKnow:[`Charities: Family Action (practical and emotional support for families under stress) and Family Rights Group (advice for families involved with children's services).`,`Charities: Action for Children (practical support, family hubs, advocacy) and Save the Children (poverty, education and UK child-poverty programmes as well as overseas work).`,`Public services: NHS (GPs, health visitors, school nurses, paediatrics, speech and language).`,`Public services: CYPMHS (Children and Young People's Mental Health Services, formerly often called CAMHS); social services/children's services (s.17 in need, s.47 protection, looked-after children); Citizens Advice (benefits, housing, debt, employment).`,`Local education authorities: schools, colleges and careers services; area SENDCos and inclusion teams; SENDIASS (impartial SEND Information, Advice and Support for parents and young people).`,`Settings signpost, refer and collaborate; they do not replace these agencies. Knowing the local offer is part of the job.`,`Family Help (Working Together 2026) and family hubs sit at the join of local authority, health and voluntary sector: targeted early help combined with s.17 support under a family help plan.`,`Match the agency to the need: debt is not a CYPMHS referral; a s.47 concern is not a job for Save the Children on site.`],sections:[{heading:`Charities and public services`,body:[`Family Action and Action for Children may provide parenting programmes, family support workers, grants for essentials, or a presence in a family hub. Family Rights Group is the specialist charity when children's services are involved — they help families understand meetings, kinship options and their rights.`,`Save the Children is easy to think of as overseas only; in exam answers you can note UK work on child poverty and learning, but do not invent a local referral pathway you have not got.`,`NHS pathways start with the GP, health visitor (under-fives) or school nurse. Speech and language, audiology and community paediatrics sit here. CYPMHS provides specialist mental health assessment and treatment — waiting lists are long, so settings still hold the child day to day.`,`Children's services assess children in need and children at risk. Citizens Advice is the practical agency for the debt letter that is keeping a parent from the school gate.`]},{heading:`Local education authority services`,body:[`Schools and colleges are themselves services: attendance officers, pastoral teams, careers advisers for young people thinking about post-16. The LA coordinates place-planning, exclusions processes and, for some children, alternative provision.`,`Area SENDCos and inclusion teams advise early years and schools on the graduated approach, training and when an EHC needs assessment may be appropriate.`,`SENDIASS is free, impartial and for parents and young people — not the school's mouthpiece. Signposting a family there is a professional move when they disagree with the setting or need help to understand an EHCP.`,`Good practice is a named person, a clear reason for contact, consent unless safeguarding, and feedback to the family so they are not 'done to'.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Children Act`,year:`1989`,point:`s.17 children in need and s.47 significant harm — the legal basis for children's services involvement.`},{name:`Children and Families Act`,year:`2014`,point:`Local offer, EHCPs, and impartial information, advice and support (SENDIASS).`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,point:`Multi-agency working: education, health and social care cannot treat a child's needs as someone else's problem.`}],examMoves:[`Sort the agency into charity, public service or LA — that is how the spec is organised.`,`Say what the agency actually does for the family in the scenario, not just its name.`,`Do not confuse CYPMHS (mental health) with children's services (safeguarding/welfare) or SENDIASS (advice) with the SENCO (setting role).`]},quiz:[s(`6-4-q1`,`6-4`,`SENDIASS is best described as:`,[`A charity that only works overseas.`,`The school inspection arm of Ofsted.`,`The local impartial SEND Information, Advice and Support Service for parents and young people.`,`A replacement for children's services in s.47 enquiries.`],2,`SENDIASS sits with the local authority's SEND duties and is impartial advice, not social care or inspection.`),s(`6-4-q2`,`6-4`,`A parent is frightened about a children's services assessment and asks who can explain their rights. The most appropriate signpost is:`,[`Family Rights Group, which advises families involved with children's services.`,`Save the Children emergency team on site.`,`The exam board.`,`CYPMHS, because all social-care cases are mental health cases.`],0,`Family Rights Group is the named charity for families and children's services. CYPMHS is mental health; they are not interchangeable.`,`AO2`),s(`6-4-q3`,`6-4`,`Why do practitioners need both public services (NHS, CYPMHS, children's services, Citizens Advice) and LA education teams (area SENDCo, SENDIASS, schools/careers)?`,[`One agency can legally do every job.`,`Charities have replaced the NHS.`,`Settings are forbidden from signposting.`,`Children's needs are educational, health, welfare and practical; the right agency depends on the problem, and using the wrong door delays support.`],3,`AO3 is matching need to agency. A debt problem and a possible eating disorder do not go to the same door.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`6-4-o1`,topicId:`6-4`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how different agencies and services help children and their families.`,scenario:`The Ali family have mounting rent arrears. Six-year-old Yasmin is anxious, her speech is unclear, and her mother is overwhelmed. Staff are not worried about significant harm but know the family is close to crisis.`,modelAnswer:`Citizens Advice can help with benefits, housing and debt so the practical stress driving Yasmin's anxiety is addressed. NHS routes (GP, school nurse, speech and language) can assess her communication and anxiety, with CYPMHS if mental health needs become specialist. Family Action or Action for Children may offer a family support worker or hub-based parenting and practical help. If needs escalate, children's services could assess a child in need (s.17), but that is not the first door. Educationally, the school SENCO, area SENDCo/inclusion team and, for the parent, SENDIASS can support communication needs and the mother's understanding of any support plan. The practitioner's job is to signpost and refer with consent, not to become the family's social worker.`,markScheme:[`At least one charity named with a relevant function (up to 2).`,`At least one public service named with a relevant function (NHS, CYPMHS, children's services, Citizens Advice) (up to 2).`,`At least one LA education service (schools/colleges/careers, area SENDCo/inclusion, SENDIASS) (up to 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`6-5`,code:`6.5`,elementId:`e6`,title:`Building and keeping good joint working with families, agencies and professionals`,page:62,notes:{gist:`Collaboration only works when the rules of the partnership are clear. That means following policies, holding professional boundaries, protecting confidentiality and data, safeguarding welfare, getting consent right, making referrals efficiently, knowing who owns which action, and being explicit about how information — including social media — is shared.`,mustKnow:[`Policies adhered to: safeguarding, information-sharing, SEND, complaints — collaboration is not a private arrangement between two friendly adults.`,`Professional boundaries: warmth without dual relationships, gifts, babysitting or becoming the family's unofficial social worker.`,`Confidentiality and data protection: need-to-know, lawful basis, secure storage; DPA 2018 / UK GDPR.`,`Safeguarding and welfare: the child's safety overrides a parent's wish for secrecy if there is a risk of significant harm.`,`Correct consent: know when you need it (most referrals) and when you do not (safeguarding, some public-interest sharing).`,`Efficiency in referrals and escalations: right door, right information, follow-up — children are harmed by delay and by duplicate, incomplete referrals.`,`Clear expectations and ownership per role: who is the lead professional, who books the interpreter, who updates the family.`,`Clear expectations for sharing information, including social media: no discussing families on personal accounts or staff WhatsApp; use work systems.`],sections:[{heading:`Rules that keep collaboration safe`,body:[`A TAF or professionals' meeting without a policy frame becomes gossip. Agenda, minutes, consent, and an information-sharing agreement keep it professional.`,`Boundaries protect the collaboration. If you babysit for the family, you cannot later be the person who raises a safeguarding concern without a conflict. If you over-share your own problems, the family may protect you instead of using you.`,`Confidentiality is not secrecy from other professionals who need to know. It is a promise that information will not be used for curiosity or humiliation. Data protection is the legal skin around that promise.`]},{heading:`Consent, roles, referrals and social media`,body:[`Ask consent for Early Help, SALT, a family-support charity. Record it. If a child is at risk, share under Working Together and KCSIE even without consent, and tell the family unless that would increase the risk.`,`Ownership: 'we will all keep an eye' is how actions die. Name the person, the task and the date. Escalation routes (DSL, children's services, police 999) must be known before the crisis.`,`Social media: families may friend staff, post about other people's children, or run class WhatsApp groups that exclude and inflame. Policy should say staff will not connect with families on personal accounts, will not comment on pupils online, and will direct disputes into official channels. Young people in secondary settings need the same clarity about Snapchat and group chats with staff.`,`Efficiency: one well-written referral with chronology, consent status and what has already been tried beats five vague emails. Chase if you hear nothing — professional courtesy is not the same as assuming it is done.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Lawful, fair, secure processing; safeguarding sharing is permitted where necessary to protect a child.`},{name:`Working Together to Safeguard Children`,point:`Duty to work together, share information and not let children fall between agencies.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,point:`Codes of conduct, information sharing, and safer working including online.`},{name:`Children Act`,year:`2004`,point:`Duty to cooperate to improve children's wellbeing — the statutory backbone of collaborative working.`}],examMoves:[`Use the spec vocabulary: policies, boundaries, confidentiality/data, safeguarding, consent, referrals/escalations, role ownership, information-sharing/social media.`,`Show the tension: confidentiality versus safeguarding is a classic AO3 point.`,`Give a social media example if marks allow — the spec names it.`]},quiz:[s(`6-5-q1`,`6-5`,`When may a practitioner share information with another agency without parental consent?`,[`When it is necessary to safeguard a child from significant harm, in line with Working Together and KCSIE.`,`Whenever a colleague is curious.`,`To post a named case study on personal social media.`,`Never — DPA 2018 bans all sharing.`],0,`Safeguarding is a lawful basis for sharing without consent. Curiosity and social media are breaches, not exceptions.`),s(`6-5-q2`,`6-5`,`A practitioner is asked to join a family's class WhatsApp and to babysit so that 'collaboration is easier'. The professional response is:`,[`Accept both to prove they are approachable.`,`Accept babysitting but film it for evidence.`,`Decline dual relationships and personal-app contact; keep collaboration on work systems with clear role boundaries.`,`Share other families' data in the WhatsApp so everyone is 'included'.`],2,`Boundaries, data protection and clear information-sharing channels are the specification points. Dual relationships undermine later safeguarding.`,`AO2`),s(`6-5-q3`,`6-5`,`Why does 'clear ownership per role' make referrals and escalations more effective?`,[`It allows everyone to assume someone else has acted, which is safer.`,`Named people, tasks and timescales prevent delay and duplicated or incomplete referrals, so the child actually receives the service.`,`Only parents may own actions in a TAF.`,`Ofsted forbids action logs.`],1,`Efficiency and ownership are named. Vague collective responsibility is how children wait.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`6-5-o1`,topicId:`6-5`,marks:6,command:`Discuss`,stem:`Discuss why practitioners need to build, and keep, strong joint working with families, agencies and other professionals.`,modelAnswer:`Collaboration that follows policy, professional boundaries and data-protection rules keeps the child's welfare at the centre rather than adult convenience. Confidentiality builds family trust, but safeguarding and Working Together mean information must still be shared, without consent if necessary, when a child is at risk — getting consent right, and knowing the exception, prevents both reckless gossip and dangerous silence. Efficient referrals with a named owner for each action stop children falling between CYPMHS, the SENCO and children's services. Clear expectations about information-sharing, including no discussion of families on personal social media, protect dignity and safer working. Without these rules, 'partnership' becomes either a dual relationship or a set of unanswered emails, and the child is the one who loses.`,markScheme:[`Policies, boundaries and/or confidentiality/data protection (up to 2).`,`Safeguarding/welfare and correct consent, including when consent is not required (up to 2).`,`Efficient referrals/escalations, role ownership and/or information-sharing including social media (up to 2).`],ao:`AO3`}]}];function l(e,t,n,r,i,a,o=`AO1`){return{id:e,topicId:t,stem:n,options:r,answer:i,explanation:a,ao:o}}var u=[{id:`7-1`,code:`7.1`,elementId:`e7`,title:`Using national assessments and benchmarks to improve teaching`,page:63,notes:{gist:`National assessments and benchmarks (EYFS Profile, phonics screening, multiplication check, KS2 SATs, GCSEs, and non-statutory frameworks such as Development Matters) exist so pedagogy is not guesswork. They inform planning and delivery against statutory requirements, help identify needs, support monitoring of progress, and give a comparative standard so provision is consistent and professionally confident.`,mustKnow:[`Inform planning and delivery in line with statutory requirements: EYFS, National Curriculum programmes of study, and published assessment arrangements.`,`Identify needs: a child well below the Early Learning Goals, a phonics score that flags decoding, a KS2 reading result that does not match classwork.`,`Monitor progress and achievement over time, not only on one test day.`,`Provide a comparative educational standard so settings can check their delivery against national expectations and have confidence they are pitching correctly.`,`Benchmarks are typical standards or milestones (for example Development Matters checkpoint statements, national GCSE grade distributions) used to compare a child or a cohort.`,`They must not become the whole curriculum: teaching to the test narrows pedagogy; using the data to adapt teaching is the professional use.`,`For babies and toddlers there is no SAT — observation against EYFS and Development Matters is the national frame.`],sections:[{heading:`What the national tools are for`,body:[`The EYFS Profile at the end of Reception judges whether children have met Early Learning Goals. It should shape Year 1 pedagogy (more play and language for those not at GLD) rather than sit in a drawer.`,`The Year 1 phonics screening check, Year 4 multiplication tables check, KS2 national tests, and GCSEs/vocational qualifications at 16 give comparable data. Leaders use them to ask whether groups (disadvantaged, SEND, EAL, looked-after) are being served.`,`Development Matters and, where used, Birth to 5 Matters are non-statutory but widely used benchmarks in early years. They stop planning being only 'what we have always done'.`]},{heading:`From data to pedagogy`,body:[`Identify need: a low phonics score is a prompt for extra decoding practice, hearing check, or SEND investigation — not a label of failure. A child exceeding ELGs needs stretch, not another year of the same.`,`Monitor: compare a child's journey to typical benchmarks and to their own starting point. Progress from a very low baseline can be excellent even if the national standard is not yet met.`,`Comparative standard: if a whole cohort is below national in writing, pedagogy (modelling, opportunities, handwriting, oracy) needs changing, not only individual children. Confidence of delivery comes from knowing you are aligned with what is statutorily expected, then adapting for the children in front of you.`,`Misuse: publishing ranks on a classroom wall, narrowing to test items, or ignoring children who are 'on track' so they stall. Effective pedagogy uses the benchmark, then returns to rich, age-appropriate experience.`]}],legislation:[{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Sets the early learning goals and the requirement for the EYFS Profile; assessment should inform provision.`},{name:`Education Act`,year:`2002`,point:`National Curriculum and associated assessment arrangements in maintained schools.`}],examMoves:[`Name a real national assessment or benchmark (ELG/EYFSP, phonics screen, SATs, GCSEs, Development Matters) then say how it changes teaching.`,`Cover at least two of: statutory planning, identifying need, monitoring, comparative standard.`,`ESP stretch: data without a pedagogical change is not 'informing' anything.`]},quiz:[l(`7-1-q1`,`7-1`,`National assessment and benchmark outcomes are used to:`,[`Replace all practitioner observation.`,`Inform planning and delivery against statutory requirements, identify needs, and monitor progress against a comparative standard.`,`Rank families on a public website as a legal duty on every key person.`,`Prove that children under five must sit GCSEs.`],1,`The specification lists statutory planning, identifying need, monitoring, and a comparative standard.`),l(`7-1-q2`,`7-1`,`A Reception cohort's EYFS Profile shows many children have not met the communication and language ELGs. The most effective pedagogical response is:`,[`Adapt Year 1 and late-Reception provision towards language-rich play, adult modelling and targeted support, using the outcomes to identify who needs more.`,`Ignore the data because the Profile is only for Ofsted.`,`Increase test papers in the baby room.`,`Move all children to Year 2 immediately.`],0,`Outcomes should change planning and identify need. They are not wallpaper and they are not a reason to skip childhood.`,`AO2`),l(`7-1-q3`,`7-1`,`Why is a comparative national standard useful for practitioners' confidence of delivery?`,[`It removes professional judgement.`,`It guarantees every child the same GCSE grade.`,`It tells them their pitch matches statutory expectations, so they can justify curriculum choices and spot when a child or cohort is adrift — without reducing pedagogy to teaching to the test.`,`Benchmarks are only for private schools.`],2,`The spec's 'consistency/confidence of delivery' point is about alignment with national expectations, not about abandoning judgement.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-1-o1`,topicId:`7-1`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how national assessment and benchmark outcomes can inform effective pedagogy.`,modelAnswer:`National assessments such as the EYFS Profile, phonics screening check or KS2 tests, and benchmarks such as Development Matters, show whether delivery matches statutory expectations so practitioners can plan the next sequence with confidence. They identify children and groups who need support or stretch, and they monitor progress over time against a comparative standard. Effective pedagogy then changes (more language-rich play, extra decoding, greater challenge) rather than merely recording the score.`,markScheme:[`Names a national assessment or benchmark and links it to statutory planning/delivery (1–2).`,`Identifying need and/or monitoring progress/comparative standard with a pedagogical change (2–3).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`7-2`,code:`7.2`,elementId:`e7`,title:`Diagnostic, formative and summative assessment in practice`,page:63,notes:{gist:`Diagnostic assessment finds out what a child already knows before you teach; formative assessment gathers frequent, often informal evidence while learning is happening; summative assessment sums up attainment at the end of a period for the child, family, leaders and inspectors. Professional practice uses all three, for different jobs.`,mustKnow:[`Diagnostic: prior knowledge and skills before introducing a subject or unit — a recap quiz, a concept cartoon, a 'show me how you hold the scissors', a baseline observation.`,`Formative: frequent, often informal, ongoing evidence of progress and attainment — questioning, observation, retrieval practice, draft work, exit tickets, learning journals.`,`Summative: final, end of a period of study, overall attainment, data for stakeholders (management, parents, Ofsted) — EYFS Profile, end-of-unit tests, GCSEs, annual reports.`,`Diagnostic shapes the starting point so you do not reteach what is secure or skip what is missing.`,`Formative shapes the next five minutes and the next lesson (Black and Wiliam: assessment for learning).`,`Summative shapes reports, transitions, accountability and whether the curriculum as a whole worked.`,`The same activity can serve more than one purpose (a marked mock is summative for the grade and formative if the pupil then acts on the feedback).`],sections:[{heading:`Diagnostic and formative in daily practice`,body:[`Before a Reception shape unit, a diagnostic 'can you find something round?' stops you pitching at the wrong level. Before GCSE Macbeth, a diagnostic on tragedy vocabulary does the same.`,`Formative assessment is the heartbeat: observing whether the two-year-old can now pour, listening to a reader, circulating during a science practical. It only informs practice if you change something — grouping, recap, a model, a scaffold, or moving on.`,`Feedback is formative when the child can still use it. A score with no next step is a summative stamp wearing formative clothing.`]},{heading:`Summative and how the three work together`,body:[`Summative assessment is necessary: parents, receiving teachers, colleges, Ofsted and the child themselves need a picture of overall attainment. It should sample the curriculum fairly and be as valid and reliable as the context allows.`,`Stakeholders: management use it for curriculum review; parents use it to understand progress; Ofsted use it as one evidence source among others. It must not be gamed (off-rolling, endless practice papers that replace the curriculum).`,`A strong sequence: diagnose starting points → teach with ongoing formative checks → summative judgement → the next diagnostic starts from that new baseline. Missing any layer weakens practice: all-summative teaching is too late to help; all-formative with no sum-up leaves families and leaders without a shared picture.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Black and Wiliam`,idea:`Formative assessment (assessment for learning) that is used to adapt teaching has a significant impact on attainment.`},{name:`Dylan Wiliam`,idea:`Responsive teaching: hinge questions, exit tickets and feedback that the learner can act on.`}],examMoves:[`Define all three in a sentence each, then apply — mixing them up is the most common lost mark.`,`Diagnostic = before; formative = during; summative = after/end.`,`Say who the summative data is for (management, parents, Ofsted) if the question allows.`]},quiz:[l(`7-2-q1`,`7-2`,`Diagnostic assessment is best defined as:`,[`A final exam used only by Ofsted.`,`Any sticker given in a lesson.`,`A statutory duty to stop teaching for a term.`,`Finding out prior knowledge and skills before introducing a subject.`],3,`Diagnostic sits at the start. Summative sits at the end. Stickers are not a definition.`),l(`7-2-q2`,`7-2`,`A teacher uses mini-whiteboards mid-lesson, spots a widespread misconception, and reteaches the step. This is primarily:`,[`Summative assessment for a stakeholder report.`,`Formative assessment informing immediate practice.`,`A diagnostic baseline from last September.`,`An EYFS Profile judgement.`],1,`Frequent, in-the-moment evidence that changes teaching is formative. The Profile and reports are summative.`,`AO2`),l(`7-2-q3`,`7-2`,`Why is it a problem if a setting uses only summative assessment?`,[`By the time overall attainment is measured, teaching opportunities have been missed; diagnostic and formative evidence are what allow practice to change in time.`,`Summative assessment is illegal.`,`Parents are not stakeholders.`,`Ofsted forbids all end-of-year reports.`],0,`Each type has a job. Summative without the other two is too late to be pedagogy.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-2-o1`,topicId:`7-2`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain the difference between diagnostic, formative and summative assessment and how each informs professional practice.`,modelAnswer:`Diagnostic assessment identifies prior knowledge and skills before a topic, so the practitioner pitches correctly and does not skip gaps — for example a recap quiz or a baseline observation of scissor grip. Formative assessment is frequent and often informal: questioning, observation and draft work that change the next teaching move while there is still time to improve. Summative assessment comes at the end of a period and captures overall attainment as data for stakeholders such as parents, managers and Ofsted (an EYFS Profile, an end-of-unit test or a GCSE). Practice is strongest when the three connect: diagnose, teach and check formatively, then sum up and start the next cycle from that new baseline.`,markScheme:[`Diagnostic defined and applied to practice (up to 2).`,`Formative defined and applied (up to 2).`,`Summative defined, including stakeholders, and applied (up to 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`7-3`,code:`7.3`,elementId:`e7`,title:`Observing and reporting on learners' participation, understanding and progress`,page:63,notes:{gist:`Observation is the core assessment method in early years and remains essential in schools. Done well, it identifies developmental progress, feeds back to the child, informs next-steps planning, meets recording policies, is valid and reliable, triggers interventions, and gives colleagues, families and agencies a truthful picture of participation, understanding and progress.`,mustKnow:[`Identifies developmental progress: what the child can do now that they could not do last month, across domains.`,`Informs feedback: specific, timely comments to the child and family ('you used three colours to show the story').`,`Informs next-steps planning: the observation is wasted if provision does not change.`,`Adheres to recording policies: agreed formats, timescales, storage, no personal phones, no named children on open social media.`,`Validity: you observe what you claim to observe. Reliability: another trained adult would recognise the same evidence.`,`Enables interventions: a pattern of non-participation, conceptual confusion or stalled progress is a prompt for support, not a wait-and-see.`,`Informs colleagues, family and agencies: key person, teacher, SENCO, DSL, health visitor, social worker — need-to-know, accurate, dated.`,`Observe participation (is the child in the learning?), conceptual understanding (do they grasp the idea, or only copy?), and progress (change over time).`],sections:[{heading:`Observing participation, understanding and progress`,body:[`Participation: is the child present, attending, playing with others, contributing? A child who sits on the edge of every carpet is giving you data. So is a sixth-former who has stopped handing work in.`,`Conceptual understanding: can they explain, apply, or teach it back? A correct worksheet can hide guessing. A toddler who fetches a bigger block for a wobbly tower is showing a concept more clearly than a tick sheet.`,`Progress: compare with the child's own baseline and with typical development. A single sparkling observation is an anecdote; a dated sequence is evidence.`]},{heading:`Quality, recording and who needs to know`,body:[`Validity and reliability suffer when staff only observe their favourite children, only indoors, or write what they hoped to see. Short, factual, dated notes with the context ('during free play, with two peers') beat long, flowery stories.`,`Recording policies exist for workload, consistency and GDPR. Follow the setting's system (journal, tracker, CPOMS for welfare). Photographs need consent and must not identify children on public platforms.`,`Reports to families should be honest and usable. Reports to agencies should distinguish fact from opinion. Observation that never leaves the practitioner's head cannot enable intervention or multi-agency work.`]}],legislation:[{name:`Data Protection Act`,year:`2018`,point:`Learning journals, photographs and reports are personal data; store and share lawfully.`},{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Ongoing assessment through observation, and sharing progress with parents/carers.`}],examMoves:[`Name several purposes from the spec (progress, feedback, next steps, policy, validity/reliability, intervention, informing others).`,`Apply the triad: participation, conceptual understanding, progress.`,`Mention a recording or data-protection point so you show professional, not just 'watching children'.`]},quiz:[l(`7-3-q1`,`7-3`,`Validity in observation means:`,[`Writing the longest possible narrative.`,`Only observing outdoors.`,`Never sharing the observation with anyone.`,`Observing what you claim to be measuring, rather than a convenient or biased sample.`],3,`Validity = you are capturing the thing you say you are. Reliability is whether another adult would agree.`),l(`7-3-q2`,`7-3`,`A key person notices a two-year-old has stopped joining the snack table and no longer attempts two-word phrases they used last month. Observation is most useful here if it:`,[`Stays unrecorded because 'they might grow out of it'.`,`Is posted on the setting's public Facebook with the child's name.`,`Is dated, compared with previous progress, shared with the family and used to plan intervention (and a possible hearing/SALT pathway).`,`Is used only to prove the child is naughty at snack.`],2,`Progress, recording, informing family/agencies and enabling intervention are the spec purposes. Public posting would breach policy and DPA.`,`AO2`),l(`7-3-q3`,`7-3`,`Why must observation of conceptual understanding go beyond whether a child completed a worksheet?`,[`Completion can hide copying or guessing; observing explanation, application or play use of the idea is a more valid picture and better informs next steps.`,`Worksheets are banned in all key stages.`,`Ofsted counts only worksheets.`,`Conceptual understanding cannot be observed before A level.`],0,`Validity: participation and a finished page are not the same as understanding.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-3-o1`,topicId:`7-3`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how observation and reporting on participation, conceptual understanding and progress should be applied in an educational setting.`,modelAnswer:`Observation should capture whether the child is participating, whether they actually understand the concept (not only whether they finished a task), and whether there is progress from their own baseline. Notes must be valid, reliable, dated and stored under the setting's recording and data-protection policy so they can inform feedback, next-steps planning and, where needed, intervention. Reporting then shares that evidence with colleagues, families and agencies on a need-to-know basis rather than leaving it in one adult's head.`,markScheme:[`Participation and/or conceptual understanding and/or progress applied (up to 2).`,`Purpose: feedback, next steps, validity/reliability, recording policy, intervention, informing others (up to 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`7-4`,code:`7.4`,elementId:`e7`,title:`What practitioners do in assessment`,page:63,notes:{gist:`The practitioner is not a bystander to assessment. They observe, record and review progress against requirements; they monitor understanding and general progress; they give support and targeted feedback to improve; and they plan and prepare children for statutory and national assessments without reducing the curriculum to a test factory.`,mustKnow:[`Observe, record and review progress against requirements (EYFS, National Curriculum, qualification specifications, individual targets or EHCP outcomes).`,`Monitor understanding and general progress — daily, not only on assessment week.`,`Support and targeted feedback to improve: the assessment cycle is not complete until the child knows what to do next and is helped to do it.`,`Plan and prepare for statutory/national assessment: familiarisation with formats, reducing anxiety, access arrangements, and still teaching the full curriculum.`,`Work within the setting's assessment policy, moderation and quality assurance so judgements are consistent.`,`Include the child and family: age-appropriate self-assessment, two-way progress conversations.`,`Escalate when monitoring shows a concern (SENCO, DSL, family) rather than waiting for the next national window.`],sections:[{heading:`Observe, record, review, monitor`,body:[`Against requirements means you know the ELG, the programme of study, or the specification point you are looking at. Random cute observations that never map to a requirement do not help you review progress.`,`Recording is factual, dated and stored correctly. Review is the professional act: looking back over a month of evidence and asking what has changed.`,`Monitoring understanding is in-the-moment (a hinge question, a look at the painting, listening to the explanation). Monitoring general progress includes wellbeing, attendance and participation, because a child who has stopped coming in cannot meet a target.`]},{heading:`Feedback, support and national assessment`,body:[`Targeted feedback is specific and usable: 'add a full stop so the reader knows where the idea ends', not 'be neater'. Support may be scaffolding, a group, a resource, or a reasonable adjustment.`,`Preparing for statutory assessment is part of the role: phonics-check practice that still feels like reading; GCSE walking-talking mocks; explaining to a parent what the EYFS Profile is. Access arrangements (reader, extra time, rest breaks) are the practitioner's job to notice and apply for, not a favour.`,`The ethical line: preparation that teaches the curriculum and the format is professional; weeks of past papers that replace art, play or RSHE are not. The practitioner advocates for the child inside the accountability system.`]}],examMoves:[`Use the four spec verbs: observe/record/review; monitor; support/feedback; plan and prepare for statutory assessment.`,`Apply to a real assessment (ELG, phonics, SAT, GCSE) so it does not sound generic.`,`Feedback that cannot be used is not the practitioner role completed.`]},quiz:[l(`7-4-q1`,`7-4`,`A core part of the practitioner's role in assessment is to:`,[`Outsource all observation to Ofsted inspectors.`,`Observe, record and review progress against requirements, and give targeted feedback so the child can improve.`,`Publish every child's raw scores on social media.`,`Only assess on the final day of Year 11.`],1,`The spec lists observing/recording/reviewing against requirements and support/feedback to improve.`),l(`7-4-q2`,`7-4`,`A Year 2 teacher notices a pupil can decode in class but panics in any timed format. The practitioner role includes:`,[`Ignoring national assessments as irrelevant.`,`Removing the child from all literacy.`,`Asking the child to write their own SATs paper.`,`Monitoring that pattern, supporting with familiarisation and possible access arrangements, and still teaching reading, not only test technique.`],3,`Plan and prepare for statutory assessment without abandoning the curriculum or the child's needs.`,`AO2`),l(`7-4-q3`,`7-4`,`Why is monitoring 'general progress' as well as task understanding part of the role?`,[`General progress is a medical diagnosis.`,`KCSIE forbids noticing attendance.`,`Attendance, participation and wellbeing change whether academic progress is possible; a practitioner who only marks books can miss the reason a child has stalled.`,`Only exam boards may monitor children.`],2,`The spec separates understanding and general progress. Both sit with the practitioner.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-4-o1`,topicId:`7-4`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain what a practitioner does at each stage of assessment.`,modelAnswer:`Practitioners observe, record and review children's progress against the relevant requirements (for example EYFS early learning goals or National Curriculum objectives) and monitor both conceptual understanding and general progress such as participation. They then provide support and targeted feedback so the child can improve, and they plan and prepare learners for statutory or national assessments — including access needs — without reducing the whole curriculum to test practice.`,markScheme:[`Observe/record/review against requirements and/or monitor understanding and general progress (up to 2).`,`Support/targeted feedback and/or planning and preparing for statutory/national assessment (up to 2).`],ao:`AO1`}]},{id:`7-5`,code:`7.5`,elementId:`e7`,title:`Reflection models and how to use them`,page:63,notes:{gist:`Reflection turns experience into better practice. The specification requires Kolb's Experiential Learning Cycle, Gibbs' Reflective Cycle, and Boud, Keogh and Walker's model. Use the named stages, then show a practitioner moving round the cycle after a real incident — not a vague 'I would think about it'.`,mustKnow:[`Kolb Experiential Learning Cycle: Concrete experience → Reflective observation → Abstract conceptualisation → Active experimentation.`,`Gibbs Reflective Cycle: Description → Feelings → Evaluation → Analysis → Conclusion → Action plan.`,`Boud, Keogh and Walker: Experience → Reflective process → Outcomes.`,`Kolb emphasises learning by doing and then trying a new idea in practice; it is a cycle, not a one-off.`,`Gibbs is useful after incidents because it makes feelings legitimate and ends in an action plan.`,`Boud, Keogh and Walker stress returning to the experience, attending to feelings, and re-evaluating — outcomes include new skills, ideas and a shift in self-belief.`,`Application: after a failed transition, a behaviour incident, a lesson that flopped, or feedback from a mentor — pick a model and walk it through.`,`Reflection-in-action (in the moment) and reflection-on-action (afterwards, Schön) complement these cycles.`],sections:[{heading:`Kolb and Gibbs in a setting`,body:[`Kolb: you run a new circle-time song (concrete experience); you notice half the children turned away (reflective observation); you link this to age, length and sitting time (abstract conceptualisation); you try a shorter, movement-based welcome next day (active experimentation). Then the cycle starts again.`,`Gibbs after a playground incident: describe the facts without judgement; name your feelings (anger, panic, guilt) so they do not drive the next decision; evaluate what went well and badly; analyse why (triggers, staffing, your proximity); conclude what you now think; write an action plan (script, extra adult, change of route to the door).`,`Gibbs is longer; in a six-mark answer you do not need a paragraph per stage, but you must not skip feelings or the action plan — those are the model's point.`]},{heading:`Boud, Keogh and Walker, and choosing a model`,body:[`Experience: the event as lived. Reflective process: returning to it, attending to feelings, re-evaluating what it means (perhaps with a mentor). Outcomes: new understanding, new skills, or a change in attitude — for example 'I used to think the child was defiant; I now see a trigger at collection'.`,`This model is strong for emotionally loaded work (safeguarding, a parent complaint) because feelings are not an optional extra.`,`Choose Kolb when you want a teaching experiment; Gibbs when you need a structured incident debrief; Boud when the learning is personal and attitudinal. Naming the model and mis-listing the stages loses AO1.`]}],theorists:[{name:`David Kolb`,idea:`Experiential Learning Cycle: concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualisation, active experimentation.`},{name:`Graham Gibbs`,idea:`Reflective Cycle: description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action plan.`},{name:`Boud, Keogh and Walker`,idea:`Reflection as experience, a reflective process (including feelings), and outcomes that change practice and the person.`},{name:`Donald Schön`,idea:`Reflection-in-action and reflection-on-action — useful extra vocabulary alongside the three required models.`}],examMoves:[`List the stages in the right order. Mixing Gibbs with Kolb is a classic fail.`,`Apply one model to the scenario all the way to a changed action.`,`Feelings appear in Gibbs and in Boud — if you skip emotion you have not used those models.`]},quiz:[l(`7-5-q1`,`7-5`,`Which sequence correctly represents Kolb's Experiential Learning Cycle?`,[`Description; Feelings; Evaluation; Analysis; Conclusion; Action plan.`,`Experience; Reflective process; Outcomes.`,`Concrete experience; Reflective observation; Abstract conceptualisation; Active experimentation.`,`Plan; Do; Mark; Report to Ofsted.`],2,`Kolb is experience–observe–conceptualise–experiment. Gibbs is the six-stage incident cycle. Boud is the three-part model.`),l(`7-5-q2`,`7-5`,`After a child is hurt at home-time, a practitioner uses Gibbs. Which stage are they in when they write 'next time I will position myself by the gate and use the agreed script'?`,[`Feelings.`,`Action plan.`,`Description.`,`Concrete experience.`],1,`Gibbs ends with an action plan. Concrete experience is Kolb. Description and feelings come earlier in Gibbs.`,`AO2`),l(`7-5-q3`,`7-5`,`Why might Boud, Keogh and Walker's model be particularly useful after a distressing safeguarding event?`,[`It ignores feelings.`,`It has no outcomes.`,`It replaces the DSL.`,`It treats attending to feelings as part of the reflective process and looks for outcomes in skills, ideas and attitudes, not only a new activity.`],3,`Boud et al. put emotion and personal change at the centre. That fits emotionally loaded professional work.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-5-o1`,topicId:`7-5`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain how one model of reflection can be applied to improve practice in an educational setting.`,scenario:`A trainee's first whole-class phonics session unravels: several children call out, one cries, and the trainee talks faster and louder until the session is abandoned.`,modelAnswer:`Using Gibbs' Reflective Cycle, the trainee would first describe the facts (length, sitting still, calling out, the child crying, talking louder) without excuses. They would name feelings — panic, embarrassment, perhaps irritation — so those feelings do not silently drive the next session. Evaluation would weigh what worked (a few children blended the sounds) against what did not. Analysis might link the calling-out to age, unclear expectations, and the trainee's escalating tone (Element 5 communication). The conclusion would be that a shorter, more interactive session with a modelled turn-taking game would match stage better. The action plan would be concrete: a 10-minute input, a stop/go game for impulse control, a co-teacher to support the distressed child, and a calm voice — then the cycle is used again after the next attempt. Kolb would frame the same events as concrete experience, reflective observation, a new conceptualisation of phonics as active, and active experimentation next day.`,markScheme:[`Correctly names a required model and its stages (Kolb / Gibbs / Boud, Keogh and Walker) (up to 2).`,`Applies stages to the scenario (up to 3).`,`Ends in changed practice / outcomes / experimentation (1).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`7-6`,code:`7.6`,elementId:`e7`,title:`Why CPD and developmental feedback matter`,page:64,notes:{gist:`CPD keeps knowledge and skills current so provision and outcomes improve. It is also how the workforce meets regulatory requirements, legislative changes and current debates. Developmental feedback improves performance, motivation and growth; constructive criticism supports reflective practice so you can see the next developmental need.`,mustKnow:[`CPD keeps knowledge and skills up to date (safeguarding, SEND, pedagogy, first aid).`,`The aim is better provision and better outcomes for learners — not the certificate.`,`Meets regulatory requirements (for example EYFS paediatric first aid, KCSIE annual safeguarding training).`,`Tracks legislative changes (a new KCSIE, a revised EYFS, data-protection updates).`,`Engages with current priorities, debates and approaches (for example early reading, inclusion, AI and technology, mental health).`,`Enables meaningful team contribution and progression or promotion.`,`Feedback improves performance and motivation; it supports personal and professional growth.`,`Constructive criticism is specific, evidence-based and forward-looking; it is not humiliation.`,`Feedback supports reflective practice so you can identify your own developmental needs (link to 7.5 and 7.7).`],sections:[{heading:`Why CPD is not optional`,body:[`A practitioner trained ten years ago who has not updated on KCSIE, Prevent or the current EYFS is a risk. Regulatory training (safeguarding, first aid, food hygiene, Team Teach if used) is a floor, not the whole of CPD.`,`Children's outcomes improve when staff learn a better way to teach early maths, to use AAC, or to run a restorative conversation — then actually apply it. CPD that never leaves the folder is not development.`,`Teams need a mix of expertise. Your CPD on EAL or attachment is a contribution to the whole staff, not only a private hobby. Progression (HLTA, SENCO, room leader) is built from evidenced development, not from time served.`]},{heading:`Developmental feedback`,body:[`Feedback that works is specific ('you turned your back on the rest of the group when you comforted Sam') and names a next step. Vague 'you were great' feels nice and changes nothing. Personal attack ('you're not cut out for this') destroys motivation and is not professional.`,`Motivation: people grow when they feel the work is noticed and the next challenge is achievable (link to Bandura's self-efficacy). Appraisal, mentoring, peer observation and student-teacher feedback all count.`,`Constructive criticism is a gift to reflective practice: it gives you data you cannot see yourself. Combined with a model such as Gibbs, it becomes an action plan rather than a rumination.`]}],theorists:[{name:`Donald Schön`,idea:`The reflective practitioner: professional growth depends on examining practice, not only on accumulating years.`},{name:`Albert Bandura`,idea:`Self-efficacy: developmental feedback that is specific and achievable raises belief that performance can improve.`}],legislation:[{name:`EYFS statutory framework`,point:`Staff must have appropriate qualifications, training, skills and knowledge, including paediatric first aid where required.`},{name:`Keeping Children Safe in Education`,point:`Annual safeguarding training and updates so staff understand current guidance.`}],examMoves:[`Split the answer: why CPD (knowledge, outcomes, regulation, law, debates, team, progression) then why feedback (performance, motivation, growth, constructive criticism, reflection).`,`Name a real training area (safeguarding, SEND, first aid) so it does not sound generic.`,`Constructive criticism ≠ being unkind; show you know the difference.`]},quiz:[l(`7-6-q1`,`7-6`,`Which is a reason the specification gives for CPD?`,[`It keeps knowledge and skills up to date and helps meet regulatory requirements and legislative changes.`,`It replaces the need to follow any legislation.`,`It is only for teachers who want to leave the sector.`,`It is complete after the T Level diploma is awarded.`],0,`Up-to-date knowledge, regulation and legislative change are named. CPD is career-long.`),l(`7-6-q2`,`7-6`,`A mentor says 'when you shouted over the crying child, several others froze; next time crouch, lower your voice and let a colleague take the group'. This is developmental feedback because it:`,[`Is personal abuse.`,`Must be ignored if it feels uncomfortable.`,`Is specific, evidence-based and gives a next step that can improve performance and feed reflective practice.`,`Replaces all CPD courses.`],2,`Constructive criticism names the issue and the improvement. It supports growth; it does not replace statutory training.`,`AO2`),l(`7-6-q3`,`7-6`,`Why does CPD that never changes classroom practice fail the specification's purpose?`,[`Certificates are illegal.`,`Ofsted counts only hours in a hotel.`,`Feedback and CPD are unrelated.`,`The point of CPD is improved provision and outcomes, plus a more useful contribution to the team — attendance without application does not inform practice.`],3,`AO3: CPD and feedback exist to inform practice, not to decorate a CV.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-6-o1`,topicId:`7-6`,marks:6,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain why continuing professional development and developmental feedback are important in informing practice.`,modelAnswer:`CPD keeps practitioners' knowledge and skills current so provision and children's outcomes improve, and it is how staff meet regulatory requirements and legislative changes such as updated KCSIE or EYFS first-aid rules. It also engages staff with current debates and approaches and enables them to contribute to the team and to progress. Developmental feedback improves performance and motivation when it is constructive: specific, evidence-based criticism plus a next step supports personal and professional growth. Together, CPD and feedback feed reflective practice so the practitioner can identify developmental needs and actually change what they do with children, rather than collecting certificates.`,markScheme:[`CPD: up-to-date knowledge/skills, provision/outcomes, regulation/legislation, debates, team/progression (up to 3).`,`Feedback: performance, motivation, growth, constructive criticism, reflective practice/needs (up to 3).`],ao:`AO2`}]},{id:`7-7`,code:`7.7`,elementId:`e7`,title:`How practitioners can meet their own development needs`,page:64,notes:{gist:`Identifying a developmental need is useless without a plan to meet it. Practitioners use self-directed learning, shadowing and visits, external training, appraisal and peer observation, and the setting and reviewing of professional goals. Mix methods: a course without practice, or reading without feedback, will not shift what you do on Monday.`,mustKnow:[`Self-directed learning, reading and research: journals, books, podcasts, the latest KCSIE, a SEND Code of Practice chapter, a research summary.`,`Shadowing and visits to other settings: seeing a different key-person system, a strong phonics lesson, a specialist provision.`,`External training, workshops and conferences: first aid, safeguarding, subject knowledge, local authority SEND briefings.`,`Appraisal, peer observation and feedback: a structured look at your practice with a target attached.`,`Setting and reviewing professional goals: specific, realistic, dated, and revisited — not a wish list in September that is forgotten by October.`,`Needs come from reflection, feedback, inspection, new roles, and gaps you notice in outcomes for children.`,`Record CPD: what you did, what you learned, what you changed. That evidence supports appraisal, registration and interviews.`],sections:[{heading:`Learning from self, peers and other settings`,body:[`Self-directed learning suits a specific gap ('I do not understand demand avoidance') and fits around shifts. It needs a test in practice: try one strategy, then reflect (Kolb's experimentation).`,`Shadowing a colleague or visiting another nursery or school is how you see standards beyond your own staff room. Go with a question ('how do they run lunchtimes?') and a note of what you will try.`,`Peer observation only works with a protocol: agreed focus, kind and specific feedback, and a follow-up. Used well it is one of the cheapest, highest-impact ways to meet a need. Used as a test, people perform a show lesson and learn nothing.`]},{heading:`Courses, appraisal and goals`,body:[`External training is essential for regulated skills (paediatric first aid, designated safeguarding). Workshops and conferences expose you to current debates you will not meet in your own corridor. The need is met only if you cascade and apply.`,`Appraisal should be a two-way professional conversation: your goals, the setting's priorities, and the children whose outcomes worry you. It is a meeting of 7.6 (feedback) and 7.7 (how you will meet the need).`,`Goals that work look like 'by March I will have shadowed the SENCO, completed the local autism module, and introduced a visual timetable in my group, reviewed with my mentor' — not 'get better at behaviour'. Review them; drop or stretch them; log the impact on children.`]}],theorists:[{name:`David Kolb`,idea:`Meeting a developmental need completes the cycle only when new ideas are actively experimented with in practice.`},{name:`Graham Gibbs`,idea:`An action plan at the end of reflection is a professional goal in miniature — then it must be reviewed.`}],examMoves:[`Name methods from the spec list rather than 'I would go on a course' alone.`,`Always close the loop: identify need → choose method → apply → review the goal.`,`Apply to a realistic need (safeguarding update, EAL, a new age group, leadership).`]},quiz:[l(`7-7-q1`,`7-7`,`Which method of meeting developmental needs is named in the specification?`,[`Deleting all feedback.`,`Waiting until retirement to train.`,`Using personal social media to discuss families.`,`Shadowing and visits to other settings.`],3,`Shadowing and visits are a specification method, alongside self-directed learning, external training, appraisal/peer observation, and professional goals.`),l(`7-7-q2`,`7-7`,`A practitioner realises they freeze during behaviour that challenges. Which plan best meets that developmental need?`,[`Agree an appraisal goal, shadow a trained colleague, attend the setting's de-escalation workshop, then review after peer observation.`,`Avoid all children who become dysregulated.`,`Read one article and never practise.`,`Post the incidents on a public forum to crowdsource advice.`],0,`The spec wants a mix: goals, shadowing, external/internal training, and feedback through observation — applied, not theoretical.`,`AO2`),l(`7-7-q3`,`7-7`,`Why must professional goals be reviewed, not only set?`,[`Ofsted forbids written goals.`,`Reviewing is how you know the need was actually met and whether practice with children changed; an unreviewed September list is not development.`,`Goals are legally unchangeable.`,`Self-directed reading cannot count as CPD.`],1,`The spec pairs setting and reviewing goals. The loop is the professional standard.`,`AO3`)],open:[{id:`7-7-o1`,topicId:`7-7`,marks:4,command:`Explain`,stem:`Explain two ways a practitioner can keep developing their own skills and knowledge.`,modelAnswer:`Practitioners can use self-directed reading and research, then test a new strategy in practice and reflect on it, which suits a specific knowledge gap such as a new piece of safeguarding guidance. They can also shadow a colleague or visit another setting, or use appraisal, peer observation and feedback, to see skilled practice and set a reviewed professional goal — for example completing a workshop and then being observed using the new approach.`,markScheme:[`1 mark for each valid method (self-directed learning/reading/research; shadowing/visits; external training/workshops/conferences; appraisal/peer observation/feedback; setting and reviewing goals) (max 2).`,`1 mark each for how it meets a need / is reviewed or applied (max 2).`],ao:`AO2`}]}],d=[...t,...n,...r,...i,...o,...c,...u],f=Object.fromEntries(d.map(e=>[e.id,e]));function p(t){if(f[t])return f[t];let n=e[t];if(n)return{id:t,code:n.code,elementId:n.elementId,title:n.title,page:n.page,notes:{gist:`${n.title}. Use Ask AI with this heading for a model 4-, 6- or 15-mark answer while you work through the rest of the element.`,mustKnow:[`This is specification point ${n.code} from the NCFE CACHE Education and Early Years T Level (QN 610/5748/4).`,`Use named legislation (with year) and named theorists rather than generic phrases.`,`Apply every point to a child, family or setting — that is AO2.`,`Finish 6- and 15-mark answers with impact on the child and what the practitioner should do.`],sections:[],examMoves:[`Identify = name it. Outline = two short points. Explain = because / therefore. Analyse = break apart and show the link. Evaluate = weigh and judge.`]},quiz:[],open:[]}}function m(e){return f[e]?.quiz??[]}function h(){return d.flatMap(e=>e.quiz)}function g(){return d.flatMap(e=>e.open)}export{f as a,m as i,g as n,d as o,p as r,h as t};